      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE,</p>
        <p>The Court and the War.</p>
        <p>Queen Victoria did not permit the Crimean War to interfere with her periodical visits to Windsch., Balmoral and Osborne, but the King and Queen, in the prese4t crisis, have given up their usual plans and relinquished all pleasure, sport, and change, with the exception of flying visits to Windsor and Norfolk. Sandringham, where the Royal Family has been quietly spend- ing Christmas, is not far removed from the East Coast, and doubtless the Kaiser would find great pleasure in destroying the beautiful country house in which he has been often hos- pitably entertained, despite the fact that Queen Alexandra, with a woman's intuition, judged his character more accurately than the late King Edward, wip whom, however, the Em- peror was never a favourite.</p>
        <p>The May Court Club of Ottawa. The May Court Club of Ottawa, which con- sists of one hundred and fifty girl members, is doing good work for qanadiart soldiers, more specially for Princess Pat's regiinent of light Infantry. The Countess of Aberdeen intro- duce's] the Old English festival of May Day, and the club resulted from a May Day party she gave at Government House. The members have taken an active part in all kinds of philan- thropic work in Ottawa, and as soon as war broke out they .organised working classes, and undertook the making of. all hospital supplies in the way of needlework. They also have a bureau for unemployed women. Many of the members have joined the Red Cross Society, and all are working very busily. The. Duchess of Connaught, and her daughter, the Princes Patricia, have always taken a great interest in the club and its many activities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>Russian Woman Aviator.</head>
        <p>A Russian Woman Aviator.</p>
        <p>The Russian Government has accepted khe services of a clever lady aviator, and she is now a member of the Imperial Flying Corps, on the same footing as Men. Britain and Ger- many refused to' recognise medical women at the front, and those who have organised hos- pitals in France and Belginm. have done so</p>
        <p>HOME NOTES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>Profitable Recipes and Useful Suggestions.</head>
        <p>Profitable Recipes and Useful Suggestions.</p>
        <p>Japanese Wisdom. The Japanese host never entrusts the making of tea to his servants on high occasions; it is a _ _</p>
        <p>task he performs himself,</p>
        <p>Cleaning Raincoat. To clean a raincoat, it should be' dipped in cold soft water, spread on a table, and scrubbed all over with a large nail-brush and soap. Then put it on a coat-hanger, and, if possible, dry in the open air.</p>
        <p>Broiled Halibut. Slice the fish, salt and pepper, and lay in melted butter-one half-hour, allowing one table- spoonful of the butter to one pound of fish. Then roll in flour and broil for twent.y minutes. Serve hot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <p>Cold Cabinet Pudding. Break three eggs into a basin, and mix well with one pint of milk. Melt oz. of gelatine in a little water, let it soak for half an hour, add it to the custard, and place it in a jug with six lumps of sugar. Place the jug in a pan of boiling water and stir until the custard thick- ens. Flavour with . vanilla. Crumble three Penny sponge cakes and soak in a wine-glassful of sherry. Soak a mould in cold water and decorate with angelica, pour in some custard and let it set, add sponge cake and custard until full, and .put aside to set. This quantity wlll make two small puddings.</p>
        <p>Date Pudding.. Required: Six stale sponge cakes, 4oz. of dates, linz. of castor sugar, the grated rind of one lemon, two eggs, .milk to soak cakes. Soak the cakes in enough milk to moisten them. Stone the dates and cut them into thin strips. Beat up the cakes with a fork, and the dates and lemon-rind and the eggs well beaten. Slightly butter a pie-dish, pour in the mixture. Bake in a moderate oven about half an hour. If you wish to make a more dainty-looking dish, whip the whites of two or three eggs to a very stiff froth, flavour with vanilla and Castor sugar. Heap this roughly on the cooked pud- ding, sprinkling it now and then with castor • sugar; put it back in the oven in a very cool part till it is a very pale brown. If liked, sprinkle over the top with pink sugar or " hundreds-and-thousands.':'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>Boots and Shoes.</head>
        <p>Boots and Shoes.</p>
        <p>The first thing to be done with a pair of new boot's or shoes, sags the " F#mily Journal," is to set each one on a dinner-plate large enough</p>
        <p>c 3,2 CHILDREN'S CORNER t Conducted by UNCLE NED. ii:1420-6e0005.4)41.9.,042,042.30, A HEARTY INVITATION to join this Corner is extended to all children who read it regu- larly. Application for membership, stating name, address and age, should be made to: "UNCLE NED, 'Examiner" Office, Bewsey- street, Warrington." Every girl or boy, whose name is in the Big Book, is entitled to enter the competitions held from time to time, to the winners of which useful prizes are awarded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>WELCOME TO NEW YEAR.</head>
        <p>WELCOME TO NEW YEAR.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR CHILDREN,-</p>
        <p>First of all this week let me say how glad I am that our Christmas Corner proved to your liking, and thank you for the many letters you have sent to tell me so. They please me very much. Well, now, the New Year is come at last, and I suppose we are all veiy glad to have it with us. Did you bid the old year good-bye? I daresay many of you will say,. "Oh! You can't say good-bye to the old year," and think I am asking a useless question; but if you only think over everything that has taken place during the life of the old year, and count up all the happy times it has given you—how you have been pro- vided for, clothed, fed and sheltered—you twill be sad that it has passed from you, and will feel that you have indeed lost a dear friend. For it has been a good, friend to us in Eng- land; we have all been kept safe and sound and comfortable, although in other countries not so far away brave ?nen, have had to fall in their thousands, and towns have been razed to the ground by the hands of the wicked.</p>
        <p>So- the kind old year is gone, and we are sorry. We hope and believe that the New Year, too, will be a dear friend to us—and then we shall go forward in our lives, and learn to grow better, and when the time comes for it to pass from our midst, we shall bid it, too, a sad farewell. . . . Have you ever •seen the old year die? It dies in the night, when the earth is sad and still, and the weeping moon is lost in the mist, and all the church bells ring, to tell that men are sorry. DI) you know why bells are chosen? It is becaule they give the most</p>
        <p>"Let us found a society of mutual admira- tion,", proposed a young gentleman to his fair companion. • "I will begin by admiring your beautiful eyes. Now, what do you admire about me?"</p>
        <p>"Well," was the reply, " I—l admire your good taste."</p>
        <p>Spoke Too Quickly. "Sir, I wish to marry your daughter Susan." "You do, eh. Are you in a position to sup- port a family?" "Oh, yes, sir." "Better' be sure of it. There are ten of us."</p>
        <p>Boston Woman's Chain of Teeth. A Boston society woman carries,in her purse, for good luck, one of her first teeth. She has the first teeth of all of her six children set in gold and strung on a , fine chain, Which .she wears about her neck. Almost all youngsters nowadays have to endure having their first teeth pulled so the second row will come in straight and perfect. Mrs. Sereno Payne has a mascot in the shape of a big rag doll which belonged to a pet child of her acquaintance. The child accidentally left the doll if the Payne home and it brought such good luck Mrs. Payne replaced it With a better specimen and pre- served the mascot. Thomas Nelson Page al- ways keeps a small ivory ,elephant on the desk where he writes.</p>
        <p>'beautiful sound. One of our greatest writers said that bells are the music nighest bordering upon heaven, and that of all sound of all bells, the most solemn and touching is the peal that rings out the old year. Another writer said they are "wild," and perhaps they are—with grief.</p>
        <p>- I want us always to remember that, whilst new friends are a splendid thing, the old friends are truly the best. Very often we do not learn to know their Ins, until they are gone past our recall; let his, then, try to realise it while yet they are with us.</p>
        <p>Yonr loving' UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>THOUGHT FOR THE WEEK. Never put up your umbrella till it rains.-- Alice Hegan Rice.</p>
        <p>BEFORE AND AFTER.</p>
        <p>"How happy little Tommy looked when he sat down to the Christmas feast! The table fairly groaned under the weight of good thin3s." "'That's very nice." "Yes; but after the feast it was little Tommy who groaned under the weight of the good things !" •</p>
        <p>ALWAYS ASK FOR , WELCOAVg, SAVE THE COUPONS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <p>.+ ++++++.)•+•++++++</p>
        <p>••••••••:•••)•:'•••:•••:••••• .0040•••+.4.••:•••••••••••••••••••:•• ••••••:••:••:••••144...:.•.:•• • WOMAN'S WORLD. 0.0 • 4.•:" •• + • 4,41 • • popular than they were last winter, and this is said to be due to the war. Many and in- genious are the ways in which they are ar- ranged, and the plaids are novel combinations of lines and colours. Shell jackets, as worn by Highland regiments, sometimes take the form of a bolero, a pretty lace vest giving a touch of lightness. Black and white checked coats and skirts are very smart, but still smarter are the suits that have a white coat and black skirt, or vice-versa.. Skirts of plaids are usually full, and provided the fulness is kept in its proper place, the effect is good. At present the fronts and backs are plain, and resemble panels, the fulness being at the side's, where it is confined by straps or stitchings. Belts often appear only in front, and pass under the coat or dress at the sides and back.</p>
        <p>quite independently of the Go4ernments of those countries. But the Russians accepted the sertices of women doctors on condition that they did not exceed fifty per cent. of the practi- tioners. The Russian Minister of War was the first to initiate and to facilitate the entry of girls into the medical profession. Russian women have done noble deeds during the war, and when it is over they will be admitted into all the professions on equal terms with men.</p>
        <p>Pretty Novelties for the Neck. Details freshen a well-worn tailor-made, or an afternoon dress that has already seen some service. For the latter, a throatlet of gauze and velvet with floral petals -spattered here and there, and fastening on one side with a choux of white marabout, is charming,. and could easily be, made at home of odds and .ends. that most people acquire the habit of collecting. Or a band of some dainty white fur, finished off with a bow of velvet ribbon, or a cluster of small blossoms may be utilised. For outdoor wear nothing is smarter limn the popular set, consisting of muff, tie and toque, made of velvet, silk, or brocade, and trimmed with marabout, now obtainable in white and 'grey, as well as black. The ties, however, should always be short, otherwise they lose their sm-artness! The, material set is more popular than the fur, and is made of such divers materials that no two are alike. They form very suitable Presents, and may be built at home from odds and ends of plush, velvet or silk.</p>
        <p>Singing and Picture Teas. • In order to help young artists, who were about to make their debut when the war-nroke out, many well-known women in London fire giving singing teas in their drawing-rooms, once or twice a week. A charge is 'made for entrance (the tea, of course, is given by the hostess), and the amount taken provides the fees for the singers and musicians. This gives the artists a chance of being heard and of getting engage- ments. Picture teas are organised on the same lines. Young artists arrange their sketches to the greatest advantage in the drawing-rooms, the daughters of the house generally undertak- ing the principal arrangements for such func- tions, which serve a very useful purpose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>War and Fashions.</head>
        <p>War and Fashions.</p>
        <p>Paris is slowly resuming something of her former gaiety, for which the New Year was always a special occasion, but cannot altogether shake off the depression of her darkened boule- vards at night, and general early closing. Nevertheless, she is determined to maintain her supremacy in the world of fashions, and to bring her influence to bear in deciding women's modes during 1915. Whilst with us Russian effects are all the vogue, Parisian women are appearing in khaki walking costumes, as a compliment to the British Army. Khaki is not a very suitable colour for our dark and mirky streets in mid-winter, but experts predict that it will become popular in the spring. For the time being, fashionable women are favouring a well-wrapped-up appe,arance, with fuller skirts, and long coats, with fur down the front, and round the hem and neck, whilst an, astrachan toque, with a cockade, completes the Cossack- like effect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Winter Coats.</head>
        <p>Winter Coats.</p>
        <p>Long coats have short capes of embossed vel- vet, very quaint in shape, that end at the waist, and fittish with a big collar of fur. The latter often terminates at the sides of the throat, after the Manner bf a military collar; leaving the neck bare and exposed. Linings frequently match the fur in colour. Many women prefer coats showing the front of the dress, because tiley are so much easier to walk in.. All the new coats button close to the neck and finish with a fur collar. Sleeves are long and wide, at the shoulder, the majority are loose at the wrist, and fit easily and comfortably everywhere. Fur capes are wider than formerly and made in the Spanish style, the fulness being restrained by a strap that keeps, the garment in place. That the floppy waist has had its day, and that trimmer styles -will replace it, is abundantly evident to those who can read the , signs and portents of La Mode, but coats are still loose, especially at the waist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>Scottish Influence. Plaids, checks and diced materials are more</head>
        <p>The Scottish Influence. Plaids, checks and diced materials are more</p>
        <p>to take the sole, and pour into this boiled lin- seed oil sufficient to fill the plate to the upper edges of the sole. Let each shoe remain thus for about eight hours, when it will be thor- oughly saturated. Linseed oil should not be</p>
        <p>app- lied to the upper leather, as it will soon become dry, rendering the leather hard and tough. In the soles, however, it will exclude the dampness and enlarge the pegs, so :.:.hat the soles will never get loose horn the uppers, while, if they are sewn, the oil will prevent the thread from rotting. Give the upper leather a thorough greasing with equal., parts of lard and tallow, or tallow and neat's-foot oil. 'rreated in this manner, boots and shoes will be found to weal; more than doubly as long, and will always feel easy on the feet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>HERE AND THERE.</head>
        <p>HERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>A Warning. "Were are you going?" asked the butcher of his wife. "I'm going to the grocer's for some sugar." "Well, say—don't buy. it at the store across the street from my plate. The man over there borrowed my scales this morning."</p>
        <p>His Good Taste.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>TONGUE TWISTERS.</head>
        <p>TONGUE TWISTERS.</p>
        <p>There are many sentences that are so hard to say several times in quick succession that they have been named "Tongue-twisters." "Truly rural" seems quite a simple expression, and yet there. are very few people who can say it quickly six times running without twisting it into something like " Toore loorttl." Here are some in the form of verses:</p>
        <p>Oliver Oglethorpe ogled an owl and oyster. Did Oliver Oglethorpe ogle an owl and oyster?</p>
        <p>If Oliver Oglethorpe ogled an owl and oyster, Where are the owl and oyster Oliver Ogle- thorpe ogled? .Quixote Quicksight quizzled a queerish quid- box. . Did Quixote Quicksight quizzle a queerish quidbox? If Quixote Quicksight quizxled a queerish quidbox, Where's the queerish quidbox Quixote Quick- sight quizzled? - Peter 'Piper picked a peck.of pepper.' Did Peter Piper -pick a peck of pepper? If Peter Piper picked a peck of pepper, Where's the peck of pepper Peter Piper picked? Captain Crackspull cracked a ,catchpoll's cockscomb. Did' Captain Crackspull crack a catchpoll's cockscomb? If Captain Crackspull cracked a eatchpoll's cockscomb, Where's the catapoll'e cockscomb Captain Crackspull cracked? We must have some more of those next week, eh?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Useful Information in Popular Language.-</p>
        <p>Shortness of Breath.</p>
        <p>When shortness of breath comes from trifling exertion, such as going up a single flight of .stairs, the trouble may not be "serious, but it should be attended to, or it may grow worse: In such a case the cause may be weakness from a run-down condition, as from anwmia. It may be the result of sickness, or the result of severe indigestion. It frequently occurs in those who are obese, where the fht has filled up the body cavity so that the lungs do not have room to expand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>How 'to Cripple Digestion. Hot drinks weaken the digestive organs. The less drunk at meal-time the better, especially at such extremely high temperatures. Frozen foods and drinks block digestion until the digestive tract has again resumed its normal temperature, and experiments have shown that this sometimes requires an hour or two. Dur- ing this time the gerins are getting in good work. Io not make a cold storage plant of your stomach. Peppers and the various condi- ments and fiery sauces are as unnatural as they are unPhysiological. Their irritating effect sets up nervous spasms of the walls of the stomach and bowels, thus causing stagnation of the.con- tents, favouring gastric catarrh, and thereby increasing the absorption Of poisons.</p>
        <p>Effects of Strong Coffee. Dr. Gilman Thompson; Professor of Medicine in Cornell University Medical College, of New York City, asserts that the use of coffee to pro- duce wakefulness at night "soon results in forming a coffee or tea habit in which the indi- vidual becomes a slave to the beverage," and when deprived of it "suffers, from languor, prostration, and restlessness and craving." Two or three cups three times* a day produce "mus- cular tremors, nervousness, anxiety, apprehen- sion, palpitation of the heart, vertigo, heart burn, dyspepsia, constipation, insomnia and emaciation." Doctor Thompson well suggests that coffee users may discover the ill-effects which have been produced by suddenly stopping the drug. The degree of craving experienced is an evidence of the damage which has been done and the influence which the drug has ob- tained over the system. He says that coffee is a poison, and should never be given to chih, diren, in whom "coffee gives rise to insomnia, night terrors, nervousness and tremor." Dr. Thompson also asserts that acute coffee poison- ing gives rise to "excitability, with a tendency to delirium an tachycardia" (rapid beating of the heart)—" Good Health."</p>
        <p>BETTER THAN GOLD. (BY EDMUND PILLIFANT.) Better than gold is a loving friend .In the time of greatest need; The person on whom we may depend ' Is better than gold indeed. Better than gold is a happy home, Though it be a humble cot; No place can be found where man may roam To rival that hallowed spot. Better than gold is a healthy mind, And, the limbs which move with ease, And the constitution where we find Not a weakness or disease. Better than gold is a spotless name, Arld a reputation fair, And a life that never merits blame From a person anywhere. Better than gold is a conscience clear, And a mind that is at rest, And a heart that has no cause to fear As it beats within the breast.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>CONSUMPTION.</head>
        <p>CONSUMPTION.</p>
        <p>More Marvellous Recoveries.</p>
        <p>A further list containing names and ad- dresses of a few more ex-victims of the great White Plague, Consumption, and many of them were looked upon as being far advanced and hopeless cases : Mr. A. H. ilollands, 12, Belgrave-st„ Eccles Aylesford, Kent. Mr. Alfred Poster, 177, . Lewisham-rd, Lewisham.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thos. Cousin, Mickley Moor, Prudhoe, Northumberland. ' Mrs. H. Bowen, 38, Malden-rd., Acton, W. Mr. Wm. 'Mills. 25, Crooked Billet, Wimble- don.</p>
        <p>Miss Dolton, 1, Rowlands Cottages, Coldhar- bour-lane,._yVaddon, Croydon. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mrs. Willy, James-st., Blaby, Leicester. Daughter of Mrs. 11. Waite, Broad-st., En- derby, Leicester. Mr. S. Moore, Bell-lane, Fosdyke, Boston, Lines. Mr. McDonnell, Forphy, Lisnaskea, Co. Fer- managh, Ireland. . Son of Mrs. Millington, 3, West-avenue, Wood- lands, near Doncaster. Mr. W. C. Conquest, 170, Westnioreland-rd., Walworth, S.E. Miss M. F. Bowden, 23, Gloucester-rd., Trow- bridge, Wilts. Mrs.Wigley, 49, Broughton-BC, Battersea, S,W. If you doubt it, write yourself to one, any, or all of them for direct evidence. They, among hundreds of others, have been ,giVen a new lease of life by taking STEVENS CoNsummos CURE. If you are suffering from this scourge of the human race, send to - day for a free sample supply of this self - same life - giving substance. The personal advice of Mr. Stevens himself is also at your command.' A postcard or letter to 206, Worple-road, Wimbledon, will bring you a free samnle and full particulars without any charge whatever.—Advt.</p>
        <p>_ Upon the commencement of his Congrega- tional ministry in Warrington, the Rev. C. P. Pitt was accorded a splendid welcome on August 31st at his church in Elmwood Avenue. November 26th was a red-letter' day in the history of the Bold-street Wesleyan Church, for it was the occasion of a visit, in connection with the 64th anniversary celebrations, of the President of Conference (the Rev. Dinsrtale 'P. Young), who lectured on "Christianity and the Present Warn" _</p>
        <p>The diamond jui)ilee f St. John's Presby- terian Church was celebrated in November.</p>
      </div>
