      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>Some of the Changes in the Old Year.</head>
        <p>Some of the Changes in the Old Year.</p>
        <p>INTERCESSION SERVICES TO-MORROW</p>
        <p>To-morrow, the. first, Sunday of the New Year, will be observed as a day of intercession in most of the churches. The King and Queen will participate in this national religious act, and His Majesty has expressed his confidence that employers of labour will remove all pos- sible obstacles that stand in the way of their men going to church on this particular occa- sion.</p>
        <p>There - has been a very large demand for copies of the form of service authorised by the Archbishop of Canterbury and the Archbishop of York for use. The Society for Promoting Christian Knowledge have received orders for about 2,500,000 copies. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>OUR RIGHTEOUS CAUSE.</head>
        <p>OUR RIGHTEOUS CAUSE.</p>
        <p>The Rector of Warrington (Canon Willis), speaking during a special carol service at the Parish Church last Sunday evening, said fife war in which we are engaged is a righteous war, and in his name and the name of the congrega- tion he asked that the blessing of God might fall .upon our arms. He also gave formal notice of to-morrow's universal intercession services.</p>
        <p>- The Church in France has expressed its desire to devote the day to special devotions, and as far as is possible the Church of Belgium will do the same. The sympathy of the Church of the Russian Empire is also assured. Our own dominions will observe the day with us, and many people in neutral 'countries, notably in the United States, will join in the intercessions.</p>
        <p>EVENTS OF THE PAST TWELVEMONTH. A noteworthy event in local Anglican circles last year was the completion by the Rev. B. Percival, on January 4th, of the twenty-first year of his valuable work in the vicariate of the: parish of St. Peter's; Warrington. "I can honestly assure you\that I have tried to realise my responsibility," he assured a congregation gathered to commemorate the attainment of his majority" in the service of the church.</p>
        <p>At the ripe age of 71 there passed away, on the eleventh day of the year, the Rev. T. P. Williamson, an earnest and devoted minister of the Gospel, who, in a letter of instruction to his executors regarding his burial, wrote: "Perhaps a hearse may be found a convenience, but -a handcart, covered by a pall, is better, to my way of thinking." The Rev. Dr. Stanley A. Mellor was, in January, after an all-too-brieeptay in Warring- ton as minister, of the Cairo-gEreet .Chapel, ap- pointed to the pastorate of the Hope-street Church, Liverpool, Oiere he succeeded the Rev. H. D. Roberts, one of the best known clerics in the great port.</p>
        <p>- It was made known .during February that in 1913, parishioners of St. Paul's contributed no less a sum than £17.1 18s. 10d. in free-will offer- ings.</p>
        <p>The twenty-first birthday of the Wycliffe Society was celebrated on Sunday, March Ist, and the following day. The Rev. Herman F. S. Collier, fornlerly the Organising secretary of the Waifs' and Strays' Society, accepted the benefice of Padgate in April last.</p>
        <p>The Wesleyan Methodist Synod (Liverpool District) was held in Warrington in the wee ending May 16th, and was presided over by the Rev. Isaiah Parker, of Waterloo, Liverpool. . 'The foundation stone of the new St. Bene- dict's Roman Catholic Church in Orford-lane was laid by the Right Rev. the Lord Abbot of Ampleforth on July 12th. It is expected that the building, which is to cost about £6,000, and which will accommodate 600 people, will be completed early in the cowing summer. In July it was announced that the Rev: G. S. Tudor Evans, vicar of Lostock Gralam, near Northwich,. had accepted the 4ctorship of Oughtrington, vacant by the cession of the Rev. E. T. Wethered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>FREE CHURCH COUNCIL WORK.</head>
        <p>FREE CHURCH COUNCIL WORK.</p>
        <p>The past year in connection with the Free Church Council has been one of quiet religious work. At the commencement of the year new officers were elected. Mr. C. E. Parker desired to be relieved of the office that he had held so long and filled so efficiently. For several years he was the President of the Council, and the Council was reluctant to release him, but he deemed a change would be good, and the Rev. T. T. James was elected to the vacant office. Mr. E. W. Jones, through stress of business, desired to vacate the secretaryship of the Conn- qil, ,and the Rev. H. Ogden was elected to fill his place. The Council was loud in its praises of the officers whO had served in the past, and was gratified in securing their co-operation in the future. At the beginning of the year the united week of prayer was held, and on the second Sunday evening in January (at the close of the united prayer meetings) the united Com- munion sftrvice was held in St. John's Presby- terian Church. The church was full, and there was a quiet joy in the sense of Free Church unity and co-operation in Warrington. Successful Outdoor Campaign. During the summer months an out-of-door campaign was very successfully carried through. At the Sunday evening meetings greater crowds gathered than even the leaders anticipated, and great praise was due to Mr. J. C. Holland for all his arrangements. The Council felt that there was the need to "go out into the highways and hedges."</p>
        <p>The Council has also shared in peace meetings with the Peace Society, and joined in temper- ance meetings with the Temperance Unions. At the last executive meeting strong resolutions were passed deploring the fact that the Licens- ing Authority of the town had been so supine in its relation to the hours for the sale of drink during war time. Copies were sent to the local Press: Another resolution was forwarded to the Prime Minister and Home Secretary, urging the strengthening of the recent Act.*</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>Free Churches and the War.</head>
        <p>The Free Churches and the War.</p>
        <p>When the war cloud was about to burst, mem- bers of the executive met to consider a resolu- tion urging The Government to remain neutral, but before action could be-taken war had been declared between this country and Germany. All the members of the Council now see that neutrality was iinpossible. There was no option to a self-respecting country. Since the declara- tion of war, Free Churchmen have taken up arms, and it is safe to compute that at least 200,000 of them are now serving with the colours. That figure is Probably under, rather than over, the estimate, one Nonconformist body alone having 60,000 serving their country. It is safe to assume that a quarter of a million Free Churchmen have taken up arms for the defence of their country. Special Services for "Tommy Atkins."</p>
        <p>At the beginning of the war intercession ser- vices, arranged by the Council, were held weekly, but recently the services have been held monthly—on the second Tuesday of the month— in some central church in the town. Whilst the Territorials were in the district, Sunday morning services were arranged for the men arid officers.</p>
        <p>For the first time in the history of the Free Churches of Warrington an official chaplain to the local Workhouse has been appointed. The Guardians asked the Council to nominate one of its ministers, and the Secretary, the Rev. H. Ogden, was nominated, and the appointment was made by the Guardians last July. The value of the appointment is seen in the in- creased attndances at the Free Church service at the Workhouse and in the work in the wards and the infirmary. The National "Come to Church" Canipaign. The interest of the Council just now is centred upon the National "Come to Church" Canipaign. The desire of the National Free Church Council is that an invitation should. be given to every household in the kingdom to come to church the last two Sundays in January (24th and 31st), 1915. Warrington has under- taken to share in the campaign, and as far as possible an invitation will be brought to every house' in Warrington, inviting the members of</p>
        <p>(Continued in next column.)</p>
        <p>Supt. Williamson said the police ventures to suggest that it was absolutely necessary to ask for a 24 hours' remand. Prisoner was not yet quite sober, and they had been able to get noth- ing out of him.. Since co/Ong them he had used the filthiest of .language, and would not give a civil answer to any question. He had no leave- paper in his possession, and whether he was on lave, or a deserter, or had overetayed his leave, he would not say. He had used about the worst language that had been heard in the building. Prisoner said he had not been asked to say whether he was on leave, or a deserter, or any- thing; whereupon 'Supt. William4on stated that every possible effort to elicit . this information had been made. .</p>
        <p>First Time He Had Touched Drink.</p>
        <p>In reply to the Chairman, prisoner said that he was on leave for three days, having come home to Warrington on Monday. His leave- ticket was at his home at 31, Willis-street. This was the first time he had ever touched drink— he had been having a little jollification, as he had come home. He asked to be allowed to go to his home.</p>
        <p>The Chairman told prisoner. that he would be remanded for 24 hours; ,he would be in safe hands, and taken care of. It was for his own good., Jor they would like him to go back to his ship—men such as he were wanted just now. He remarked upon the fact that prisoner had a good conduct stripe on his arm. Supt. Williamson: His previous conduct would not be like it was last night, then! It is in prisoner's own interests that he :should not be allowed out.</p>
        <p>Muir was accordingly remanded in custody, the police being instructed to get him back to his ship in the meantime, if they could do so.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Keeping House &amp; Keeping Well</head>
        <p>Keeping House &amp; Keeping Well</p>
        <p>The woman who "keeps house" has enough to do when she is in good, sound health; but if she is weak, tired all the time, and suffering from morn to night with an aching back, house- wol.k becomes a heavy burden. Doan's Backache Kidney Pills have brought new life, health and strength to thousands of women afflicted in this way. Women are subject to kidney disease. The clothing they wear—the work they do, with its worry and strain—the lack of proper exercise— all tend to it.</p>
        <p>Backache, bearing-down pains, headache, dizziness, faintness, fits of "blues," are common symptoms of kidney disease. When any one of theso ills appears, together with irregularities in the action of the kidneys, it is evidence that the kidneys are weak and require help. Doan's Backache Kidney Pills have helped thousands of weak women through trying times when kidney disease means so much added misery. They do not disturb stomach or bowels and contain no poisonous, dangerous nor habit- forming drugs. They are a safe medicine for women and men, and their Success is due to the fact that the formula is good, and that Doan's Pills are a special medicine for kidney and bladder diseases only. .</p>
        <p>clear that in this case you. have been the aggressor. You have disturbed the peace of the community, and will be fined five shillings, including costs.</p>
        <p>JUVENILE COURT.</p>
        <p>Young - Stone-Throwers.,—Tiqes: boys were summoned for throwing stones in Priestley- street 'on 'December 9th.—They pleaded not guilty.—Martin Magian, a newsboy, said they threw stones at his back wheel as he was cyc- ling ,along Priestley-street, and the bicycle "skidded." Detective-Sergeant , Reddington stated that the boys admitted throwing the stones, but they said they had been throwing at a can in the street, not at the bicyele.—The Chief Constatte informed the Bench that pollee had no previous recorda against 'the boys, and added- that the damage ttlat was done about town: in the shape of the breaking of street- lamps through stone-throwing was ettoxmous. The three boys were licensed for street-trading. —Defendants were each fined 2s. 6d.</p>
        <p>Troublesome Paper-Sellers. —A newsboy pleaded guilty to selling. paper in ,Church- street on December 27th without wearing his badge.—The Chief Constable pointed out that; during the last few months, the police had cautioned over a hundred boys about trading without their badges. The defendant, who had been cautioned, but had not previously appeared before the Court, said that he had lost his badge, hut that the agent had told him it would be all right.—The Chairman (to defend- ant) : There is hardly any excuse. You will be fined 2s. 6d.</p>
        <p>A Warrington</p>
        <p>Opinion.</p>
        <p>Mrs. C. Porter, of 51, Marbury-street, Latch- ford, Warrington, says: "I caught a severe cold on my kidneys about six months ago, and as a result I began to suffer with fearful stabbing pains in the small of my back. The attacks wonld come on very suddenly, .and nearly brought me to the ground at times. It felt as if I was being stabbed with knives.. "When I got to bed the pain was so bad that I could not turn over. I got up in the mornings feeling as tired as, if I had -just done a day's work.. I was sleepy and listless all the time— all my energy seemed to leave me. "There were other diistressing signs of kidney weakness, so I began a conrse of Doan's Back- ache Kidney Pills, and soon I began to feel better. My back did not ache so much, and as I persevered with the Pills the pains left me altogether, and soon I was quite well again. I heartily praise Doan's Pills, for they have done me a power of 'good: (Signed) "C. PORTER." •</p>
