      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>TICKETLESS TRAVELLERS. Alleged Attempt to Pick Canadian</head>
        <p>TICKETLESS TRAVELLERS. Alleged Attempt to Pick Canadian</p>
        <p>Soldieis' Pockets.</p>
        <p>Two Londoners—Sidney Gentry; painter, and William John Grundy, fitter's labourer, both of 11, Short's-gardens, W.—were charged, at the Warrington Borough Police Court on Thursday, with having on the previous day travelled from Euston to Warrington, on the London and North-Western Railway, without tickets and with intent to avoid payment of their fares. The Chief Constable stated that, at about four o'clock that morning, Constable-Berry had been called to the Bank Quay Station to take charge of the two ran.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE COURT</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. A. J. Jolley (in the chair) and C. S. Burgess (sitting as a Children's Court). Birch for Soldier's Son.—An eleven-year-old boy was charged with .stealing a number of paper ornaments—the property of the Peacock Bazaar Co.—from a stall in the Warlington Market on the previous Thursday. He had been noticed to be walking about the stall, and act- ing in a suspicious manner,. and - Sergt. Sum- mers, keeping him under ollservation; saw him pick up a fancy paper article and walk away. The officer overtook hini, and found thirteen similar articles in his possession.—The Clerk (to the defendant) : What did yoli want to do with them? No answer.—The Chairman: Were you going to sell them? Yes, sir.—,The ,Chief Con- stable said•that the boy's father had enlisted, sin* when the mother had lost control of her son.—The boy was ordered G receive six strokes of the birch rod. TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Councillor Roberts (in the chair), and Messrs. J. Rymer Young, T. .Locker, and C. jlickson Holmes.</p>
        <p>One More Chance.—Annie Mary Keenan, of 115, Mersey-street, Warrington, appeared once again on the charge of obtaining goods by false pretences, for which charge she had been re- manded several time.—The Chief Constable said that Mr. Laws (the police court missioner) and Mr. Wainwright (i.S.P.C.C. Inspector) had continued their good offices in the case, and' it was desirable that the woman should be bound over for twelve months.—This course was adopoted, the defendant's husband again stand- ing surety in the sum of .25.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. J. Rymer Young (in the chair), and A. J. Jolley, and Dr. Naden.-</p>
        <p>"Head It !"—llenry- Welsby, labourer, of 86, Plumpton-street; Aaron Ashburner, labourer, of 52, Plumpton-street; Walter Appleton, of 20, Portland-street; and Joseph Martin, baker, of 4, Howard-street (who did not appear), were summoned for gaming in the vicinity of Plump- ton-street—They pleaded guilty.—Detective- Sergeant Reddington stated that, at about 3.15 on the afternoon of Sunday, December 6th, he saw the four Men tossing coins—playing a game called "Head It.", There were others also, but' they got away.—The Chief Constable said Welsby had been there before, for being drunk and disorderly; and that was his fifth appear- ance for gaming. The others had not been there previously.—Welsby and Ashburner were each fined 20s. (including 'costs), whilst Apple- ton and Martin- were fined 10s. each, also in- cluding costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>FRIDAY.</head>
        <p>FRIDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. S. Wright (in the chair). Aldermen Pierpoint and Holton, and Messrs. G. Pilling, A. J. Jolley and C. S. Burgess. Why the Drunkenness Statistics are High.— A homeless general labourer named Geo. Naylor, alias Burns, was charged with having been drunk and ,disorderly in Bridge-street on ThUrs- day, and the Chief Constable asked the Bench to deal with him in such a manner as would deter strangers of his type from coining to the town and getting into a filthy drunken condi- tion. The local drunkenness records were going up by leaps and bounds, and it was mainly duo to men of prisoner's class coming into the town and behaving in such a manner as to bring dis- credit upon it. Prisoner was fined 20s. and costs, or a month's imprisonment, lie said he had no money.</p>
        <p>The second inquiry was into the circumstances touching the deatheof Mary Dykes, aged fourteen weeks, daughter of Private Abraham Dykes, of the South Lancashire Regiment, and Mary Dykes, of 9, Dale-street, Warrington. Mrs. Dykes said .that at about a quarter to twelve on the morning of Tuesday, December 22nd, she put the baby in an armchair near the fireguard. There were two pillows on the chair, and she covered the baby with a shawl. A short time afterwards she went out into the yard, and when she came back found the room filled with smoke. The shawl was smouldering. and taking the baby from the chair, she found it was badly burned. It was taken to the In- firmafy, and died on Christmas Day. James Moreton, .ironworker, of 7, Dale-street, said he heard that another child was in one of the bedrooms at Mrs. Dykes', and brought it out. One of the pillows on the armchair was burning, and he threw it into the street. "Under the armchair there was a.-large piece of burnt .coal, which must have fallen on to the fender and thence on to the shawl. The fireguard was an " open " one. In this case also\ the verdict was one of "Acci- dental death."</p>
        <p>SCALDED ON CHRISTMAS. The third inquest was on the body of Cather- ine Ann Atkinson, aged 1 years, the daughter of. Harry and Eliza Atkinson, of 14, Ernest. street, Warrington, who had died on Monday from scalds.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Atkinson stated that on Boxing Day she took the child into the house of Elizabeth Kelly, 10, Ernest-street. She then gave it to a young woman' named Nora Corcoran, who wanted to nurse it. In trying to reach a cup off the boiler, Corcoran knocked over a pan of hot water on the oven, and the water fell over the baby. The child wass taken to the Infirmary and then home again, when a doctor was sent for. The following day it appeared much worse. and witness visited the Infirmary, where she was advised to consult a doctor. She then con- sulted Dr. Ghandi, who advised her to take the child to the Infirmary, and said it should have been kept there at first.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: Yes. Witness: I do not see why it was sent home. It took fits when it was brought home. The Coroner said he was not saying anything against the mother, but even with the best inten- tions, she could not have given it the attention that was to be received, at the,lnfirmary. They must not, however, blame the authorities there, for the building was very crowded at present— there were a number of wounded soldiers there —and they had to send out cases that were not so serious. Perhaps they had not thought it a very serious case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>0 I – iv. ,:5,,,: ~.,..i. . 9</head>
        <p>0 I - iv. ,:5,,,: ~.,..i. . 9</p>
