      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>COURTS.</head>
        <p>THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>!-;AID THE GRUEL WAS COLD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>prison for Refractory Pauper.</head>
        <p>prison for Refractory Pauper.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Christmas Eve, before Mr. J. Rymer Young (in the chair), Dr. Naden, and Mr. A. J. Jolley, George Turner was charged with having been disorderly in the Union Workhouse.—Mr. Lox- ham, Workhouse master, said that the casuals, of whom prisoner was one, had refused their breakfast, stating that the gruel was cold. Prisoner was excited, insolent, and used most abusive and disgusting language. It was im- possible for the gruel to be cold, as it had just come from the kitchen. Prisoner had tiven his wrong name to the porter on entering.—A porter said that he saw the gruel an hour after it had been given out, and, the basins were than 'quite warm.</p>
        <p>- Turner was sent to prison for 14 days, and on leaving the dock said: "Make it a month, if you like; it does not trouble me! I shall get out of the bad weather !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>"A 'LITTLE JOLLIFICATION." Disorderly Sailor and His Bad Language.</head>
        <p>"A 'LITTLE JOLLIFICATION." Disorderly Sailor and His Bad Language.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Wednesday, before Messrs. J. W. Hayward (ih the chair), W. T. Williams, J. Smethurst, and C. Jackson Holmes, • Lionel Muir, a stoker on H.M.S. Dido (sta- tioned at Harwich), was charged with being drunk and' disorderly in Hill-street; Warring- ton, the Previous night. Acting-Sergeant Armstrong said that shortly after eleven o'clock he was on duty in Hill- street and saw the prisoner—who was drunk— creating a disturbance., He requested him several times to go home; but Muir refused, and persisted in using bad language. Witness got the assistance of Acting-Sergeant Edwards, and prisoner was brought to the Police Station and locked up. He had to be carried nearly all the way, and his language. was. filthy.'</p>
        <p>Not Sober Even Then!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>NEWSBOYS DISAGREE. Enormous Damage By Young Stone- Throwers. if</head>
        <p>NEWSBOYS DISAGREE. Enormous Damage By Young Stone- Throwers. if</p>
        <p>"TRADING" WITHOUT BADGES.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Thursday, before Mr. J. Rymer Young and other magistrates, Percy Haigh, an "Echo" newsboy, was sum- moned for disorderly behaviour in Priestley- street on the 9th of December.</p>
        <p>Martin Maginn, a newsboy, said that at 6.30 p.m. on the day in question he was cycling down Priestley-street, and Haigh began to throw bricks at him. He came back and asked H.aigh if he knew anything about the bricks, and Haigh said "No," and took off his coat, wanting to fight him. The Chief Constable explained that the trouble arose out of the sale of newspapers, and asked what. the annoyance was, Maginn ,(who sold the "Express") replied that they tried to "heat" each other.,</p>
        <p>Detective-Sergeant Reddington said that Maginn told him what had happened; and he questioned Haigh, who sh-id, "Yes, I did take my coat off." Defendant explained that on December Bth a newsboy came into the office and told him that Maginn was "going to -upset" him. \ Maginn_denied saying this. _ _ _</p>
        <p>TI4 Chief Constable- said that that sort of thing went on day after day between the paper- boys, and for some time the police had had to detail a plain-clothes officer to• stop the rows. The defendant bad been fined twice previously for riding a bicycle on the parapet. The Chairman (to defendant) : It is very</p>
        <p>The Canadians' Complaint.</p>
        <p>There were a number of Canadian soldiers in the 'ame railway carriage, and these had made a complaint, to the guard-The did not say that it' was true or that it was not, but it had led to the detection—that the two had been at- tempting to pick their pockets. Tho guard had then asked Gentry and Grundy for their tickets and found that they had none. Upon arrival at Warrington the men had. been handed over to Mr. Blinstou, the ticket collector. He (the Chief Constable) asked for a week's adjourn- ment, in order that inquiries might be made into the allegations made by the Canadian soldiers. He woukl also communicate with the Metropolitan Police.</p>
        <p>The-prisoners, added Mr. 'Nicholls, said they had wanted to get out of London. Judging by the distance they came, they had wanted to get giut of England! 'Prisoners were remanded for a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>CHURCHES—continued:.</head>
        <p>THE CHURCHES—continued:.</p>
        <p>the household to attend church on those two special dates. In addition, an effort will be made to induce parents to connect their children with some Sunday school.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>For God, For King, And For Nation,</head>
        <p>For God, For King, And For Nation,</p>
        <p>Fr e* Churchmen at this particular period, in common with all serious-minded people, realise the need, in this tithe of trial and sorrow, of lifting up the nation and bringing the people nearer to God. The Free Churches rejoice in the nation's triumphs. They share in the thanksgicing when the Allies succeed; they also give of the best of their manhood. They suc- cour in the hour of need; .they comfort in the time of sorrow; they infuse courage into hearts that are drooping. The local Cowici.l has borne its share of toil in the past, and in the coming year it hopes to continue the common work of Free Churches—work which is for God, the King, and the nation.</p>
      </div>
