      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>FATAL CHRISTMAS PARTY</head>
        <p>FATAL CHRISTMAS PARTY</p>
        <p>Wiredrawer Dies as Result of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>Fall</head>
        <p>a Fall</p>
        <p>CHILDREN BURNED AND</p>
        <p>SCALDED.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon the Borough Coroner (MI) S. Brighouse) and a jury inquired into the circumstances of four accidents, which terrain:- ated fatally; death in two cases being due to burning, and in a third to scalding, the victims in each, of 'these three cases being young children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>PINAFORE CAUGHT FIFE.</head>
        <p>PINAFORE CAUGHT FIFE.</p>
        <p>The first inquest was on the body of Rose Brown, aged nearly three years, the daughter of George Henry and Martha Brown, of Long-lane House Farm, Orford.</p>
        <p>The mdtherid 'that ma the afternoon of Friday, December -11th, she took a pail to her husband in the shippon,. leaving the child in the _kitchen. As ' she entered the shippon she beard the child scream, and "running back found her ,on fire. Her husband extinguished the flames. The injuries, however, were serious, and flour was applied, Dr. Anderson being also -ium- moned. Her daughter was afterwards removed to the Infirmary, and for two weeks progressed very nicely, but she had a relapse and died on Christmas Day. After the accident, they found a small stick, burnt at one end, on the kitchen hearth:. Presumably the 'child had lighted this at the fire by pushing it through the fireguard, her pinafore thus catching fire.</p>
        <p>THE BURNING SHAWL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>SAD ENDING TO PARTY:</head>
        <p>SAD ENDING TO PARTY:</p>
        <p>The last inquest was upon the -body of Eli Swindells (55), wiredrawer, husband of Martha Swindells, of 23, Ripley-street, Warrington. The widow said that on Christmas Day her husband—a fairly healthy man—went to bed early in the morning and had a good rest. In, the afternoon he got up, and some friendg came in to spend the day with Urn. Her husband had some drink, and made himself rather jolly. At .one o'clock on Boxing Day morning, they were seeing their friends off at the door, and one of her husband's slippers came off, causing him to slip and fall violently to the floor. His head struck the tiles, and he rolled over. Dr. Segar was called in at 1.30, but her husband died on Monday night, never having regained full consciousness.</p>
        <p>Dr. Segar said Swindells was suffering from concussion, and died of luiemorrhage of the brain—the result of the fall.</p>
        <p>IT IS REMARKABLE What ease and helpfulness may be derived from a Pair of Glasses which have been specially made FOR YOU. It will take us, say, from a quarter to half an hour to test your sight, but the benefit to you will be life-long.</p>
        <p>Johnston Watson,</p>
        <p>. SIGHT TESTING OPTICIAN, 84, BRIDGE ST. , Warrington.</p>
        <p>Steel Spectacles from 2/6. Rolled Gold from 8/6.</p>
        <p>Hours of Business, 9 to 7-30. Saturday, 9 to 9-30. Please Note.—Mr. Wstson attends on Monday and Tuesday from 11 to 4-30; Wednesday, 11 to 5-30; Thursday, 11 to 1; Friday and Saturday, 11 to 5-30.</p>
      </div>
