      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>"RING OUT THE FALSE."</head>
        <p>"RING OUT THE FALSE."</p>
        <p>Never was a nation more intent upon the task of ringing out the False and ringing in the True than is Great Britain at this particular New Year. Suddenly the fOld Year revealed to us a canker in the heart of civilisation, which we had suspected,, but could scarcely credit, a canker which mania fested itself in a manner that, fortu- nately for humanity, .can only happen at rare intervals in the world's history. The world was wagging along very harmoniously 'and successfully in 1.914 when the Prussian militarist outburst occurred, and revealed, as we have said, the dangerous and unstable foundation upon which civilisation was resting. We and our Allies tackled the unnatural growth which appears to have come through from the darkest of the Dark Ages, and, though we have not eradicated it, we have effec- tually scotched its growth. As we leave 1914 behind us and enter upon 1915 we may well rejoice both in the retrospect and the prospect. Things might have been'much worse, and they could hardly hore been much better than they are. They will be much better in the future. Germany and Austria, the aggressors, were ready, and, having the advantage of numbers, equipment, and situation, they chose their own time to deliver a blow that was to make Europe their slaves. But they missed aim, and, unready as we were for the treacherous stroke, we took up the challenge and assisted France to stem an invasion that had about it all the elements of disaster to the attacked. In this, the twenty- second week of the war, we see the Ramshackle Empire, Austria-Hungary, in almost the last stages of being bat- tered to pieces, while Germany is rushing about from west to east like a wounded and caged wild beast, which she really is. In the coming year our great new Armies will take up the tale at the point our glorious and small old Army leaves it. Meanwhile our Navy holds the sea, our Empire is solid and sending us all the help it can, at home all parties are united. We have tackled the False and feel assured</p>
        <p>that PROVIDENCE will permit of our establishing the True during the com- ing months. The True will include the demolition of the militarist element on the continent, and the setting up of rights of nations that have long been swamped and strangled by the methods of those who have now dared to come out into the open to fight for the doc- trine that Might is Right. We are going to maintain the fact that Right is 'Right, even in the land that calls its head KAISER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>NEW YEAR HONOURS.</head>
        <p>NEW YEAR HONOURS.</p>
        <p>The New Year honours are a fair reflex of the present state of affair.. In normal times the EARL Of DERBY is an uncompromising opponent of the pre- sent Government and all their doings. To-day he is doing his utmost to see that they have the necessary force with which to carry the war to a successful issue, and they on their part have con- ferred upon 'him the greatest honour they could possibly confer by making him a Knight of the Order of the Garter. HIS LORDSHIP is chairman of the Territorial Force Association of Lancashire, and has spoken and worked with great effect in the recruit- ing campaign. He should have visited Warrington, but sickness and other circumstances intervened. The num- ber of Earls of Derby who have been decorated with the Garter is remark- able. LORD DERBY'S grandfather, uncle and father-14th, 15th and 16th Earls respectively—all received it, as did the Ist, 3rd, 4th, sth, 6th, 7th .and 13th Earls. LORD ST. ALDWYN, a former Conservative Chancellor, assisted Mr. LLOYD GEORGE, the present ft adi ca Chancellor, in the recent financial crisis 7—and he receives an Earldom. There are other similar recognitions c..f patriotic service, the claims of Labour and Liberalism being met in full, but not for party reasons. The Liberal Government have done well in the greatest crisis that ever betel a country; they show their real greatness by ack- nowledging all the sources from which they draw their strength.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>r;,41 –</head>
        <p>r;,41 -</p>
        <p>7 . 4 s,:k3 •_ • •</p>
        <p>Ailit</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>THE KAISER'S FLAMES OF HELL FIRE.</p>
        <p>NEW YEAR HOPE.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. K. Crosfield writes us is follows: NEW YEAR'S EVE, 1914.</p>
        <p>The last few grains of sand are running in the hour glass, and before many minutes more are sped the Old Year will have passed away for ever. On what a prospect of horror and desolation will the New Year open! Once the Kaiser succumbed, shall we say, to the pressure of Prussian militarism, growing more irritable, and weakening in his advocacy of Peace with advancing years, as M. Jules Cambon (French Ambassador at Berlin) sug- gests, and influenced perhaps even by the popu- larity of the Crown Prince amongst German Junkers—once the Kaiser finally threw in his lot with the War party of Germany—he lit flames of Hell Fire in Europe, which were cer- tain to make fearful ravages; .he inaugurated throughout the broad Plain of Central Europe an era of</p>
        <p>"Red ruin, and the breaking up of laws," on a vast and disastrous scale beyond anything for which we have a parallel in history.</p>
        <p>HOW LONG?</p>
        <p>And in England, America, France, Russia, everywhere, men are asking "How long is this mad and frightful worship of Moloch to go on?" "row long will the War last?" Who can answer with certainty? Unhappily no one can do so. Still, taking all the factors into account, not only the weariness and disgust and bitter repentance of Austria, and the ominous situation in Hungary, but also the waiting, ivatching attitude of Roumania, and Bulgaria, and Italy bearing in mind as well the frightful sacrifices! of human life this war entails, and its overwhelm- ing cost—it does indeed seem that we have very good grounds for hoping and believ- ing that estimates of a war lasting from two to three years were a long way over the mark. We may hope that another six or eight months will see it ended; it is possible, though, alas ! it does not seem at all probable, thit another six or eight weeks might do so. True, at the pres- ent time the mass of people in Germany are utterly ignorant of the real state of affairs, but sooner or late,ik the time will come when they will be faced With the fact that there is no prospect or possibility of final victory ahead of them.</p>
        <p>When the Allies' terms are made known, the enormous sacrifices of territory, and the stu- pendous indemnities that will be demanded, may for a time so stagger Germany that there may be a final convulsive effort to continue the war. But, even in that case, not for long, because a nation of educated People, when they see their country invaded, and the true facts of the situation are at lenkth forced upon them, will realise that, although the Allies' terms mean overwhelming disaste r for Austria and Germany, even that is better for them than the alternative of annihilation. WHEN PEACE DOES COME; When the lasting peace does come, with its far-reaching, thorough-going settlement, the great burden of excessive armaments that weighs on the shoulders of Europe, will be lightened. The frightful loss of life, and fright- ful increase in human suffering, can never be expiated by Germany or any other people. But while, in that respect, the present is a time of fearful horror and distress, the future, after this war, still holds for us the good hope mild confident belief that civilisation will make pro- gress on saner and wiser lines than heretofore.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>HELP THE WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>HELP THE WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>AMBULANCE BRIGADE HOSPITAL FOR FRANCE.</p>
        <p>Last week we called attention to the fact that the St. John Ambulance Brigade, whir have 7,000 members engaged on active service, are raising funds to establish a hospital to provide 520 beds in France, and that N 2. 4 District, of which Warrington is a part, is endeavouring to provide a ward at a cost of £l--,500. Azi, ad- vertisement in another column shows that over £l5 has been subscribed Mr. A. E. Spann, the local honorary secretary, will be pleased to receive contributions, large or small. Colonel Trimble, deputy commissioner of No. 4 District, has been given charge of the hospital, which, it is expected, will be ready in February.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>INFIRMARY SPECIAL WAR FUND.</head>
        <p>INFIRMARY SPECIAL WAR FUND.</p>
        <p>Satisfactory Response.</p>
        <p>Over £1,020 has now been subscribed towards the £1,500 which the Board of Management of the Infirmary are asking for to meet the extra expense that will be cast upon them by pro- viding accommodation for wounded soldiers. It is extremely interesting to find so many- firms, ladies and gentlemen interesting them- selves in equipping a bed. Contributors of £lO will be entitled to have one of the beds in the military ward named after the givers. We notice that the employes of the Penketh Tanning Company, Ltd., the Labour Bureau, and Messrs. Richmond, Ltd., have already taken advantage of this opportunity of provid- ing a permanent record of the war, and it is to be hoped their example will be generally fol- lowed.</p>
        <p>Subscriptions of the regulation amount to this fund carry life governorships like subscriptions to the general fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>WORKS WAR ALLOWANCES.</head>
        <p>WORKS WAR ALLOWANCES.</p>
        <p>It was found that so manremployes had en- listed that the total allowances became a very serious matter. At Messrs. ylands' Bros., for instance, the allowances amounted to £BO a week, which is equivalent to £4,000 a year. CASE OF THE MAFIBIED MEN.</p>
        <p>About 168 married men have enlisted from Messrs. Rylands Bros., Ltd., and their wives are being allowed 10s. per week. It has now been, decided that, whilst this grant will be, continued to these 168 wives, no grant will be made to the wife of a married man who in future enlists, the idea, we presume, being ..to</p>
        <p>retain as many of the married men as possible, while the single men may go to serve their country.</p>
        <p>The -existing arrangements at the other big works will be found set out below : The Pearson and Knowles Co.—A sum of 10s. per week was allowed at the first, but the allowance is now fixed at ss. per week. Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd.— Half wages, witht a minimum of 10s., plus is. extra for each child under 14 Yelis of age, are being allowed, whilst a thrift scheme has been started, whereby any surplus over and above the amount necessary to maintain a home is put in a savings bank to the credit of the man concerned, interest being allowed at the rate of 5 per cent. Messrs. Monks, Hall and CO.—ln ad4ition to making a, generous weekly allowance, this firm have sent a Christmas present to each of their men and to their wives and children. Messrs. Whitecross Co., Ltd.—The depend- ents of men are receiving ss. per week, and the wives receive an additional shilling for each child under 14 years of age. Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co.—Ten shil- lings per week is being allowed to the wives, and is. per week for each child under 14 years. Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., Ltd.—The Army pay is added to so as to make it. up to the amount of the husband's full wages.</p>
        <p>Warrington E.I. Co-operative Society.—An addition is made to the Government allowance in order to make up the amount received by the wife to an aver-age, wage of 30s. per week. Formerly half-wages were paid. St. Helens Cable Co.—The Government allow- ance is made up to the amount of the man's former average wage. British Aluminium Co., Ltd.—Ten shillings per week. Messrs. N. Greening and Son, and Messrs. Peter Walker and Son, Ltd., have made no change. The Mersey White Lead Co. continue to pay the wives of their men 10s. a week, with Is. extra for each child. The Richmond Gas Stove and Meter CO. pay 7s. 6d. per week; and Mr. T. Locker, wirecloth manufacturer, allows 10s. per week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
