      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <p>BIRTHS. MARRIAGES. DEATHS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>WAR TIJVIE CHRISTMAS.</head>
        <p>A WAR TIJVIE CHRISTMAS.</p>
        <p>How the Great Festival was Observed Locally.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>CHASTENED BUT SINCERE.</head>
        <p>CHASTENED BUT SINCERE.</p>
        <p>Christmastide was observed locally in a way at once becoming and dignified. At most of our public institutions there was sufficient dis- tinctiveness to impress those who took part in the festival with the fact that it was Christmas, while, on the other hand, the pleasures of the occasion were subdued, in keeping with the calamitous times through which we are passing. The streets were free from the unruly scenes by which the season is sometimes dis- graced. Soldiers of the King, home for a short holiday, were to be seen in plenty, their dress and their smart bearing lending a realistic touch to the kind of life to which we are now becoming accustomed—in which we have such constant reminders that we are engaged in a life-and-death war.</p>
        <p>"Business as usual" has been the motto, and the shopkeepers have had a busy time. Good old Santa Claus went.his " usual " rounds, and "as usual" the recipients of his presents were delighted. The wounded soldiers in our midst were also given a comparatively good time, and, indeed, everyone seems to have enjoyed a "real" Christmas.</p>
        <p>There was a touch of the old-fashioned Christmas in the weather, for on Friday morn- ing the earth wore a becoming mantle of hoar- frost, and there was something refreshing and exhilarating in the sharp, keen air. In the morning, under such ideal Christmas condi- tions, and with such a picturesque setting, large numbers, of people journeyed to the varions places of worship, which had been neatly decorated in appropriate fashion.</p>
        <p>A hearty vote of thanks was accorded the Mayor and Mayoress and the artistes, on the proposition of Mr. Parr.</p>
        <p>GIFTS FOR PATIENTS.</p>
        <p>The usual distribution of gifts to the patients took place on Tuesday afternoon. Lady Greens; 11 handed the soldiers their presents, along with clasp knives and tobacco, and Mrs. Rylands, with Miss Henn, who deputised for Mrs. Armitage, distributed the other gifts. The members of the staff also received a gift each. Violin selections were played by Mr. R. H. Pear- son, and Mr. Guthrie presided at the piano: Amongst those present were Mr. J. H. Smeth- urst, Mrs. Smethurst, Mr. W. P. Rylands, Mr. F. A. Frost, Mr. W. Daintith, Mr. John Fair- dough, and Mr. J. H. J. Hampson. (secretary of the institution).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>GOOD CHEER AT THE WORKHOUSE</head>
        <p>GOOD CHEER AT THE WORKHOUSE</p>
        <p>The inmates at the Workhouse thoroughly enjoyed themselves, for they had a sumptuous dinner, and in the evening a concert pro- moted by the officers was given for their bene- fit. The Master and Matron are to be congratu- lated for the expellent taste shown in the decorations which had been carried out under their supervision. The Mayor and Mayoress, with Master Reginald Peacock, were present, with the fol- lowing: The Rev. E. Downhara (acting chap- lain), the Rev. H. Ogden (Nonconformist minister), Messrs. J. A. Hughes and W. C. Brooks (Guardians), Dr. Jaggar (resident medi- cal officer), Miss Emmerton (superintendent nurse), and Alderman James Evans. On behalf of the House Committee, Mr. Brooks wished the inmates and those present the compliments of the season. The Master said they, were all pleased and proud to have the Mayor and Mayoress with them that day., (Hear, hear.) Alluding to the war, he said that although they were in the midst of their festivities they could not forget the brave lads at the front, and it was pleasure, able to read that there were so Many people in England who were sending presents for those who were in the trenches. (Applause.) '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>BLESSINGS ON OUR TROOPS.</head>
        <p>BLESSINGS ON OUR TROOPS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. Ogden said that they should re- member that it was only thrbugh the brave men on the sea and in the trenches that they were able to have that and similar feasts that day. He would therefore ask for God's bles- sings on those brave men who were fighting for their King and country against those Who had been rightly called the "Baby-killers." (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Three hearty cheers were given for the Rev. W. Bracecamp, who is now at Tunbridge Wells. The Mayor expressed his pleasure at once again meeting •the inmates, and, referring to the war, said that except at Scarborough no great disaster had occurred in this country. They must not be depressed because that town and the Hartlepools had been bombarded by German murderers, for they were all confident that our Navy would be able to prevent them from doing any more harm. He was sure they would never get: as far as Warrington. If the Germans did land in England they would most surely get a very 'warm reception. (Laughter and applaUse.) A vote of thanks was accorded the Mayor and the Mayoress, on the proposition of Alderman James Evans.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>ID6ATE HOMES' CHILDREN AS ACTORS.</head>
        <p>ID6ATE HOMES' CHILDREN AS ACTORS.</p>
        <p>At the Cottage Homes thq children had a most enjoyable time, Mr. and Mrs. Lawrence, the superintendent and matron, having done all that was possible to make the Christmas as bright and cheery as possible for their charges. The Mayor and Mayoress attended during din- ner, and, accompanied by Mrs. Hall and 'the Suovrintendent and Matron, they inspected the various departments in this branch of local Poor Law administration. In a brief speech, The Mayor said that a great deal of credit was due to the Superintendent and Matron and the staff, who took such a kindly interest in the welfare of the children.</p>
        <p>Three hearty cheers were given the Mayor and Mayoress, at the instance of the Superintendent. "Beauty and the Beast" was the title of a sketch in which the Children appeared in the evening, and the manner in which they acted their parts proved that Mrs. Ililditch's efforts in training thetn had met with success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>COMRADES, FIGHTING AND FALLEN</head>
        <p>COMRADES, FIGHTING AND FALLEN</p>
        <p>" Tommy Atkils " knows how to enjoy Christ- mas, and a peep into Orford Barracks would have revealed the fact that here he was no exception. In addition to the recruits, there were several wounded " Toramies," *ho en- joyed themselves as much as possible. The ex- cellent arrangementa had been made by Sergt.- Major Quick, Colour-Sergt. Stevens, and a small committee. The dining-room had been artistic- ally decoraterl with seasonable greetings to the officers, flags and festoons of evergreens. The "boys" at the front were not forgotten, for one cheery motto ran, "The best of health and a safe return to our comrades at the front." There was, however, quite a pathetic touch about the wreath of holly, with its inscription, "In memory of our comrades who have fallen at the front."</p>
        <p>The dinner was a great success, as was also the concert in the evening, over which Sergt.-Major Quick presided. Many pleasing and entertain- ing items were rendered by the following; The Chairman, Capt. Stott, Sergts. Walker. Wil- liams and Campbell, Corpl. Capping, Lance- Corpl. Davis, Drummer Hughes, and Ptes. Pratt, Moss, Roberts and Chadwick. Mr. Dan Jones was an efficient accompanist. The loyal tdfists having been honoured and tribute paid to the fallen solaiers, the Chair- man proposed the health of Colonel Moggridge and the officers of the depot. In reply, Colonel Moggridge spoke of the good feeling which existed bezween the officers and the men. The extraordinary cheerfulness of the Brutish soldier a.! the front La i inatieFsed every- one., At home they were comfortably seated, being amused, and on other days than Christ- mm they were well looked after with the blan- kets and fires. That ought to make them think more of the fellows at the front, who were en- during extreme hardships in such a bright and happy manner. They should drink to the health of "The British Soldier."</p>
        <p>The toast having been honoured, Sergt. Camp- bell proposed iv; vote of thanks to the enter- tainment committee, which was carried in a hearty fashion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>WOUNDED AT THE INFIRMARY.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED AT THE INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>Christmas was made exceedingly pleasant for the w9unded soldiers in the Infirmary, for, in addition to the usual Christmas fare, an excel- lent concert was given in the afternoon in the military ward. The Mayor and Mayoress, Mr. J. H. Smethurst, Mr. R. C. Parr, Miss C. Hammond (matron), the medical staff, and nurses were also present. Miss Gladys Toft and Miss Craven delighted with their songs, and Mr. Walter Moores caused much laughter with his humorous numbers. Messrs. A. Prow and W. B. Randall were the accompanists. The Mayor expressed his best wishes to the soldiers, and said he was certain that the people of Warrington would do all they could for them whilst they were in the town. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>MUSICAL ENTERTAINMENT.</head>
        <p>MUSICAL ENTERTAINMENT.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon a musical entertain- ment, arranged by Mr. W. B. Randall, organist, was given to the inmates of the Infirmary in the military ward, those who kindly assisted in a very enjoyable programme being Madam Sara Silcock, Mr. Hatton, Mr. Hannan and Mr. R. H. Pearson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>CAROLS AT RADDON COURT.</head>
        <p>CAROLS AT RADDON COURT.</p>
        <p>At Raddon Court Temporary Red Cross Hospital the soldiers were also well looked after and fully appreciated all that had been done for their benefit and elijoyment. Several parties of carollers sang at the hospital on Christmas Eve, and on Christmas Day almost all the British soldiers were able to go to Chrtst Church for the morning service, 'Lind afterwards enjoyed a typical Christmas dinner. Three large turkeys and six plum puddings, all gifts of kind friends, were served, and amuse- ment was caused by the rings and sixpences hidden within the latter. Dr. Bower acted as carver, and during dessert speeches were made by several of the men, expressing their grati- tude for the treatment received at Raddon Court. Cheers were given for the King and his Allies.</p>
        <p>In the afternoon the Mayor and Mayoress distributed the presents off the Christmas tree, every patient and every member of the staff receiving a parcel. At tea, among other dainties, was a large iced cake, the gift of Miss Amy Grounds, who had sold patriotic badges in order to buy it. The evening was happily spent in playing games. Besides presents from local friends, the sol- diers received a Christmas card from the King and Queen, with portraits of Their Majesties, and a box of cigarettes with a pretty portrait on the lid from Queen Alexandra. On Boxing Day a deputation of Messrs. Cros- field's workgirls came with gifts of soap, &amp;c., in boxes, and an entertainment was given by a class of girls, under the, supervision of Mrs. Grounds. Miss Sylvia Broadbent explained that these children had been sewing for the patients since the beginning of the war, and had collected cigarettes and khaki handkerchiefs for all the men.</p>
        <p>At th 6 conclusion of the entertainment three cheers were given for the men in the trenches and the National Anthems of the Allies were sung.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>TEA PARTY AND ENTERTAINMENT.</head>
        <p>TEA PARTY AND ENTERTAINMENT.</p>
        <p>On Monday a tea party and entertainment were given at the hospital, to which were in- vited, .besides the present and past nursing staff, all the 'members of Cheshire and Cheshire 48, the township leaders of the Dares- bury 8.R.C.5., and a few other friends. The hall, day-room and wards had been very pret- tily decorated with flags, Chinese lanterns and evergreens, largely owing to the kindness of Mr. Hutton, who sent experienced men to help the staff in carrying out the decorations. It was pleasing to see the numerous members of detachments in their trim Red Cross uniform in the gaily-adorned hall, where tea was served. The entertainment that followed was of an interesting and varied character. Several of the nurses contributed songs, violin solos, dances and recitations, and muoh talent was shown among the patients. Private Bell and Sapper)Reast sang, Private Mann recited with much pathos, Private Frogget gave a clever selection on the mouth organ, and Private Boodle whistled to the accompaniment of the gramophone. Several of the Belgian soldiers sang Flemish and French songs, while one sang "Tipperary." Mrs. Broadbent presided, and said she took a deep interest tin the hospital. She was 'de- lighted to see the men improving in health, and hoped they would look back with pleasure to the Christmas spent at Raddon Court, and that in the year which was soon to begin peace might reign on the earth. Cheers were given for the men in the trenches, for the Allies, for the Navy, and for Mrs. Broadbeiit.</p>
        <p>The Matron-read the translation of a letter of thanks from the senior Belgian patient, thank- ing the staff in pretty and touching language.</p>
        <p>CERTIFICATES FOR NURSES.</p>
        <p>Tho presentation of certificates at Jiaddon/ Court was another pleasing ceremony during Christmastide. Mrs. Lyon, of Appleton, kindly officiated, an I complimentA the nurses on the</p>
        <p>useful work they Were doing. The certificates were awarded to those members of the V.A.D.S., ten in number, who had passed an advanced examination in nursing conducted by Dr. Bowes and Dr. Law, the lecturer having been Nurse Phillips. The remainder of the certificates, 17 in number, were awarded for a first success in nursing. The examiner was Dr. lipdgkinson. Keen interest has been felt ,in *the neighbour- hood in nursing since the outbreak of 'war, and several courses of lectures have been held. Several courses of first-aid have also been held for both men and women, and in January more lectures will be given at the Parr Hall. War- rington having been affiliated to the Daresbury Division temporarily until the Lancashire organisation embraces it, Miss Waring, Latch- ford House, has kindly consented to become</p>
        <p>township leader, and any inquiries as to the forthcoming lectures in Warrington should be addressed to her.</p>
        <p>AN UP-TO-DATE HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>The D.D.11.5. of the Western Command recently inspected the hospital and expressed his pleasure at the arrangements, which, he said, were verikup to date. Dr. Burton, deputy county director for the baresbury Division, also expressed his satisfaction, after an inspection.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>BLUE COAT SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>BLUE COAT SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>At the Blue Coat School on Christmas Eve the members of the band played several appro- priate selections and hymns, and afterwards en- joyed mince pies and coffee. On Christmas morning the children attended Trinity Church, and later enjoyed a substantial dinner and an excellent tea, which had been generously con- tributed to by Mr. G. Dakin and Messrs. Carter and Son. Games of various kinds were after- wards played until bed time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>" TOMMIES" " KIDDIES ENTERTAINED.</head>
        <p>" TOMMIES" " KIDDIES ENTER- TAINED.</p>
        <p>About a hundred and fifty of the children of men -belonging to St. Peter's Parish who have gone to the Front were, on Thursday, enter- tained to tea in the Schoolroom by Mr, Reuben Higham. Full justice having been done to the good things provided each little guest received a present, at the hands of Mrs. Higham, from a huge Christmas tree.</p>
        <p>Significant. In a letter published by a German newspaper a Landwehr officer writes: "On the German front officers and men do not salute in the usual way, but by saying God punish England,' while the reply is 'May He punish. England. " This admission that the Germans themselves cannot do it is significant.—" Punch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>ENEMY RESOURCES.</head>
        <p>ENEMY RESOURCES.</p>
        <p>How Long Can Germany Stand</p>
        <p>the Strain?</p>
        <p>BY C. E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>There are many who think that the present war will come to an end not by the defeat of Germany on the battle field, but by exhaustion in the economic and industrial spheres. The question is a very complicated one, and I do not profess to offer any definite opinion, but would try to put before ray readers some of the factors which will enable them to form their own judgment. Urgent patriotic appeals are now being made to•the German people to bring in all the gold they have in coin and jewellery to the Treasury in exchange for German notes, which by the way, are not of face value, even if they could be re-exchanged. The notes are issued without any proportionate backing in gold, as with our British notes, and no gold is paid out by the banks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Why This Concern About Gold?</head>
        <p>Why This Concern About Gold?</p>
        <p>_ . The- people cannot eat gold, or clothe them- selves with it, or build houses of it, nor does it enter into the materials for the manufacture of ammunition or equipment for war. If the German people are certain of the solvency of their country, and have confidenCe that it can ultimately redeem all the paper money issued at face value, then as long as Germany is self- sustaining, and has a printing press to make notes for currency, she is independent of gold. If sufficient labour can be got to produce food, this vital requirement is not of immediate im- portance. Germany, in normal times, grows nine-tenths of the food required, and one-tenth can with economy be done without. That the need for this economy is realised is evident from the following advice which has been given to the public :</p>
        <p>"You should do as far as possible with- out eggs, for hens consume corn which in one way or another can be used for human beings. "You should eat little or no meat at breakfast or supper, and certain days of the</p>
        <p>week should go without meat altogether. "Use baking powder instead of eggs, milk instead of meat, and jam instead of butter. "Fat and suet must be employed as if it were fine gold, for it is one of the most valuable foods in the trenches, since it gives warmth. Every bread crust has its value?' With such economy Germany can, in food stuffs, hold out for a long time. If she were as well supplied with the necessities for ammuni- tion, accoutrement and the like, the exhaustion of Germany might be a long way off. But there. are several essential war products which Ger- many cannot make, or make in anything like sufficient quantities. The principal of these are cepper, rubber, lead, tin and the like. These will have to be bought abroad. Now as exports have almost 'ceased, it is difficult to see how Germany can pay for these by exchange. Ignoring for the moment the blockade of con- traband -goods, the only method left by which these necessary things can be procured is by payment in gold. No country will accept Ger- man notes, not alone because they have a depre- ciated value, but also because they are not negotiable in the world's market. Austria and Turkey are the solitary exceptions. At the present time .German paper money is being forced on Turkey. Hence gold, which has its value in itself, and is not a mere promise to pay, is the only tender which Germany can offer to outsiders, and this is the reason why the German Government is appealing to the patriot- ism of the people to pay in gold to the banks and take paper money in exchange. Suppose the German Government got sufficient gold to purchase the requisite materials, there is still the question of how they are to get the materials into the country. The small neutral powers adjacent to Germany cannot supply = the requisites, and they must be procured mainly from North and South America and smuggled in through adjoining countries. Supposing private individuals elude the scrutiny made at the port of shipment, the vessels run the *k of being examined and seized by British men of war.</p>
        <p>Germany Prepared, But Not For Long. It is certain Germany had large supplies in stock at the commencement of war, but it is equally certain that Germany did not contem- plate, and did not prepare for a war of the present magnitude and probable duration. Hence her warnings to her army to be careful of ammunition,' and her feverish anxiety to gather together in the hands of the Govern- ment all the gold in the country. We see then the value of gold to Germany in her present position and also the possibility of the gold not being sufficient; and even if suffi- cient, there is the difficulty of effecting the necessary exchange to carry on the war for any long period. Hence the period of Germany's exhaustion must come, and the sooner it comes I for herself the better.</p>
        <p>People talk with great assurance of the huge indemnity Germany will have to pay for the wrong she has done, but it is difficult to see from what source this indemnity is to be paid, especially if the drain in her resources and consequent destruction of her wealth goes on for a lengthy period. The gold reserve, even if intact at the end of the war, is altogether insufficient, and the only way in which a large indemnity can be paid is by postponement, thus mortgaging the toil of the Germans, in future generations. Here we have one of the) anomalies of the war. We trust to indemnities partially to recoup our- selves and our Allies, and every day that war is prolonged the ability of Germany to pay an indemnity is lessened by the destruction of the wealth out of which it could be paid. Were we to try and take this indemnity in kind by seizing the assets of Germany, what could we take? There is considerable value in the plant of the German industries, but thiS would enor- mously depreciate by removal, and its removal would make it still more difficult for Germany to pay the part of the indemnity, of necessity postponed. We might get a part of the German fleet.' bit this would be little use in rebuilding devastated Belgium. Perhaps the best thing that could be done with the ships would be to dismantle them and sell the old iron to make bridges to re- place those destroyed, thus converting them from instruments of destruction to those of con- struction. Drifting to Bankruptcy. •</p>
        <p>Financially, Germany appears to be drifting towards bankruptcy; if not bankruptcy, she is piling up, debt at an enormous rate, which future generations will have to pay. She was increasing this debt before the war, the cost of the fleet and the army being a terrible drain upon the national resources. She is spending on the war at least £2,000,000 a day, over and above what she is able to wring out of the people in the lands she has. devastated. The borrowing of money for the war is costing her 18 per cent. more than in our own case, because of her inferior finantlial status, and the limita- tion of the area from which she can borrbw. The rate of exchange has generally been against her, hence for anything she buys outside her borders she has to pay much more than we have here, and in addition the extra freights ship- pers demand for carrying contraband goods. She has lost in value of ships and cargoes between £50,000,000 and. £80,000,000. This in- dustry is entirely out of action, and no longer earns, as in normal times, freights to the value of £100,000,000.</p>
        <p>Germany's normal national income depended largely upon import taxes on goods coming from outside her borders; this has nearly all stopped. The peculiar idea which some enter- tained that the foreigner pays the tax is now of no significance, because the goods are not going in; hence the tax is not collected. Nobody pays, and the Government depending on the tax is short by this amount. The strain in Ger- many is therefore tremendous; it comes from two sides, through diminished income and greatly increased expenditure. I cannot see how Possibly Germany can con- tinue the war more than six montlis. The strain will be too great, and she will break under it—Probably before June.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Winter Diet</head>
        <p>A Winter Diet</p>
        <p>should include plenty of heat producers. TM, most wholesome and delicious are puddings made with Shredded ATORA Beef Suet, which needs no ehoppivag. Ask your ,grocer for it. Sole Manufacturers—Hugon &amp; Co., Ltd., Man- chester.</p>
      </div>
