      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Musical Success.</head>
        <p>Musical Success.</p>
        <p>,),Liss Dorothy Harding, pupil of Miss Jessie l'a.rrar, A.T.C.L., has been successful in passing the junior examination in connection with the Trinity College of Music, London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Gift of Clothing.</head>
        <p>Gift of Clothing.</p>
        <p>The Rev. William T. Jaye, inCjumbent.of EM- manuel Church, acknowledgeo. with grateful thanks the receipt of a parcel of clothing from the Warrington Ntedlework</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Sanctioned.</head>
        <p>Sanctioned.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board have given their sanction to the borrowing by the Town Council of X 4,192 for the purchase of land for the ex- tension of Victoria Park, the money to be repaid in sixty years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Christmas-Week Alt eather.</head>
        <p>Christmas-Week Alt eather.</p>
        <p>The warmest day in Christmas Week was Boxing Day, when a maximum temperature of 48 degrees was recorded. The coldest day re- cently was December 22nd, when the glass dropped down to 23 degrees.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Corn Merchant's £lO,OOO</head>
        <p>Corn Merchant's £lO,OOO</p>
        <p>The late Mr. William Barlow, of 39, Bewsey- street, who carried on a corn and flour business in Winwick-street, and who passed away at the age of 86, left a fortune amounting to A:3,857, probate of his will having been granted recently.</p>
        <p>Spiritualism. The local Spiritualist Society announce a re- turn visit of the wonderful clairvoyant, my. Tom Tyrrell, of Blackburn, for to-morrow. Mr. Tyrrell will speak at 3.15 and 630. It is claimed for this gentleman that he gives striking proof of the continuity of life after what is generally calltd death.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Diminution in Crime.</head>
        <p>A Diminution in Crime.</p>
        <p>The records at the Police Station show a diminution in crime in the borough during 1914, and, with only one exception, the cases dealt With have not been of other than the normal nature. Fourteen members of the .Borough Force have enlisted and gone to the front. Of this number, one has been killed, another serionsly wounded, and a third invalided home. All the others are presumably uninjured.</p>
        <p>Warrington Markel. nusiness was quiet at this week's market, fLiwing to the holidays, there being few samples '-'nered. Wheat still continued to harden in whilst oats were quite at late rates, viz., s. 6d. to 3s. 9d. per 451 b. Other prices fetched Were: OM straw, 21s. to 225. per ton; wheat straw, 21s. to 225. per ton; cloveehay, 3 ss. Per ' ton; meadow 'hay', ',.1t2 15s. fiei; ten, f.o:r.; XX tour, 365. 6d.. per 2801 b. ; best bran; Is. .4d. per 201 b.</p>
        <p>A Vtreek of Prayer. Next Week will be observed a week of prayer in various of the Free Churches of the town. Monday evening's prayer meeting will be in the Independent ,Methodist Church, Friars' Green; TueSday's in the Bold-street Wesleyan Church, and Wednesday's in St. John's Presbyterian Church, whilst on Sunday, January 10th, a united communion service will be held in the Golborne-street Baptist Church.</p>
        <p>New Rifle Range.</p>
        <p>The rifle range adjoining the—Baths has now been completed and has been inspected by the War Office Authorities.</p>
        <p>Christmas Communicants.</p>
        <p>No fewer than 620 persons made Holy Com- munion at the Parish Church on Christmas Day.</p>
        <p>Rev. T. Vickers' Departure Delayed. After all, the Rev. T. 'Vickers will not be leaving St. Clement's Church, at least, not for the present, the war having made it impos- sible for the authorities to carry out arrange- ments at present with missionary clergy desir- ing to serve the Church in that part of the world chosen as his sphere of labour by the rev. gentleman, viz., East Africa.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Lady's Demise. With regret we record the demise of Mrs. H. Colley, of Rolleston-street, Warrington, which took place at her home on Wednesday morning, after a lengthy illness. Mrs. Colley—who was formerly a valued worker in Wycliffe Congrega- tional Church circles, and to whose death the Pastor (the Rey. T. T. James) suitably referred at the Watch-night service—leaves a husband and son to mourn their sad loss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>111 r. J. R, Wardle Retiring.</head>
        <p>111 r. J. R, Wardle Retiring.</p>
        <p>A. very pleasing Ceremony took place on Thurs- day ip Messrs. Crosfield's Works' Dining Hall, when Mr J. R. Wardle (late secretary of the company) was presented\ by Mr. A. V. Baxter, on behalf of the directors, managers, and general office staff of the firm, with a silver tea and coffee service. Over two hundred employes of the commercialo department were present to wish Mr; Wardle every happiness on his retire- ment. An elegant gold watch bracelet was also handed to Mr. Wardle for presentation to Mrs. Wardle.</p>
        <p>Successful Secondary Schoolboys. In the list of successful candidates for the Second Division Clerkships (Civil Servace) recently issued, the names of two Secondary School pupils - are included. Walter C. Balm- ford secured the 140th place, and Samuel B. Williamson the 175th position. The former has received an appointment at the War Office. - Leonard Oakley, of 155, Wellfield-street, War- rington, a scholar of the same school for the past three years, has been succe4sful in passing the examination for entry as Boy Artificer into ILAI. Navy, and has, proceeded to Devonport to join his ship. Leonard Oakley was recom- mended by the Education Committee on the result of an examination .locally conducted, to compete with boys recommended by certain other Education Authorities in the country, And is the third Warrington boy to secure entry into the Navy as Boy Artificer by this means. ' Leather Trade Brisk.</p>
        <p>There was, writes the Warrington correspond- ent of the "Leather Trades Review," very little slackening in business before the holidays, leather of all classes moving out as quickly as made. Although there was naturally a reduced • number of inquiries, the fact remains that trade all round was far better than anything esperi- enced in previous Christmas ...vets. The fact that there have been no •equ-.±;ts this year for deliveries to be held over the italt•i:tys has. of .course, contributed towarls the present setis- factory position. Not for very many years past have there been such small stocks in the tanner- ies at the end of the year as there are at "re- sent. - Prices keep firm, and advaw,es are being steadily made on sLoulders and bellies. flat January • will see this movement gencr:...l is al- most a certainty, particularly if there is a .little quickening in the demand. Meanwh'lt,:tariners seem to grow more cautions, and are manifese.y disinclined to go much further ahead with their bookings, as, taking the cue from the utterances of certain Ministers, an opinion is held that the war May finish sooner. than was thought at one time.</p>
        <p>Railway Official Retires. Mr. S. Twigger, who has been "assistant in the electrical department of the L. and N—W. Railway at Warrington for the past ten years, , retired from the service on Wednesday last week. after completing 49 years with the company. Mr. Twigger is one of the remaining few who were Originally, in the service of the Electrical and International Telegraph Company, and who at that time did all The telegraph construction and work for the railway company—work which was in 1870 taken over by the Post Office on the commercial, side, and by the railway companies in so far as it applied to their work. To mark ' the occasion of • his retirement, Mr. Twigger's colleagues invited hint to join them at lunch at the Crewe Royal lintel, and there presented him' with 'a dining-room chiming clock, en- closed in rosewood ease, and on Wednesday evening he was the recipient of a pair of very pretty green bronze figure ornaments, 'which were presented to him by the staff at the War- rington depot. It is the vriah of all those who came in daily contaet with Mr. Twigger that kia may long live to enjoy his well-earned re- Lent, . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Local War Fonds.</head>
        <p>The Local War Fonds.</p>
        <p>The sum of £435 16s. 3d. has so far been sub- scribed to the Local War Relief Fund, being an increase of .236 3s. 11c1. during the week ended December 31st. Subscriptions received by the treasurers of the National Relief Fund up to this week total .:t4,526 19s. 4d., an increase of £Bl 16s. Id. over the previous week. The sum of .f.. 856 6s. Bd. has been received on account of the local War Refugees' Fund, and £l2B is. 10d. has been subscribed to the Warrington Tobacco Fund for the 2nd South Lancs.</p>
        <p>The total of the Prince of Wales' Fund yes- :erday was £4,283,000. Christmas at the Post Office.</p>
        <p>No fewer than 2,500,000 postal packets, &amp;c., were handled at the local Post office during the Christmas rush, and the very able and effi- cient manner in which these were dealt with reflects great credit on the Postmaster- (Mr. Chambers) and his capable staff, who, although handicapped through several of their fellow workers having joined the forces, made the best of the assistance they received from women workers and Boy Scouts, and did their utmost to ensure the people of Warrington and district getting their Christmas cards and parcels as early as possible.</p>
        <p>Loaves Go " Up " on Monday. In view .of recent increases in the price of flour,%the price of bread has been raised in all parts of the country. The Warrington and Dis- trict Master Bakers' Association give notice that the prices from Monday next per four-pound loaf will he: Best bread, 7111.; seconds bread, 60. The Warrington E.I. Co-operative Society give notice to the same effect. The increase is equal to one penny per loaf. The Co-operative Society state that "flop-, has advanced at least 10s. per sack over what it stood at in August. This is equal to lid. increased cost per 41b. loaf and fully justifies the proposed increase in the price of bread."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Watch-night Services.</head>
        <p>Watch-night Services.</p>
        <p>Watch Night serviCes were held on New Year's Eve in many Warrington churches —their significance this year being even deeper than in previous years.—At the Wycliffe Con- gregational Church service—which was very well attended—the Rev. T. T. James officiated, Mr. Epworth presiding at the organ. Speaking upon John ix. 4, "I must work the works of Rim that sent Me, while it is day; the night cometh when no man can work," the Pastor said that life was an opportunity sent by God, and it had a tremendous value—though modern theology (speaking of such in the wider sense) did not seem to think so. They must, however, realise that the time for taking the opportuni- ties of life was limited, and make the most of chances as they came.—At the Golborne-street Baptist Church the Rev. H. Ogden presided over a good cpngregation, and -Mr. S. 'tickers played the organ. The preacher spoke upon Psalm xc., emphasising the brevity of life, and pointing out the permanence only of work that was pleasing in Ile sight of God. Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The Pleasant Afternoon service at Wycliffe Congregational Church on Sunday' Was well attended. Mr. B. Farrar presided, and gave an address on the festive season. He re- ferred to the fact that owing to the war the Christmas celebrations had this year been tinged with a hote of sadness. Truly never had a Christmas been kept Amid such terrible strife, and yet even the Soldiers at the front, and the sailors on the ships of war at sea, had managed to experience something of the old happiness associated with the festival.—The Rev. T. T. James read letters from a number of old ViTycliffians now serving with the colours, in which the writers tendered thanks for the Christmas gifts forwarded them from the church, Sunday School and P.S.A. Society. He ' also read a letter written by, the Rev. W. Brace- camp, from Tunbridge Wells, where he is offi- ciating as chaplain to the 4th South Lancashire Regiment, in which he thanked the members ot the Society for the good wishes recently ex- pressed to him in a letter written on their behalf.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Yuletide .Nuptials.</head>
        <p>Yuletide .Nuptials.</p>
        <p>A pretty and interesting wedding took place at the Parish Church during the Christmas season, the contracting parties being Mr. Sam Shakeshaft-Lloyd. youngest son of Mr. Lloyd, of 3, Marsh-street, and Miss Lily Berry, only daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Berry, of 188, Gorsey- lane. The Rector (Canon Willis) officiated, and Mr. Darby presided at the organ, playing, amongst other -selections, Mendelssohn's Wed- ding March. The .bride, who was given away .by her father, was becomingly gowned in white satin, draped with pearls, orange blossom and chiffon. A wreath of orange blossom and myrtle encircled her head, and • she carried a fine bouquet of arum lilies. After the ceremony a company of over fifty-relatives and friends sat down to the wedding breakfast, the guests in- cluding Canon Willis, who made a brief speech, ,in which he wished the newly-married couple long life and happiness. Subsequently the happy pair left for Weston-super-Mare, where they will reside.. The bride's travelling cos- tume was of navy blue cloth, with a Parisian hat to match. The bridegroom formerly sang in the choir of the Parish Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S WARRINGTON TEAM</p>
        <p>To Meet St. Ilelens Away.</p>
        <p>After yesterday's niatch the Warrington team to meet St. Helens, at St. 4elens, to-day, was selected as follows J. Dainteth.</p>
        <p>J. Waywell, J. 11: E: Brookes (captain),</p>
        <p>_ J. Baker. S. Young, T. Laws. H. Cox, J. Fearnley, it Mather, M. Green, \ W. Cunlille, T. Cunliffe. Warrington last met St. Helens as recently as November 28th, when they beat them by 10 points to 3. Warrington "A" are without a match to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>SECONDARY SCHOOL CHRISTMAS</head>
        <p>SECONDARY SCHOOL CHRISTMAS</p>
        <p>SOCIALS.</p>
        <p>Scholars Who Have liaised £72 For</p>
        <p>War Funds.</p>
        <p>The usual socials were held in the School- rooms on Saturday, December 12th (juniors), and Saturday, Deeember 19th (seniors), the. attendance of 200 constituting a record. A long pfogramme of dancing was gone through, and the members of the teaching staff had little difficulty in persuading the pupils to take part in the various items. During' the interval refreshments were served in the Cookery Room, the staff acting as waiters and waitresses, executing the manifold orders of the school with dispatch and (shall we say?) due humility. The songs of Albert Greenough, Sophie Cross (juniors),. and Mary Greenough and Marion Littlewood (seniors) were received with hearty applause. As a result of the financial success of the socials it was decided to raise a fund for send- ing Christmas presents to the Secondary School boys serving with Ms Majesty's Forces. The sum . realised amounted to £7, Making a total of £72 collected by the teachers and pupils in mid of the various War Funds. ° t</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 2, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 2, 1915.</p>
        <p>THE FRIENDLY SOCIETY YEAR.</p>
        <p>Varying Experiences in an Unsettled Period.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>WAR AND INSURANCE BRAVELY FACED.</head>
        <p>WAR AND INSURANCE BRAVELY FACED.</p>
        <p>We have collected much information concern- ing the local Lodges of the several Friendly Societies, a summary of which we give below. The Independent Order of Oddfellows (Man- chester Unity) are not able to furnish details at present, and doubtless the same reason ex- plains the absence of mention of some other societies. These details will 'appear in due course.</p>
        <p>in health, and will claim the benefits which their membership allows them. STRICTER SICKNESS REGULATIONS.</p>
        <p>"We, in common with others, have paid away for sickness claims during the present year more than the average, and this has necessitated the adoption of stricter regulations governing this benefit. Greater attention will be paid to sick visiting by members of the society and the doc- tor must certify at least once St week that he has seen the member and state the nature of the illness causing total incapacity from work. We are loOking forward to the New Year with great joy and expectation, for next Whit-week our society will hold its annual Movable Dele- gation in Warrington. May the outcome be not only an increase numerically and financially to the Order of Druids, but to every • other society in the town !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>SHEPHERDS HAVE UNUSUALLY HEAVY SICKNESS.</head>
        <p>SHEPHERDS HAVE UNUSUALLY HEAVY SICKNESS.</p>
        <p>So far as the Warrington District of the Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds was concerned the year opened with unusually heavy sickness, the like of which was never before experienced amongst the members of the majority of the Lodges in the district, and which continued for tte first quarter of the year. Thanks to the careful management of the past, the society was in a better position to withstand the heavy call, Oat, still, as was only to be expected under the unusual strain, losses were made in some of the Lodges during the arst quarter; losses which, in spite of more normal sickness later on, were never overtaken, with the result that 'or the year the financial position does not close very satisfactorily. Numerically, there is a very slight decrease. , This is to be expected after the boom of the coming into operation of the Insurance Act, -then members joined the voluntary section of the society under the impression that it was necessary before they would be accepted for State Insurance. The result is that many members have during the last year allowed their membership to lapse, being content to be State- insured only. To this, and to the fact that recruiting for new members has been somewhat at a standstill during the year, .is attributed the slight decrease in membership. In February last a special meeting of the district was had to meet the Chief Shepherd of the Order Bro. Alex. licEwen), who had come to Warrington with a view to establishing a juvenile section of zhe , society in this district. This meeting was an outstanding feature of the year in the society; in fact, it constituted a red-letter event in the history of the society during the last 50 years. At this meeting a juvenile section was established, and so well has it prospered that at the end of the year nearly 200 young persons under the age of 16 years have entered into membership of the society. As time goes on the fruits of that meeting should have a beneficial effect on the parent society from a recruiting standpoint. The terms of membership of the juvenile section are very easy, and the prin- cipal inducement to young folk to join is the bonus in cash of 75 per cent. of the contributions paid in when the juvenile member attains the age of 21 years. The claims made upon the District Funeral Fund for death S during the year have been about normal, and a substantial gain has been made in this fund.</p>
        <p>At the outbreak of the war the society decided that all members serving with the Forces should be relieved from paying their contributions. This will apply to over eighty members in this district.</p>
        <p>At the commencement of the year the State section of the society was centralised, a fact which proved a great relief to the secretaries of the Lodges, who have now more time to look after the voluntary side of the society, which was apt to be neglected owing to the exacting nature of the work required from officers for the State section. Taking all into considera- tion, the society is fortunate to be in such a good position this year-end. It has, more than ever, proved its usefulness in times of distress and sickness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>MUCH SICKNESS: FEW DEATHS.</head>
        <p>MUCH SICKNESS: FEW DEATHS.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington District of the National Independent Ofder of Oddfellows—which com- prises the following Lodges: "Loyal Garibaldi" (196 members), "Loyal Perseverance" (58), "Loyal Prosperity" (59), and "Loyal Friend- ship" (156)—excessive sickness has been experi- encea, especially in the case of two Lodges, one of which, alone, paid out over £2OO in sickness benefits. But, although the sickness record was so heavy, the district was exceedingly fortunate in having had to meet only five death claims— a very small number out of a total membership of getting on for 500. The juvenile branch has a membership of 255, and its funds total £311; the funds of the Adult Lodge's standing at : " Garibaldi," £1,218; "Perseverance," '0t206; "Prosperity," £43; "Friendship," £1,248. The District Funeral Fund totals £477.</p>
        <p>About seventy members of the Lodges in the district are serving with thfi, colours, included in their number being the District Grand Master.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>AN ADAPTABLE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>AN ADAPTABLE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Reviewing the past year's work at a recent meeting of the local branch of the Rational Association Friendly Society, the Secretary (Mr. A. Lingard) reported that the branch had done fairly well as regards new members, considering thc existing conditions of the country. Friendly Soaieties had had to work hard since the Insur- ance Act came into foice, but there was one thing in particular he would like to mention in regard to that Act, and that was the fact that it had had the effect of bringing more children into their society, owing to their special system of maternity benefits. No other friendly society, he claimed, was better equipped for carrying out the Insurance Act than was the R.A.F.S., because it had tables of contributions and bene- fits to suit all grades of workers, desirous of supplementing the State benefits. _ _ . _ _ _</p>
        <p>A- new feature introduced into the manner of working the society is the 'holding of annual district or other meetings—under the direction of the Board of Management—whereat delegates from the different branches will meet for the purposes of discussion, &amp;c. It was reported to the meeting that Mr. Fred Andrews, who has held the position of chairman of the Warring- ton branch for the past seven years, had ()tiered himself for home defence and that his services had been accepted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>TIIE DRUIDS.</head>
        <p>TIIE DRUIDS.</p>
        <p>Local Grand Master Chosen.</p>
        <p>Bro. Edwin Allen, Warrfngton, was honoured in June by being chosen Grand Master of the Order of Druids at the annual Movable Dele- gation at Sheffield. The year 1914 has not been lucky for the Voluntary Friendly Societies. The member- ship throughout the country has very consider- ably decreased, and the sickness experience.has been above the average and actuarial expecta- tion. Voluntary thrift amongst the, young men of to-day cannot be considered popular, judging from the membership of the societies. It has been almost impossible to obtain new initiates, and many of the older members have lapsed. Of course, this has been an exceptional year, the war (coming at a time when by experience the societies look forward to increaiing their membership) has militated against success, and another factor working against the societies is the National Insurance Act. "Personally," writes Mr. Edwin Allen, secretary of the War- rington District of the Ancient Order of Druids, "I deeply regret this decrease, for the volnn- tary spcieties have done, and are still doing, a vast amount of- good. When we remember that last year the members paid in contributions for 'sickness and funeral benefits upwards of seven million pounds sterling and received in sick:- floss benefits upwards of five million pounds, no wonder those who take a deep interest in the movement desire the good work should continue and prosper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>£2OO ADDED TO THE FUNDS.</head>
        <p>£2OO ADDED TO THE FUNDS.</p>
        <p>"Speaking locally, my society deplores having to record a decrease in membership of 75 during the year, while on the other hand our funds have increased over ..t2OO during the same peiiod. Over 90 of our members have joined the Army or Navy, and their membership will be protected during their absence. I hope that this war will not act prejudicially upon our funds, but we cannot ignore the fact that pos- sibly some of the members will return injured</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>FORESTERS' EXPERIENCE.</head>
        <p>FORESTERS' EXPERIENCE.</p>
        <p>The Warrington District of the Ancient Order of Foresters, which has the honour of having the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) as its treasurer—Mr. Robert Thornley being secretary —is composed of seven Courts, viz. :—"J aaziel," "Mercy," "Warrington," "Hope," "May Duke," "Kidd," and " Winmarleigh," whilst, also, linked up with it is the Foresters' Burial Society. • The expetience of the Order locally has been much the same as that of the other Friendly Societies as regards sickness, whilst—and in this respect also it is like the others—many of its members are serving with the colours. On January 24th Brother Llewellyn Davies was, on the occasion of his retirement, after 24 years' service as secretary of Court "Mercy," presented with a suitably inscribed gold watch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>N.D.F.S. HAS A SUCCESSFUL YEAR.</head>
        <p>N.D.F.S. HAS A SUCCESSFUL YEAR.</p>
        <p>The Warrington District of the National Deposit Friendly Society has experienced a suc- cessful year, although a larger percentage of sick claims than usual has had, to be met. The majority of such claims have come from mem- bers who have joined the State section only. The society is still increasing ,its membership locally, the number of new members made dur- ing the year being quite up to the average. There are now over 200 members in Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>FREE GARDENERS' BAD SIX</head>
        <p>FREE GARDENERS' BAD SIX</p>
        <p>For the first six months the "Moss Rose" (Warrington) Lodge of the National United Order ef Free Gardeners' Friendly Society was working at a loss, which, in the whole, totalled nearly £2O; but since July things improved considerably up till about a fortnight ago, when the bad weather setting. in, things commenced to go awry again. Twenty-four.members of the Lodge have enlisted, out of a,total membership of 120, and a levy will be made to keep them in benefit. Despite bad times, however, the Lodge is able to show a gain of nearly £2O on the year's working.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>ITEMS OF INTEREST.</head>
        <p>ITEMS OF INTEREST.</p>
        <p>For the first time in the history of modern Buffaloism in Warrington the Fourth Degree of the Roll of Honour Exaltation was conferred upon Bro. Thomas Davies at the "Tom Sang- ster" Lodge in February last. Quite a red-letter day in the history of the local Friendly Society movement was Saturday, January 17th, when the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock)—already a .Forester of nearly a quar- ter of a century's standing—was initiated as a member of the 'Prince of Wales' " Lodge of the Manchester Unity of Oddfellows. On Thursday, June 18th, his Worship and Mrs. Peacock invited a large 'company of the members of local Friendly Societies to an "At Home" in the Town Hall, the occasion being chosen by Friendly Society men and women in the town as an appropriate one for presenting Bro. and Sister Peacock with• a handsome solid silver tea and coffee set.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, November 14th, P.G. Bro. Wm. Watson, of the 1.0. F. (Manchester Unity) was presented with a gold watch and chain in recog- nition of 'tis services during fifty years' mem- bership of the Widows' and Orphans' Fund, for thirty of years of which he had acted as trea- surer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>TERRITORIALS'CHRISTMAS</head>
        <p>TERRITORIALS'CHRISTMAS</p>
        <p>A Soldiers' "Christmas Banquet" at Tunbridge. "THE FAMOUS YEAR 1914."</p>
        <p>We. hear on good authority that the 4th South Lancashires-7-in fact, soldiers to the number of s,ooo—stationed in Tunbridge Wells had a very go*od t,ime on Christmas Day. There were the "usual services in the morning, conducted by the Chaplain, the Rev. W. Eracecamp, which were very well attended. In the afternoon sports in connection with each battalion were held, while in the evening a sumptuous repast was provided, one gentle- man of the town giving 2,500 dinners and a souvenir programme menu. The remainder were provided for in the same generous way by other residents of 'Tunbridge Wells. The Aouvenir is an artistic production, set- ting out the names of the countries forming the Empire, with the reminder that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>"United We Stand."</head>
        <p>"United We Stand."</p>
        <p>The menu was: Roast turkey (stuffed), roast ribs of beef, roast loin pork and apple sauce, baked and boiled potatoes, Brussels sprouts, savoy cabbage, Christmas plum pudding, mince pies and dessert. The sentiment was as hearty as the invita- tion to eat. It ran :</p>
        <p>"Hearty. greetings and welcome from Tun- bridge Wells to the King's soldiers quartered in the town and their Belgian comrades on Christ- mas Day, in the famous year 1914." The men are in excellent health, and their one ambition is to get to the front as soon as 1 possible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>"A REAL, GOOD SPIRITUAL TIME."</head>
        <p>"A REAL, GOOD SPIRITUAL TIME."</p>
        <p>Rev. W. Bracecamp Writes of His Work With 4th South Lancs.</p>
        <p>Writing to the members of the Wycliffe P.S:A. Society--who forwarded him., a resolutiott ex- pressing their good wishes on the occasion oil his leaving Warrington to take up his duties as chaplain to the 4th South Lancashire Re *- 4f, ment, at present quartered at Tunbridge W a —the Rev. W. Bracecamp,i vicar of St. Pau 's, Warrington, says : " I have been having a real, good spiritual time among my boys. I am thankful to say that Many of them have decided for Christ since they came here. I am more and more con- vinced that I am exactly where God would have me be. I have plenty to do. I preached no less than six times last Sunday, and at the moment I do not know how I am to get through the work on Christmas Day. Gentleman's Gigantic Dinner Party.</p>
        <p>. . . You will be glad to know that the boys are going to be well looked after on Christ- mas Day. One gentleman is giving a Christmas dinner to 2,500, and the other inhabitants are feeding the remaining 2,500. I hope the mem- bers (of the P.S.A. Society) are attending regu- larly to their duty. There must be no falling away now. We want to pray and work more than ever. We must settle the question of war for all time, no matter what the sacrifice we have 'to make. . . . lam trying to get a few days' leave, but I do not know whether it will come off. It is hard to be away from loved ones at this time, but it is no worse for me than it is for thousands of others."</p>
        <p>The Only Thing to Save the Situation. The reverend gentleman concludes his letter with an expression of the hope that God's rich- est blessing may rest upon the work at Wycliffe, "and that each member of the P.S.A. -Society may realise that true religion is the only thing that can possibly save the situatioa:'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>NEW ORGAN</head>
        <p>NEW ORGAN</p>
        <p>FOR ST. JAMES CHURCH,</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>OPENED BY MR. DARBY.</p>
        <p>Until recently St. James' Church, Latchford, which was built in 1829, containedian organ, the date of which was buried in antiquity. We do not find the instrument mentioned in the church records, and no one appears to know anything of its origin. This old organ was added to by Messrs. Gray and Davison, who have had it under their care for over 50 years. There was no builder's name on the organ. It contained two manuals and pedal clavier. The Great Organ CC to F had eight speaking stops, Stopped Diapason, Open Diapason, Gamba Principal, Twelfth, Fifteenth, three rank Mix- ture, and Trumpet. The Swell Organ Tenor C to F had four speaking stops, Stopped Diapason, Open Diapason, Hautbois, and Principal. The Pedal Organ CCC .to C (two octaves) had one stop Bourdon. There were two couplers, Great to Pedal and Swell to Great.</p>
        <p>CHOIRMAN'S FRUITFUL SUGGESTION. For a long time this organ had been a grief to the church authorities. The choir, also, shared this grief, believing that a more modern organ, with its 20th century voicing, would be a better background and support ,for their singing. So one of the senior choirmen, with over 20 years' service as a chorister, gave a sovereign towards a new organ. This was accepted by the present incumbent (the Rev. H. B. Firth), who agreed to have a special collection in church. The result of this collection was that over £4O was raised and added to the sovereign. The Vicar then wrote to the Carnegie Trust, who eventu- ally kindly promised half the amount towards the total cost. The parishioners set to work, and have accomplished the other half, the re- sult being that the new organ is built and, we believe, paid for at an approximate cost of £4OO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>SPECIFICATION OF THE NEW ORGAN.</head>
        <p>SPECIFICATION OF THE NEW ORGAN.</p>
        <p>The specification of the new organ, which has been built by Messrs. Rushworth and Dreaper, Ltd., Liverpool, is as follows: Two Complete Manuals, Compass CC to C, 61 notes. Pedal Clavier, Compass CCC to F, 30 notes. Great Organ (Wind Pressure 41n.).</p>
        <p>_ 1. Lieblich Bordun, wood, 16ft., 61 pipes. 2. Open Diapason, metal, Bft., 51 pipes. 3. Dolce, metal, Bft. 61 pipes. 4. Hob]. Flute, wood, Bft., 61 pipes. 5. Principal, metal, 4ft., 61 pipes. 6. Grave Mixture, two ranks, metal, 3ft. and 2ft., 122 pipes. Swell Organ (Wind_ Pressure 3/in.).</p>
        <p>1. Lieblich Gedact, wood, Bft., 61 pipes. 2. Echo Gamba, spotted metal, Bft., 61 pipes. 3. Harmonic Flute, metal, 4ft., 61 pipes. 4. Horn (Harm. Trebles), metal, Bft., 61 pipes. 5. Contra Oboe, metal, 16ft., 61 pipes. 6. Tremulant. Pedal Organ (Wind Pressure 4in.).</p>
        <p>1. Sub Bass, wood, 16ft., 30 pipes. 2. Bordun (from Great), wood, 16ft. Couplers.</p>
        <p>1. Swell Octave. 2. Swell to Great. 3. Great o Pedal. 4. Swell to Pedal. Accessories.</p>
        <p>2 Combination Foot Pistons to the Great Organ. 2 Combination Foot Pistons to the Swell Organ. Balanced Swell Pedal. Tubular Pneumatic Action throughout. A separate Reservoir is provided for the Swell Organ. The stop jambs are at an angle of 45 degrees. The case is of beautiful panelled oak. Front pipes are silvered. The pedal board is concave and radiating.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>DEDICATION.</head>
        <p>DEDICATION.</p>
        <p>, The dedication of the new organ took place on Christmas Day, immediately before the 8 a.m. Holy Communion service. The, surpliced choir and clergy proceeded from the vestry to the choir stalls, from whence the Vicar \dedicated the new organ. It was opened by the organist, Mr. E. Davies, and used for the first time at the 8 a.m. Holy Communion service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>FORMAL OPENING.</head>
        <p>FORMAL OPENING.</p>
        <p>Local Organisrs Speak Highly of New Organ.</p>
        <p>The formal opening took place on Wednesday, when the accomplished organist of the War- rington Parish Church, Mr. F. M. Darby, gave an excellent recital.</p>
        <p>The programme included: Movements from "Magnificat " (a), (b), (c), (d), Lemargre; Air and Variations in A, Hesse; Arcadian Idyll, Lemare; Toccata and Fugue in D minor, Bach; Evening Song, Bairstow ; hymn 550; Overture to Athalia, Handel; "The Last Sleep of the Virgin," Massenet; Largo from "New World Symphony," Dvorak; In Memoriam, "Those Killed in the War,",Chipp; Offertoire on Two Christmas Themes, Guilmant ; hymn 595. All these pieces were carefully selected and most efficiently played; the registration of the stops being admirably suited both to the build- ing and the showing off of the beautiful reed stops. The Open Diapason on the Great Organ is a beautiful stop, and not too loud for the church; it came out particularly fine in Bach's Fugue. Its floating qualities were highly 'spoken of by many local organists who were present, To our mind the 'gems of the evening were Bairstow's Evening Song and Bach's Toccata and Fugue in D Minor, although the whole pro- gramme gave us the greatest pleasure. There is a keen desire that Mr. Darby may, give another recital during the present season. At present the organ is blown by hand, but part of Wednesday's collection, we believe, is to be set aside to pay for an electrical blowing in- stallation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>SPICES.</head>
        <p>SPICES.</p>
        <p>Seasonable Adult School Lecturette ORIGIN OF NICE THINGS.</p>
        <p>A lecturette appropriate to the festive season was given at the Stockton Heath Adult School, Mr. T. Brown taking for his subject "Spices." The . romantic history of spices was briefly touched upon, and it was pointed out that the development of the Cape. of Good Hope and the East Indies was largely due to the natural suit- ability for the production of condiments. The most suitable climate for the purpose is of a tropical or semi-tropical character, and very few spices are produced in temperate zones. Spices were found to be utilised by the natives of the tropical areas as a means of improving the taste of their foods.</p>
        <p>_ Vanilla is the fruit or seed pod of a climbing; orchid, grown in Centrel America, and was in- troduced into Europe by Spaniards. It is largely used in chocolate manufacture: Vanilla is now also produced artificially with equal success.</p>
        <p>Nutmeg and mace are the seed and covering reqpectively of a bushy tree grown in Malay. The tree is of the unsexual variety, the flowers being either male or female. Cloves constitute the flower buds .of a tree growing to upwards of 20ft. high. Its name is derived from the French,-meaning a nail, owing to its similarity in form. The tree is provided with oil glands, which evolve a pungent aroma. Cloves were used by the Chinese in 266 B.C. as a spice. They are now obtained from Penang. Ginger is an underground stem, now largely grown in Jamaica. This plant rapidly absorbs the nutriment from the soil, and instead of manuring the ground, new tracts are devoted to its growth, and the country is being gradually desolated. _ _ _</p>
        <p>A large number of other spices were briefly noticed, and samples were shown. including dill fruit, coriander, cummin, &amp;c. Numerous ques- tions were addressed to the speaker and satis- factory explanations were forthcoming. The Bible lesson was opened by Mr. 11. Parker upon the subject of the jdeal fellowship of Paul. In the ensuing discussion several aspects of the question were brought o&amp; by the members.</p>
        <p>Scarlet Fever.</p>
        <p>On December '26th 91 patients were under treatment for scarlet fever in the Borough Hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>POLITICS OF 19111.</head>
        <p>THE POLITICS OF 19111.</p>
        <p>A Common Danger That Made a Divided</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Nation One.</head>
        <p>Nation One.</p>
        <p>RETROSPECT AND PROSPECT.</p>
        <p>Just as, for generations to come, 1914 will be remembered as the year in which the great European War broke out, so any review of the year's events divides itself into two most sharply defined periods—before and after the declaration of hostilities. If there had been no inter- national crisis, 1914 would have been still a very notable year. The fact that in it the term had been completed which was necessary, under the Parliament Act, for the measures of Home Rule and Welsh Disestablishment to be placed auto- matically on the Statute Book, would alone have given to the past year no slight political significance. If it had not been for the suspen- sion of normal Parliamentary work, the foun- aation might have been laid in a Revenue Bill— technical reasons prevented this from being done in the proposed Part IV. of the Finance Bill— for a most important reform in local finance. A measure of educational reform, °Alined in the previous year by Mr. J. A. Pease, could not even be introduced, but there would not have been time available for this, even if no war had intervened. ULSTER "INCIDENTS."</p>
        <p>Right up to the very eve of the war the Irish question occupied the public mind in this country, with keen controversies and exciting incidents. In March, General Paget, command- ing the forces in Ireland, informed the War Office by telegram that Brigadier-General Gough and fifty-seven officers of the Third Cavalry Brigade, "preferred to accept dismissal if ordered North." Information had been re- ceived that four military depots in Ulster were insufficiently guarded, and it was deemed advis- able to take special precautions for safeguard- ing them. General Paget had called a meeting of divisional generals and brigadier-generals, because for "rtain reasons "he felt that the time had some when he must ascertain upon what general officers and others he might rely." General Paget's remarks were misunderstood, and General Gough sought assurances from the War Office that those under his command would not be used "in the event of the Home Rule Bill becoming law . . . . to en: force it on Ulster." In giving_such assurances Colonel Seely failed to realise that their effect, taken in conjunction with the questions in General Gough's letter, would be a bargain be- tween the State and an officer as to the officer's duty in certain circumstances. Consequently, Colonel Seely retired from the War Office. The crisis was closed by Mr. Asquith's assumption of the office of Secretary of State for War, and the issue of a "Special Order" which laid down (1) that no officer or soldier should be questioned in future by his commander as to his attitude under hypothetical circumstances; (2) that an officer or soldier should not be al- lowed to ask for assurances as to order F which he might be required to obey; (3) that it is the duty of every officer and soldier to obey all law- ful commands given through the proper chan- nels.</p>
        <p>Shortly after the trouble at the Curragh, the Ulster Volunteers effected a gun-running coup at Larne, in County Antrim, and at two points on the coast of County Down. The coup was carried out by means which involved the coer- cion of Government servants and "a grave awl unprecedented outrage" im the authority of the law. Mr. Bonar Law confessed in the House of Commons that "it was, of course, illegal." Sir Edward Carson declared that he took full re- sponsibility.</p>
        <p>tempt to deny the moral wrong which Germany was committing. He used nothing but the !tyrant's plea of necessity. Germany mat "hack her -way through." The utter unscrupulousness with which Germany addressed herself to this task—the ruthless war waged upon non-com- batants, with no regard to age or sex; the wanton destruction of seats of learning axici shrines of religion; the atrocities deliberately organised to create terror—soon revolted the moral consciousness of the whole world. DELIBERATELY PLANNED. Time has brought to light more ample proofs of the fixed and deliberate purposefulness with which Germany planned, prepared, and brought about the welter of bloodshed into which Europe has been cast. In a manifesto by which she hoped to enlist the sympathy of neutral; nations, she actually proclaimed that she knew that the fateful step upon which Austria-Hun- gary ventured with her encouragement was calculated to provoke a- European War, and that she joyfully associated herself in it with her ally. In the French Yellow Book there is recorded a remarkable conversation between the Kaiser and Count von Moltke, Chief of the Imperial Staff, on the one side, and the King of the Belgians on the other. In this conversa- tion, a year before war broke out, the Kaiser announced that war with France was inevit- able. "Your Majesty," Count von Moltke said to King Albert, "has no idea of the irresistible enthusiasm which will sweep over Germany on that day." We know, too, now, that Austria contemplated a year ago firing the train of international conflagration, and was warned by her ally, Italy, that she would have no part or lot in the matter. At Cardiff, Mr. Asquith dis- closed the extraordinary proposals which Ger- many had made to this country in 1912, presum- ing upon our earnest desire for good relations. Mr. Asquith told us: • "They asked us to pledge ourselves abso- lutely to neutrality in the event of Germany being engaged in war—and this, mind you, at a time when Germany was enormously in- creasing both her aggressive and defensive resources, and especially upon the sea. They asked us for a free hand, so far as we were concerned, if and when they selected the opportunity to overbear, to dominate the European world. To such a demand but one answer was possible, and that was the answer we gave." PROGRESS "STEP BY STEP."</p>
        <p>No attempt can be made here to sketch the progress of the Great War, or describe the un- paralleled feats of our soldiers in the field. Step by step the German armies are being forced back upon their own frontiers. Her plan of flinging enormous masses against the advancing enemy—now on the Western, now on the Eastern side—has never achieved any decisive success, and is only hastening the inevitable moment of exhaustion and collapse. The Ger- man, mercantile fleet, of which she was so in- ordinately proud, is shut out from the seas. In the Bight of Heligoland and off the Falkland Isles she has had an earnest of the crushing blow which her main Fleet must suffer if, and when, it ventures out of the Kiel Canal. In the air our air-fleet has proved itself superior in both daring and skill, though we do not aim our attacks at undefended towns and women and children. THE INTERNAL EFFECTS OF THE WAR. To a civilised nation at war, the organisation of finance, industry, and commerce is not less a condition of success than armaments on land and sea. It would be too long even barely to enumerate the steps whieh the Government have taken for minimising the disturbance caused by the War to the normal life of the community. It will be sufficient to Thention the provisions made for grappling with the diffi- culties of currency and credit, the enormous War Loan which was over-subscribed, the as- sumption of eighty per cent. on war-risks upon ships and cargo, and the huge purchase of sugar which, allows of its sale at moderate prices for Purposes both of food and industry. Some hard- ships to the community are inevitable, and a dislocation and shrinkage of trade was bound to be caused by a War which affects directly the larger part of the civilised world. But no, one could have anticipated, in the wildest moods of optimism, that after five months of such a War, Great Britain would be showing such slight traces of the strain.</p>
        <p>WHEN THE SWORD WILL BE SHEATHED.</p>
        <p>As we look into the future we can 'read with no uncertainty the spirit and purpose which will lead the Allies to the only possible goal. Retrospect and prospect cannot better be summed up than in two notable utterances, one by the French Premier in the Chamber of Deputies on Deceitnber 23rd, the other by our own Prime Minister at the Guildhall on Nov- ember 9th. M. Viviani said: "France will not lay down her arms until she has avenged outraged right, regained for ever the provinces torn from her by force, restored to outraged Belgium the fulness of her material prosperity and her political independence, and broken Prussian militar- ism, so that on the basis of justice she may rebuild a. regenerated Europe." •</p>
        <p>Mr. Asquith said: "Groat Britain will never sheathe the sword until Belgium receives in full measure all and more than all that she has sacrificed, until France is adequately secured against the menace of aggression, until the rights of the smaller nationalities of Europe are placed upon an unassailable foundation, and until the military domination of Prussia is wholly and finally destroyed." It may be a longer or a shorter time before this consummation is attained, but we can admit no doubt of the fihal attainment of that for which the British Empire is prepared to spend its utmost resources to the last man and the last penny.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>DUBLIN GUN-RUNNING.</head>
        <p>DUBLIN GUN-RUNNING.</p>
        <p>As a reply to the embodiment of Sir Edward Carson's Volunteers, a large force of National Volunteers was enrolled. The Ulster gun-run- ning corps was reproduced by a body of Nationalist Volunteers. They were stopped by a force of police and two companies of Regu- lars; an attempt to take the rifles led to a scuffle, and the soldiers, on their return to Dublin, after being pelted with bottles by an angry crowd, fired with fatal effect. A Royal Commission reported that the employment of the police and military was not in accordance with law; that the responsibility rested with the Assistant Commissioner, though General Cuthbert's compliance was wrong, and that there was no case for military intervention. It is not improbable that incidents such as those briefly recorded above, together with much of the language employed in the Ulster agita- tion, persuaded the German Government that 11 Great Britain was too gravely embarrassed by internal dissensions to be able to offer any check Ito German aggression on the Continent. But the German Government was soon to learn that, however Britons May quarrel among themselves, they are immediately of one mind in repelling the threats of a foreign foe. SEEKING A BASIS OF AGREEMENT. Though the Government have always regarded the Home Rule Bill "as an attempt, at once sincere and considerate, to base on a solid foun- dation the fabric of Irish self-government," they were anxious, if it were possible without any sacrifice of principle, to conciliate Ulster pre- judices. They proposed to make this endeavour by the offer of provisional exclusion, for those counties of Ulster which might elect for it by a prOcess of local option, for a period of six years. The Lords amended the Government Amend- ment in so drastic a fashion that the House of Commons could only refuse to consider the mutilations and excrescences which they had inflicted on the Bill. His Majesty then at- tempted to provide a way out of the deadlock by calling a Conference in which representatives of the Government, Opposition, Nationalists and Ulstermen might seek for a basis of agree- ment. The failure of the Conference was an- nounced in laconic terms by the Speaker, who presided over its four meetings: "The possibility of defining an area to be excluded from the operation of the Govern- ment of Ireland was considered. "The Cgnference being unable to agree, either in principle or in detail, upon such an area, brought its meetings to a conclusion."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>"WE ARE ONE NATION."</head>
        <p>"WE ARE ONE NATION."</p>
        <p>After the European War had brought about a truce in domestic controversies, the Govern- ment placed a short Act on the Statute Book to suspend the operation of the Government of Ireland and the Welsh Church Acts for a period of twelve months from their passing, or, if at the end of twelve months the War has not ended, until such later date (not later than the end of' the War)xas may be fixed by Order in Council. The Parliamentary correspondent of the "Observer," a prominent Unionist organ, hadopreviously suggested that such a measure would be a fair and proper solution. The Sus- pensory Ack became law on the same day (September 19th) on which the Royal Assent was given to the two "Parliament Act" Bills which it suspends—the Home Rule and Welsh Disestablishinent Bills. A remarkable scene closed this remarkable session. As the Official Report records, "all the Members present joined in singing the National Anthem." When a Mem- ber exclaimed, "God save Ireland !" Mr. John Redmond, the leader of the Irish party, replied: "And God save England, too !" The comment which Mr. F. E. Smith made upon this in Tory Liverpool, three days later, ought not to be forgotten. He said: "We are one nation, and I care nothing for the ripple on the waters. A week ago we relegated it, if it should ever be raised again, to a time when the War is over. When the cry was raised in the House, 'God save Ire- land,' Mr. Redmond shouted, God save Eng- land.' It will take a great deal to make some of us forget that." • THE COMPACT AND THE PRICE.</p>
        <p>In an historic statement made by Sir Edward Grey on August 3rd, the country was first ap- prised of the forces and events which had brought us to the brink of a European War. The violation of Belgian neutrality by Germany left us no choice but to take up arms against the aggressor or forfeit all self-respect. The German Chancellor expressed his astonishment that we should go to war "just for a word— 'neutrality,' a word which in war-time had so often been disregarded—just for a scrap of paper." When our Ambassadoik declared that our solemn compact simply had to be kept, or what confidence could any one have in engage- ments given by Great Britain in the future?— the threatening reply was: "But at what price will that compact have been kept? Has the British Government thought of that?" In the Reichstag the Imperial Chancellor made no at-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>WAR STORIES.</head>
        <p>WAR STORIES.</p>
        <p>" NOTHING TO GRUMBLE AT" Writes a Cheery_ " Tommy " To His</p>
        <p>Vicar.</p>
        <p>A letter written to the Vicar of Orfora by a driver in the sth Divisional Train (2nd Army</p>
        <p>Corps), says : " I am proud to say that our troals are still forging ahead, and will do so until they have crushed that cruel enemy the Germans. Every- body was deeply impressed by the visit of our King to the front. It seemed to put new life in us. The weather has been awful lately. It has done nothing but blow and rain, but we are getting good warm clothes, together with a lined overcoat and mackintosh, so that you will see we have not got anything to grumble at. Tobacco and cigarettes are sent to us from Eng- land, whilst each of us has received a beautiful woollen belt from Queen Mary, with a nice note inside it. So you see everybody at home are doing their very best t make us happy."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>TAKE THEIR RIFLES TO CHURCH. " Tommy " Has To Worship With One Eye Open.</head>
        <p>TAKE THEIR RIFLES TO CHURCH. " Tommy " Has To Worship With One Eye Open.</p>
        <p>Writing to a friend at Orford, Private J. Swinton, of the Army Service Corps, says "As you will have noticed already from the papers, the tide has turned. We are the ag- gressors now, and things are looking very bright. Let your Christmas be a happy one, and in the midst of your pleasure, just think of us out here. This .is my first Christmas away from 'home, sweet home,' but although I would like to have spent this One in Orford, I somehew feel that this is going to be a very happy one for the Allies. "We have had services this last three Sun- day nights in a little room, with a tiny har- monium to supply the music. Being in the danger zone, we have to take our rifles, bayonets and ammunition with us. Often when the chaplain is speaking the big guns are firing not far away, and the words are inaudible. The services are thoroughly enjoyed, and are. looked forward to with pleaeure."-</p>
      </div>
