      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>ttitas to the Oita</head>
        <p>ttitas to the Oita</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. THE Enrroz of "The Examiner" invites corre- spondence on all subjects of public interest. LETTERS should be brief and pointed. Their object must be to impart or seek information, not abuse.</p>
        <p>LETTERS must in all cases be accompanied by the name and address of the writer. COMMUNICATIONS received early in the week— except urgent matters—can always be given the best attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>OUR" TOWER OF BABEL."</head>
        <p>OUR" TOWER OF BABEL."</p>
        <p>Sir,—l remember the late Alderman Forshaw, when addressing a local audience, relating a story about a gentleman who was approached by a deputation of his party to allow himself to be nominated for a seat in the Town Council. lie made the usual reply that" he would con, sider the matter," and afterwards consulted his wife. 1-I(tr comment was: "Well, John, for the last 20 years or so thou bast borne a first-class character, but if thou joins the Town Council thy character will soon be lost; so, for good- ness sake, don't."</p>
        <p>I am reminded of this little story by the extraordinary peculiarities indulged in by some of the members of our local Council, and which has become quite a marked and common feature of their so-called debates. There are excellent gentlemen among them, but others have become so notorious that it is related that some people, when discussing which place they should go to for an evening's entertainment, whether it should be the Hippodrome or the Town Council, decide on the Town Council. Such is fame! That some of the members of the Council really be- lieve they afford the pul?lic entertainment is ap- parent from the fact that the performances are repc‘atul at their every monthly appearance. They never become "stale." Some cynics even say, "They are consumed by the exuberance of their own verbosity." It is difficult to decide which of their recent performances was the best, but some said that at the last one "they excelled themselves"; others said "it was the limit." They even surprised their chief ad- mirers. Others (but, of course, there are the' doubting Thomases) said they wondered whether some of the members really had any sinpre regard for the welfare of the town— because the town never seemed to be mentioned —only " persons " were. According to others, our Parliament at West- minster quite takes a seCondasy place to our local Council, but the former has the habit— wisely or otherwise—after some so-called strenuous legislation, of taking a "rest." Our Council never even seem to dream of doing so. I suppose they think it would be infra dig. However, with my usual "cheek" or "modesty," I don't mind suggesting that if they took a " holiday " or a "rest," if it is only for a short time, both they and the town might receive some benefit. It is just possible that the affairs of the town, too, would be equally as well managed—at least, for a time—if left in the hands of the Mayor and the officials. Most of the members of the Town Council would then become "free men" indeed! It is true that the town would lose its usual monthly cheap advertisement, but I sincerely think that many of the citizens are even pre- pared for this great sacrifice. In the meantime, I wish all of them a happy New Year. and above all peace within their borders. The war without is quite sufficient at present. '</p>
        <p>E. ENGLAND.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>BEAUTY AT MIDDLE AGE.</head>
        <p>BEAUTY AT MIDDLE AGE.</p>
        <p>The Girl of To-day Mdy Find Encoorafjetnent</p>
        <p>In It.</p>
        <p>Some of the most famous beauties of history made their conquests when they were ap- proaching the half-century of life. Cleopatra was 45 when she brought Mark Antony to her feet; Helen of Troy was almost as old when men fought and died for her sake; and nearly all the women who have swayed the destinies of nations and mastered rulers were in middle age. Youth is denied such conquests. The rule holds good to-day and the yefirs between 40 and 50. are more often crowned with the greatest successes in the life of a society woman than those below them. Take a typical, well-pre- served, well-educated woman of the present day at 40, and you will find she has a heart and complexion as fresh as if she were ten years, younger. She has a keener zest for life than many a woman only half her age, for she has learned from experience not to expect too much from human nature; so she is seldom disap- pointed. Well aware how to make the best of her own, appearance, perfectly groomed, ex- quisitely polished in style and manner, she is a thing of beauty in a very real sense of the word, and she knows her power.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>THIS WOMAN'S TROUBLES GONE</head>
        <p>THIS WOMAN'S TROUBLES GONE</p>
        <p>Terrible Cramps, Dizziness, Nervousness, Misery—Her Story of How She Got Well Again.</p>
        <p>"Your remedies have relieved me of all my troubles. I would have such bearing down misery and cramps and such weak, nervous, dizzy spells that I would have to go to bed. Some days I could hardly stay up long enough to prepare my food. "The doctor's medicine did me no good so I changed to Lydia E. Pinkham's Vege:. table Compound and got good results from the first bottle. I kept on taking it and used the Sanative Wash with it, until I was well again, I think every woman who suffers as I have, could take no better medicine." (Name and address on request.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Testimony of a Nurse.</head>
        <p>Testimony of a Nurse.</p>
        <p>"I am a nurse and when I do much lift- ing I have a female weakness, but I take Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound and I cannot say nough in praise of it. I always recom- mend it for female troubles." (Name and address on request.)</p>
        <p>The makers of Lydia E. Phikham's Vegetable Compound have thousands of such letters as those above—they tell the truth, else they could not have been obtained. This medicine is no stranger— it has stood, the test for years.</p>
        <p>Write to-day for a lee private (to women only) to the Lady Manager, .the Lydia E. Pinkham Medicine Co., 42a Hampstead Road, London, N.W. For 2s. 9d. she will send you a bottle of the Lydia E. Pinkham Vegetable Compound, or for 4s. 6d. a much larger bottle. Any frbe.MiSt will s'4)oy it at these prices.</p>
        <p>r( V ' • • • en 'it.r.;) • / 5</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Bell-M usic.</p>
        <p>As I write, the old year lies a-dying (as Tennyson phrases it), and by the time this meets the reader's eye, the bells will have tolled "sad and slow" to mark his passing, and will have welcomed his successor with a merry touch—not "peal," an it please you, for of course a full peal consists of some thousands -of changes. Ever since, between 30 and 40 years ago, I made the acquaintance of the then oldest living ringer and composer of bell-music, I have been interested in these campanological tech- nicalities Grandsire Quators, Grandsii.e Cinques, Oxford Court Bob, Norwich Court Bob, Ebor Major, bits of Stedman, and so forth. Well, the bells have once more "rung out the old, rung in the new," and one's heart echoes their cheery message. As an old comrade of mine years ago observed, these' belfry-preachers with the iron tongues which speak all languages have an infinite capacity to make their mes- sage intelligible to the most debased, despair- ing, and ignorant. They reiterate °Charles Lamb's essay on New Year resolutions; they preach the gospel of peace, goodwill, charity, of atonement for wrongs done, and steadfast en- deavour to do better in the future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>" Waits."</head>
        <p>The " Waits."</p>
        <p>Such carol singing as we have had this season suggests a note respecting this ancient insti- tution. Writing on the subject quite 100 years ago, Hone tells how "preparatory to Christmas the bellman of every parish in London rang his bell at dead midnight, that his 'worthy masters and mistresses' may listen, and be assured by his vocal intonation that he is read- ing ai copy of verses in praise of their several virtues, especially their liberality, and when the festival is over he calls with his bill and hopes to be 'remembered.' " The original waits, however, were really watchmen—min- strels attached to the Court and to the City Corporation, who nightly patrolled the .streets, sounding the watch as they went along. The Puritans, who made an attempt to abolish Christmas .festivities, likewise tried to put an end to carol-singing, but at the Restoration the old celebrations were resumed. Many quaint, fantastic carols and pretty cantides of the olden time have apparently been forgotten, and per- haps it were too much to expect a revival. Two or three little girls sang one of them at my door the other night, and it was a delightful surprise. The modern hymns which nowadays the waits generally prefer are but indifferent substitutes for some of ,the appropriate old carols which they have unfortunately sup- planted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>"Got What He Wan Led."</head>
        <p>"Got What He Wan Led."</p>
        <p>The story under this heading in the "Children's Corner" last week reminded me of a similar one which I have been in the habit of telling from personal knowledge of the cir- cumstances. In the far-off bygones I knew a Quaker of the old school who on Sunday nights enticed young fellows, from the streets to his hospitable hearth, and often before dismissing them servd a tasty bit of supper. Of course, with hith " Yea " was always "Yea," and "Nay," "Nay." On one memorable occasion the host turned to a youth who appeared for the first time as one his guests, and said, "Friend Hirst, wilt thou partake of a little supper?" "No, thank you, Mr. Hanson," said the raw one with mistaken politeness, supposing that he would be further pressed. "Just as thou pleasest," pursued the Quaker; "sit over here, then, till I see how many of my friends will come to table with us." Needless to add, the remainder filed up to the evening meal, leav- ing the "odd man out" to kick himself for being so silly— and to learn the intended lesson. Since writing the foregoing I have heard that the fine old Quaker-93 he was—has just died in the Lake district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>About Proverbs.</head>
        <p>About Proverbs.</p>
        <p>The tit-bit entitled "The Dog and the Pro- verb" set me rummaging among a collection which I began making a great many years ago, and which is fairly representative of Far East as well as West, carrying one back over the ages. Lord John aussell defined the .proverb as "The wisdom of many, but the wit of one" —drawing upon "the well of English undefiled," and using the word "wit" as Bacon and his contemporaries used it, and as it is now still used by old folk in remote Lancashire villages. But to our proverbs. Some frankly prefer this homespun philosophy to muck of the jargon of the schools. "Old as. the hills" almost are some of the most familiar of these wise saws. In a book by John Heywood, compiled 400 years ago, one finds: ' A haire of the dog that bit us," "A man may take a horse to the water, but he cannot make him drink," "A penny for your thoughts," "A rolling stone never gathers moss," "Allis not gol4 that glitters," "As like chalk to cheese," "Beggars should not be choosers," "Better late than never," "Rob Peter to pay Paul," and many more such old friends. One, "It's never too late to mend," which served Charles Reade as the title of his most popular and enduring novel, is quoted in an ancient document as a proverb current in 1433, so it must be even older. An old friend of mine, who has never had to write for his living, but has always been fond of scribbling for the weeklies in his locality, remarks: Some pro- verbs are perfect in themselves, complete nuggets of concentrated wisdom, such as those of Solo- mon, or whoever wrote them as we know them, in The Book. On the other hand, there are proverbs, like the puns of Charles Lamb and Tom Hood, which lose their flavour of unctuous and ludicrous yet delightfully apt force when dissociated from their textual environments, and even the individuality of their utterer and the "motif." Sancho -Panza "bubbled over with proverbs," and nearly drove the poor knight of La Mancha demented with that damnable iteration which so irritated Jack Falstaff at Prince Hal's rebuke in a proverb. Without going so fat as to say that every pro- verb has its opposite, I have noticed that several flatly contradict each other. For ex- ample, "In the multitude of counsellors there is safety," and "Too many cooks spoil the broth," strike me as irreconcilable. Another commentator some time ago expressed it more strongly. Said he: " ' Out of sight, out of mind,' may be true. If it is, 'Absence makes the heart grow fonder' is a lie. 'Marry iü haste and repent at leisure' would be all right hut for Happy is the wooing that's not long a- doing.' They say 'A rolling stone gathers no moss,' and in the next breath they add that 'A sitting hen gathers: no feathers.' .‘ There is honour among thieves.' Well and good. But 'Set a thief to catch a thief.' Reconcile those two proverbs if you can." After all, as I recol- lect Archbishop Trench putting the chief point at issue, one has to 'place to the credit of p.ro- verbs the fact that they please the people, and have pleased them for ages; that they possess so vigorous a principle of life as to have main- tained 'their ground, ever new and ever young, through all the centuries of a nation's exist- ence—nay, that many of them have pleased not one nation only, but many, so that they have made themselves a home in the most different lands; that they have not a few of them, come down to us from remotest antiquity; and fur- ther, that some of the greatest poets, the pro- foundest. philosophers, the most learned scholars, the most genial writers in every kind. have delighted in them. have made large and frequent use of them, and have bestowed infinite labour on the gathering and elucidating of them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Some Use.</head>
        <p>Some Use.</p>
        <p>Summer Visitor: "I suppose you don't mind my being in your field, Mr. Hodge?" Mr. Hodge &amp;heartily): "The longer you stay, the better, ma'am; fact is, the birds are that troublesome, I ought to have put a scarecrow in this field before</p>
      </div>
