      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>1911 THE YEAR'S EVENTS.</head>
        <p>1911 THE YEAR'S EVENTS.</p>
        <p>Described by a Well-known Publicist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>SHADOW OF A GREAT WAR.</head>
        <p>SHADOW OF A GREAT WAR.</p>
        <p>The shadow of the great. ;:dr is over 1914. True, the first seven M-vnths .of the year had nearly completed their eourse before the first war clouds darkened the horizon. But there is a quality of cimest incredible remoteness about everything that, eccurred before last August. Those ear Her months were not un- eventful. A great sea disaster, recalling the loss of the Manic, had shocked the nation. Our polics took on an intensity whlch per- rivaded us that we were inahng history at an unprecedented rate. Before the end of July it was clear that 194 would contribute no uil.lm,r.re ta the story Of th 2 kassing, years Theni the war. In a few days all the excite rnents of the preceding months had gone pre maturely to the oblivion which awaits au things huMan. In normal times the early months of 1914 would have seemed a period of unusual storm and stress. It is an inte- resting commentary on the relative character of all our judgmeats that we view them now as the calm before the storm. THE SARAJEVO CRIME.</p>
        <p>Where are the beginnings of the war? Opinions ,would probably differ. In external events we may select the assassination at Sarajevo on Sunday, June 28th—a choice as arbitrary as any must be which attempts to arrest an infinite chain of cause and effect. On that day the Archduke Francis Ferdi- nand, heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne, was assassinated with his wife. Austria, seeking at the instigation of Germany a casus belli, read a political significance into the crime. A plainly impossible ultimatum was issued by Austria to Serbia. The ex- treme self-humiliation offered by the Serbs was not sufficient. War was intended, and on July 28th Austria and Serbia were at war. The situation was at once most grave. With Austria in the Triple Alliance were Germany and Italy. Russia had an interest in Serbia, and she was connected by the somewhat indefinite ties ol the "Entente" with England and France. There were the possibilities of the long-feared war between the Alliance and the Entente. The armies and the fleets of Europe were mobilising. Russia came to the defence of Serbia, and then Germany felt, according to the Kaiser, that the sword had been forced into her hand. How were England and France affected? Pacifists pleaded for non-intervention. Italy, on August 2nd, declared'. herself neutral, , and it was definitely stated that we had no treaty obligations to go to the rescue of France, when Germany violated the neu- trality of the Duchy of Luxembourg and attacked her English diplomats were work- ing hard for peace. The country clearly wanted peace. Then Germany did the thing which made peace impossible. She invaded Belgium. The perpetual neutrality of Bel- gium was guaranteed by a treaty to which the signatories included England and Germany. The German expectation was that England's political preoccupations would prevent her interference, and that Belgium, being weak, would offer no resistance. • They were both natural conclusions to the German mind. And they were both wrong. There was no longer a Peace Party in England. The nation rallied as one man to keep its word to Belgium. Eng- land, France, Russia, Belgium, Germany, Austria, and Serbia were now in the war.</p>
        <p>submarine has so far played the most CCM. spicuous part in this department of the war. But here again the German plan has failed. That plan was the gradual attrition of our Navy, so that when the issue is joined in .earnest the conditions might be more nearly equal. In the result we have a superiority in every department greater than that with which we started. This, year will see fifteen Dreadnoughts added to our fleet, and Ger- many's addition cannot exceed three. We can, according to the ,First Lord of the Ad- miralty, afford to lose a super-Dreadnought a month , for twelve months; an extravagance we are not likely to indulge in. Germany's principal achievements on the water were associated With the raids on commerce by the notorious Emden before she fell a victim to the °Sydney—a striking contribution by Aus- Lrp,lia to the Empire's service. Since then the British squadron, .under Vice-Admiral Sir o'rederick Sturdee, has done • further damage tmong the German raiders, sinking the three -misers to whose Ictivity Britain owed the of the Good Hope and the Monmouth. 11 among the 'great events of the war must placed the splendid feat of the B 11, which</p>
        <p>under five rows of mines to sink a /urkish vessel.. There was a change in the •_ _ _</p>
        <p>aval service when Prince Louis o'jf Batten- herg, having rendered great . services, re- :-:gned, from motives which were readily -.ppreciated, from the position of First Sea giving place to Lord Fisher.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>GERMAN PLANS.</head>
        <p>THE GERMAN PLANS.</p>
        <p>Much that was obscure at that time has since become dear in relation to .the German plans. France was first to be beaten, and then the Kaiser's soldiers would hasten eastward and settle accounts with Russia: England was not in the picture. The settle- ment with her belonged to a future occa- sion. It is clear that to such a plan "rapidity of action" was essential. France had to be beaten quickly. That was pre- vented by the resishrice of Belgium. And England came in because Christian morals are not dead in Vairope, .and the Prussian view of treaties is ,not, generally held. Once in the war our concern was with the way to -face it. Our Fleet was admirably placed at the time, and, thanks to the efficiency of the War Department in the time preceding the war, we were able to despatch an Expeditionary Force to France with a re- markable smoothness of working. At once the; Empire called on its sons, and. they re- sponded. Territorials, enrolled for Home Service, offered to the number of over 100,000 to go wherever they were needed. Lord Kitchener, now at'the War Office, set to work to raise a new army, to go into training at once. Sir John French, Inspector- General of the Forces, was in charge of our Force on the Continent, working with the French under General Joffre, and the Belgian Army. Sir John Jellicoe was in supreme command of our Fleet. To the two Sir .Tohns the • Empire looked with confidence. Mean while, India, Canada, Australia, and New Zealand made their presents of soldiers and of treasure. South Africa set to work to settle accounts with Germany in that part. of the world. The essential fact of the situation was that, while the enemy's full strength was in the field at the beginning, ours was an in- creasing strength. Time was on our side.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>WAR IN BELGIUM.</head>
        <p>THE WAR IN BELGIUM.</p>
        <p>The war developed first. on land, and the story of the subjugation of Belgium is a tale of glory and horror. Beginning ,with the heroic resistance of Liege, it has been a long series of gallant deeds by the outraged little nation, and of barbarism by the Germans the full extent of which has yet to be ascertained. Rheims and Louvain will always be associated with Europe's realisation of .the fall horror of "Deutsche Kultur." Brussels was surrendered for strategic reasons. The fall of Antwerp was accompanied by no infamy beyond the initial infamy of the Germans being in Belgium at all. From that time onward the story is one c,F terrific attacks by the Germans, repulsed with enormous loss of life. There were times when the siege. of Paris seemed imminent. "To Calais," too, was the proud cry of the Germans. The actual situation was summed up by Lord Kitchener in his declaration that. the proud boast. of the Germans that they would reach Calais was as valuable as their declaration that They were marching on Paris. The growing army as holding its own at the time of maximum difficulty. Enthusiasm was raised to the highest point when King George visited his troops, and met President Poin- care and King Albert in the field. The Prince of Wales • was already in the fighting line. Prince Albert was serving in the Navy. Never has the Throne, the centre of our national unity, stood higher in the estimation of 'wile British people.</p>
        <p>THE EAST_ERN THEATRE.</p>
        <p>There was no question now of returning In triumph to the East. But men were needed in the East. The Russian troops had revealed an astounding, because wholly unexpected, rapidity .of movement. Austria's "ramshackle old Empire," to quote the picturesque expres- sion of our Chancellor of the Exchequer, was being pulled about her ears. Troops were shifted from one frontier to the other by the harassed Germans. The Kaiser himself was now here and now there, always in a 'safe position himself, but always apparently bring- ing ill-fortune to his troops. The soldiers led by the Grand Duke Nicholas were providing the surprise of the war. Germany, who had filled England and Holland with pathetic bodies of refugees from Belgium, witnessed the spectacle of refugees from the East pour- ing into Berlin. The traditional fear of "Der barbarische Sklave " took violent hold on her. There was no more talk of mailed fist and world- conquest. Germany became the in jured innocent, defending her .hearth and home. The almost incredible mendacity of the Berlin Intelligence Bureau had left thy populace ill-prepared for this state of affairs. Then a great Serbian victory surprised the world. Belgrade, which had fallen to the Austrian arms, was retaken, and it was an- nounced that not a single Austrian was left on Serbian soil. It was an astounding feat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>WAR AT SEA.</head>
        <p>THE WAR AT SEA.</p>
        <p>The events of war have justified British con- fidence in the Navy. '.1.0 its unremitting vign we owe the fact that the war has made SC little difference to life in this country. There Lave been naval losses on both sides. no</p>
        <p>early hours of Wednesday 'morning, Decem- ber.l6th, when fast. Grernian vessels shelled peaceful towns and escaped in the fog. There was a casualty list of over 400 killed and wounded, the roll of the dead steadily increas- ing as the seriously injured succumbed' to their wounds. The Coroner's jury, which found that the slain were "killed in bora- 'lardment," expressed the opinion that "wil- ful murder" would be a more fitting descrip- tion. "An outrage to civilised opinion,' was an American Press view. Berlin hailed the occurrence as "a regular bombardment of fortified towns"! The military result was !limited to its effect as a recruiting agent in this country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>ENGLISH COAST RAIDED.</head>
        <p>ENGLISH COAST RAIDED.</p>
        <p>Still fresh in the "public mind is the raid on fimrtlepool. Scarboroutzh. and Whitby in the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>1.. JA t. , . . --, rt, – , ...,, Ilk yr</head>
        <p>1.. JA t. , . . --, rt, - , ...,, Ilk yr</p>
        <p>A REBELLION.</p>
        <p>; One incident only seemed. to point in the direction of a readisaticm of the German ex- pectation that our Empire would disintegrate. There was a small rebellion in South Africa. It began with the treachery of General Beyers. It ended with the capture of De Wet, and the death of Bevers. The Colony proved Dverwhelmingly loyal. Botha was a strong man, and the whole incident revealed the folly of those who were capable of being cajoled. by the promises of Germany. The area of the great war has grown during its progress. Germany, shocked at the intro- duction of Asiatics to fight against a Euro- pean nation, called Turkey to her aid in the war for a higher civilisation. Portugal joined the Allies, and rumours of Italy's ultimate intervention on the same side became prevalent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>SITUATION AT HOME.</head>
        <p>THE SITUATION AT HOME.</p>
        <p>Our concern with the war had never been wholly centred in other lands. The abnormal situation at home had to be faced. The rally of the nation to face it makes one of the proudest chapters in. our island's story. Everybody wanted to help. In four months the Prince of Wales's Fund for the Relief of Distress had reached £4,000,000. Political bodies sank their differences and had a com- mon object: • The work of women in the national emergency will never be forgotten. The Government, too, acted promptly. There was no failure of British credit, but remit- tances from abroad were stopped. The stop- page of British industry for a single week would have been an walling calamity to all</p>
        <p>man tne cturation of tne war. our party wars were over for the time. The Suffragettes, who had stirred indignation to an unpre- cedented pitch by burning houses and de- stroying pictures, bee-Imo recruiting propa- gandists. England had only one party. At the outset of the war Lord Morley and Mr. John Burns lind resigned from th,e C4binety, and Mr. C. P. Trevelyan had re- linquished an Under-Secretaryship, but. there was no serious division of opinion on essentials. AN OCE'AN TRAGEDY. / The disaster to the Empress of Ireland would, in a normal year, have seemed incom- parably the most dramatic and terribleievent. Through the error of a Norwegian captain, the Canadian Pacific liner went to her doom in the St. Lawrence on May 30th, with a loss of 959 lives. Among the famous victims were Sir Henry Seton - Karr, and Mr. and Mrs. Laurence Irving. A little while before this aviation had claimed another life, -.Mr. Gustav Hamel, one of the foremost. of English air- men, being lost in .a flight across from France.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>ILLUSTRIOUS DEAD.</head>
        <p>THE ILLUSTRIOUS DEAD.</p>
        <p>Apart from the toll taken in war and dis- aster, Death has been busy in 1914. The Duke of Argyll and Lord Strathcona are to be included in the' roll. Mr. Joseph Cham- berlain passed away, amid the mourning of all parties in the State;' and the death t of Mr. Henniker-Heaton removed .a great postal reformer. The Baroness von Stittner ended on the eve of war a , life devoted to the advo, cacy of peace. Christendom mourned the death of the saintly Pope Pius X..; and Eng- lish political and religious life lost a strenu- ous worker in the Rev. C. Silvester Horne, M.P. Art has to deplore the removal of two honoured names from her books—those of Sir Hubert von Herkomer, Royal Academi- cian, and holder of many international hon- ours, and Sir J. Tenniel, of Punch. , But the nation was never more deeply touched than by the death of Earl Roberts. the happy warrior," within sound ot tne enemy's guns in France, where he had gone WI inspect the Indian troops. A great assembly, including King and artisan, azu, representatives of all the intermediate grades, attended his obsequies in St. Paul' Cathedral, and the solemn procession through the streets of London will not soon be forgotten.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
