      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>JANUARY 2, 1915.</head>
        <p>JANUARY 2, 1915.</p>
        <p>sections of the population. A moratorium was proclaimed, banknotes for .21 and 10s. were issued, theßoard of Trade fixed The re- tail pric?s at which certain articles of food should be sold, a committee was appointed to advise on measures necessary to deal with any distress that might arise—happily, so far, with the exception of the cotton trade, indus- trial distress has been much less than was anticipated—the Government insured British shipping against war risks, assumed control of the railways, and interned alien enemies. The soundness of British credit, was (lemon- strated by the fact that under the most. dis- advantageous conditions, with the Stock Ex- change closed, the largest loan in history— one of £3so,ooo,ooo—was actually over-sub- scribed. The nation voted increased separa- '.;ion allowances to the dependents of fighters, and cheerfully accepted higher Income-Tax and increased duties on beer and tea. , POLITICAL EXCITEMENTS.</p>
        <p>Ireland, the centre of many stormy con- troversies, had provided one of the most remarkable political crises in. reCent history in the months preceding the war. "Talk of civil war," said the King, in calling a con- Ference of statesmen at Buckingham Palace, " is on the tongues of the most thoughtful of my subjects." There certainly had been talk of civil war. There were gun-running inci- dents in Ireland. The Ulster resistance to the Home Rule Bill was represented by an armed volunteer movement. Military measures by the Government were indignantly declared by Unionists to be in the nature of a pogrom against Ulster. Liberals as indignantly de- clared that they were nothing of the kind. There were stormy sittings of. the Commons. Then the Nationalists formed" a volunteer corps, and Ireland was "an armed camp." Whether English troops would be asked to "fight against Ulster" provided a 'military crisis. Sir John French and Sir J. Ewart re- signed. Colonel Seely ceased to be War Minister, and the 'Prime Minister took the duties of that office upon his own shoulders. Following the failure of the Royal Confer- ence, things looked very black, but the crisis iisappeared 'suddenly before the superior pro- portions of the European War. Hone Rule passed on to the Statute Book under the terms of the Parliament Act, its operation suspended by special provision for a term of not less than twelve months and not more</p>
      </div>
