      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>1 – _ •. _ 'P."' DISTRICT ti ' .!.. x 5 H ,</head>
        <p>1 - _ •. _ 'P."' DISTRICT ti ' .!.. x 5 H ,</p>
        <p>SEE ALSO PAGE 8.</p>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>A Dangerous Wall.—The Roads Committee re- ported, at the monthly meeting of the Urban District Council on Tuesday, that they had passed a resolution requesting the Clerk to write to the Earl of Ellesmere calling attention to the dangerous condition of the wall of the Bridgewater coal wharf. •</p>
        <p>Watch Night Service.—The Lymm Free Churches herd a united watch night service— conducted by Mr. J. B. D. Smith (secretary .of the local Free Church Council)—on Thursday evening; in the Primitive Methodist School- room. There was a fairly good attendance, and the service proved very helpful to all. Miss F. Shore presided at the organ. Yesterday's Football.—Yesterday afternoon an enjoyable football match was witnessed on the Pepper-street ground between the Wednes- day and Saturday Clubs—captained by G. Cope- land and C. .Whitfield. We understand that the proceeds are to provide a social and supper for the team members, and that .tie winning team will this afternoon contest the Lymm Amateurs.</p>
        <p>" Tommy's " Holiday.—A right royal welcome has been accorded to the number of Lymra " Tommies" who arrived home on Tuesday night for a few days' respite from their work in the training camps: The village had been celebrating -Christmas and the New Year in a very quiet 'manner, and naturally things have been .considerably livened up by the visitors. who look extremely well,. and gmtly improved in physical fitness.'</p>
        <p>" Messiah " A Wesleyan Church.—%t the Lymm Wesleyan Church on Sunday; evening, the choir gave a most impressive rendition of selections from the "Messiah," under the able conductorship of Mr. J. 11. Brazendale. Mr. J. Waterworth officiated in the opening and clos- ing devotions. There was a good congregation; the'soloists being the Misses Ada Guest and Ada McHugh, and Messrs. N. Chanter and B. A. Robinson. All performed very creditably.</p>
        <p>Unfenced Bridge-Approach.—The Roads Com- mittee of the Lymm Urban District Council, at a meeting held on December 21st, resolved that a letter be written to the :Manchester Ship Canal Company calling their attention to the necessity of fixing a fence on the west side of the Ditehfield Bridge, and on the .north side of the adjoining road.—This fact was reported to the Council at their meeting on Tuesday even- ing, and the committee's action was approved. Successful Musical Students.—Pupils of Mr. William Cooke, organist of Lymm Parish Church, were successful in the recent examina- tions of Trinity College, London. Miss Con- stance Wood passed the. Intermediate examina- tion in pianoforte playing, and Miss Fanny Holt, daughter of Councillor J. 11. Holt, of `Kil- donan," Lymm, passed with hoaurs the Inter- mediate • examination in violin playing. Miss Holt was prepared for the examination by Mrs. William Cooke.</p>
        <p>Christmas Day United Service.—The Free Churches of Lymm held a well-attended united service on Christmas Day in the Independent Methodist Church. Mr. William Mort occupied the pulpit, preaching an eloquent and appro- priate sermon. Mr. J. B. D. Sniith (hon. secre- tary of the local Free Church Council) referred to the great loss the Free Churches of Lymm had sustained in the death Of Mr. D. IL Green- man, and expressed their sympathy with the widow and (laughter of the deceased gentleman.</p>
        <p>Present at the Council Meeting.—The ordin- ary monthly meeting of the Cuncil was held at the Council Offices on Tuesday evening—instead of on the Monday, as usual—and there were present Messrs. T. H. Warburton (in the chair), F. Lockyer (vice-chairman), J. IL Holt, W. G. Groves, C. E. Cowper, F. Ingham, S. Critchley, R. H. Gotobed and A. Mclntyre, with the Clerk and Collector (Mr. W. MuHard), the Assistant Clerk (Mr. W. Timperley), the Medical Officer (Dr. de Jmig), the Surveyor (Mr. P. Mort), the Gas Manager (Mr. T. IT. West), and the Water Manager (Mr. C. I. Barnes).</p>
        <p>Children on Canal Beats.—lt was reported to the Urban District Council, at their meeting on Tuesday evening' that during the year sixty- -lute canal boats had been inspected within the Council's area. Gr.rally speaking, the craft were very clean and in good repair, only four contraventions of the by iftws being met with, as against 21 the previous year. Councillor Gotobed said he noticed that there were 24 children reported to be living on the boats, and asked what was being done in the matter of their education.--He was informed that steps were taken to ensure that thg children at- tended school a certain number of times each year, but that the matter was one, for the Edu- cation Authority and not for that Council.</p>
        <p>Same to You, Gentlemen !—Before business was commenced at the monthly meeting of the Lymm Urban District Council on Tuesday 'even- ing, Mr. Groves wished the Chairman (Mr. T. IL Warburton), on behalf of rne members, the couirliments of the season and a happy New Gotobed joined in the expression, and remarked that even if at times differences of opinion did exist among them, it was all, for the good, as such differences brought out points that otherwise might be missed. However, on an occasion such as that it was a pleasure to have the opportunity of giving vent to one's friendly feelings. (Hear, hear.)—The Chairman thanked the members for their good wishes, and begged to be allowed to return the.oompliment. Occa- sionally they had differences of opinion, and if they did not, there would be no fun at all. (Hear, hear, and laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>NEARLY A TRAGEDY-</head>
        <p>NEARLY A TRAGEDY-</p>
        <p>But % rled 133 Stationmaster's</p>
        <p>Presence of Mind.</p>
        <p>By the presence of mind of the Lymm Sta- tionmaster (Mr. Samuel Scragg) a tragedy was averted at the station yesterday morning. At about a quarter-past nine a train came in from Warrington, and at the time the station was naturally somewhat crowded. Whilst the at- tention of the officials was engaged, three boys walked on to the station, and in a minute or so the train again began to move. The station- master then noticed the boys, and told them to go away, which two of them did. The third, however, ran behind the end of the departing train, and made to cross the line just as an express dashed through, travelling in the oppo- site dieection. "Look out! Look out !" called the stationmaster, but the boy did not hear. Running forward, however, Mr. Scragg grabbed him by the arm and pulled him back—just in time to get him clear of the wheels of the ex- press. The boy had a ,lesson which he is not likely to forget for a long time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>CIIRIS'IIIAS FIRE ALARM.</head>
        <p>CIIRIS'IIIAS FIRE ALARM.</p>
        <p>Outbreak at Belgian Refugees' Home Quelled.</p>
        <p>An exciiing incident marked the close of Christmas Day in. Lymm, the quiet of the vil- lage being disturbed at about ten o'clock by the clang of the fire-bell. A fire, it transpired, had broken out at Beechwood Hall—the local habita- tion of the Belgian refugees—but happily it was extinguished before any serious damage had been done. _</p>
        <p>The circumstances were as follows: Shortly before ten o'clock on the evening in question, a telephonic message was received at the Coun- cil Offices, asking that the Fire Superintendent (Mr. William Kirkpatrick) an'd another fire- man should go to Beechwood Hall at once, as an outbreak of fire was suspected. They</p>
        <p>INOT (Regd. Trade Mark.) COFFEE WSIENCE The Cofee Maid, of Syminston's, says: "My motto is: 'Keep the kettle boiling I'— Then I can always have a cup of Symington's iriStantly." =OS SYMINGTON CO.. Edinbtor4h and !mien.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <p>straightway set out, but before their arrival at the hall, another message was sent asking for the brigade to be summoned. Messrs. T. Dixon and Mercer_ accordingly rang the bell, and n de- _</p>
        <p>tachment of the brigade shoi:tly set out Rena- ing the hall, they found that flames had broken. out underneath the planked floor of a bedroom, but that the Belgians had already extinguished them with buckets of water.. What might have been a disastrous fire was thus averted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>CHRISTMAS FOOTB.ILL. Victory for Wednesday Club After Keen Match.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS FOOTB.ILL. Victory for Wednesday Club After Keen Match.</p>
        <p>The success of the football match played on Christmas Day, on the Pepper-street ground, between the Lymm Wednesday and Saturday Clubs, quite exceeded 'expectations, and it is hoped that. the sum qf about Xi will be handed over to the local fled Cross Hospital—which shows that there was an excellent "gate." Mr. Alfred Watkin set the sphere rolling After which a keen contest commenced between the two teams, under the respective captaincies of G. Copeland and C. Whitfield. Mr. Hoodson (treasurer of the Working Men's Committee) acted as .a linesman, but unfortunately Mr. Tweedale was unable to fulfil the duties of Second linesman owing to a slight illness. This place was, however,. taken by Mr. Robt. Dandy. Per the Saturday team, Slant did some splen- did centre-forward Work, whilst Burrows put up a fine show on behalf of the Wednesdays. - G. Owen scored -a. neat goal for the Saturday team from a penalty. Result Wednesdays, 6 goals; Saturdays, 4 goals. Whilst the former team are to be congratulated on a well-earned victory, it must-not be forgotten that the Satur- day team performed very creditably, consider- ing that it has twice recently broken up—owing to its members recruiting-.--and Yet has survived to take part in the match.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>"MOST DANGEROUS CORNER IN</head>
        <p>"MOST DANGEROUS CORNER IN</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE." Provosdl 10 Aequire Ohl Mill for Road Impro‘ emelt t.</p>
        <p>In accordance with notice. previously given, Mr. Gotobed moved, at the monthly meeting of the Urban District Council on Tuesday, "That the Council make an offer to the Lymin Estate Development Company for the purchase of the Old Mill, situate in the village, together with the land attached thereto, by adineasure- ,ment 864 square yards or thereabouts." In doing so he said that the offer he suggested was the outcome of a meeting the Council had with Sir Wm, Lever's agent (Mr. Clarke). 9 , matter. had been very fully discussed by a com- mittee, who were practically unanimous in the decision to make the offer. It was pretty well universally acknowledged that the improvement 'which it was sought to make in the village was a most desirable one, for he could not imagine that there could he A more dangerous corner in the whole district than that round the Old Mill. With the increase of motor and other forms of traffic, it was impe-ative for the sake of the safety of the public biat. that danger should be removed. (Hear, hear.) lie was specially in- terested in the 'question, because the Mill was in the ward which he repiesented, and, living. a 3 he did, near the corne:. on which the obi h.i..ding was situate, he knew the danger it constituted. Many an accident had been nar- rowly averted there, and there could be no doubt that' the Council would be doing a real service to the ratepayers and to users of the road in general if they could get the present dan- gerous corner 'taken off.</p>
        <p>First Cost Not The Last! • Mr. Cowper seconded, remarking that it was a pleasnre to him to know that at last something was to he done in the matter.</p>
        <p>Mr. bolt, whilst not objecting to the pro- posal, said there was one thing which ought not to be forgotten, and that was that the first cost would not be the last. A big expenditure would be necessitated in filling in the cellairs, which were very much below the level of the road, and other filling in, as well as walling awl fencing, would have to be undertaken if they were going to make the place as it should be. There was a feeling in the district that if the Council had gone the right way to work about twelve months ago, they would probably have got the Old Mill thrown in with the water scheme. (bear, hear.)</p>
        <p>The Chairman intimated that further time would be afforded the Council to go into the matter before any real offer was made. Arr. ,Cowpei• said the corner at the Mill, to Which Mr. Gotobed had referred, was the most dangerous corner in Cheshire, and if by spend- ing money in making it safer the Council only saved one life the cost involved ,would be well wortn while.. • •</p>
        <p>The proposition was then put to the meeting and carried nnanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>VILLAGE HAPPENINGS IN 19Ii.</head>
        <p>VILLAGE HAPPENINGS IN 19Ii.</p>
        <p>Not Quite An Earthly Paradise.</p>
        <p>Much regret was caused .in Lymm early in March by the tragic death of Councillor W. Gould, who accidentally fell from a Midland express train near Kettering. A. mild sensation was occasiqned in Lymm at the•end of April when it was announced at a meeting of the Council that Mr. Samuel Davies had resigned his seat, after having been a mem- ber of the Council or some time, and its chair- man until shortly before his resignation. When Councillor Warburton was elected chairman of the Lymm Urban 'District Council on April 21st he said that he -should have had the chair two years earlier, but was kept out by "a wicked and infamous conspiracy." The annual report of the Medical Officer of Health for Lymm District Council contained the statement that from a sanitary Point of view the Cheshire township Was eminently suitable as a residential district. They could not, said the Medical Officer, offer an earthly Paradise at Lymm, but they did provide such sanitary con- ditions in their undoubtedly beautiful district. as equalled many, and surpassed other, districts for miles around. A by-election in the ileatley Ward of Lymm on May 23rd resulted in Mr. F. Ingham (Inde- pendent) being returned to the Urban District.. Council. he polling 212 votes as against 99 re- corded for his opponent, Mr. W. 11. Finch (T.). After at first abandoning the project, the Lymm people decided to crown their May Queen. as usual, and Miss 'Dorothy Pearson, a pupil at tile Grammar School, was selected for the honour, her Coronation taking place on Whit.. Tuesday. At a subsequent business meeting it was reported that the receipts totalled about .Cl4O, and that 'after meeting expenses there was a balance in hand of 1.26 16s. •</p>
        <p>At their meeting in September the Lymm Urban District Council received, with regret, the resignation of the Rector (the Rev. G. B. Thurston), who was described by one of the members present as the most business-like clergyman he had ever come across. In October. Mr. Alexander Mclntyre, who had formerly had a seat on the Council, was elected unopposed to fill the vacancy.</p>
        <p>The 'District Auditor's report for the past year presented to the Lymm 17r1.)an. District. Councillors at their meeting in November shaved. that the indebtedness of the district had been quadrupled iti:a year, and ,now stands at .1;36;043 ss. 41.</p>
        <p>At several meetings of the Lymm Council during the year references were made to Un- desirable visitors who made Sunday afternoon trips to the village, and for the purpose of in- dulging in more or less objectionable "drinking: orgies." One party of trippers were said to, have brought a barrel of beer with them and. to have "dished it out" opposite the house of the Councillor who first brought the matter of these Sunda y drinking excursions to the notice of the Council.</p>
        <p>0</p>
        <p>NS )04 • • 1 ji pi ,</p>
      </div>
