      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>CHRISTMAS BOX FOR WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS BOX FOR WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Five Point Win Over Leigh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>FOLLOWED BY BIG DEFEAT AT LEEDS.</head>
        <p>FOLLOWED BY BIG DEFEAT AT LEEDS.</p>
        <p>Notes by "Centre."</p>
        <p>The Christmas Day match at Wilderspool attracted only a moderate attendance, all things considered. About 2,500 persons paid for admis- sion at the turnstiles, 'and they got value for their money in the shape of an interesting. and well-contested game. Leigh were the opponents .and they put up a vastly better-fight than they did in the opening match of the season, though the Warrington lads again demonstrated their superiority and won by a margin of five points. Warrington were without the.servicesof Jolley, Who has not yet recovered from the injiiries, he UStained in. the 'Batley . match. Dainteth 'depu- tised for him, and did very well in the, to him. nwonted position.</p>
        <p>LEIGH'S UNFULFILLED PROMISE. -I'igh opened most promisingly, giving a smart display of handling and indulging in a- iluniber of dashing onslaughts, but the home •team l soon found the measure of them, though not before Winstanley had cleverly bnodwinked, them and had put on a really brilliant try, Which went unconverted. loused by the re verse, the home team ,took- to attacking, and, -though•oft-times repulsed, the Leigh mOn could .not shake them off. At length came the reward. of -their perseverance, Critchley placing a pen- alty goal. Leigh then commenced a series of impetuous attacks, in which scant regard was paid to the laws of the game, and several times the referee found it necessary to penalise them. For a most glaring infringement -Warrington :Were awarded a free-kick, and Brookes taking it, gave the Wilderspool bays the lead. War- rington then tried what they could do in the Way' of pressing, and they considerably embar- rassed the visitors' defence, which at one time showd decided signs of going to pieces.</p>
        <p>thing more than a reverse; it *was a debacle. True, some excuse may be found for Warrington in the fact that they were bereft of the services of Jolley and Bradshaw, but, to say the least of it, the figure they cut on the Headingley ground was disappointing. The match 'was originally put down for settlement on October 21st, but owing to Warrington being engaged in a Lancashire Cup-tie on that day, a postpone- ment was necessary. In the interim Leeds have come on considerably, and their win on Mon- day made the sixth that they have scored in succession. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>WARRINGTON ASSERT THEMSELVES. –</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON ASSERT THEMSELVES. -</p>
        <p>however,. the visitors pulled iheii;el:e";—to- gether shortly before half-time, and scrappy Play on their part gave way to combined action, Which resulted in Keegan passing to Grundy, When the latter was hi good position, and en- abling him to get over, the goal-kick failing. Throughout the second half Critchley was an absentee from the •Warrington ranks, that .Player having injured himself in endeavouring to prevent Leigh's second fry. Thus handi- 'Capped Warrington played a more wary game, and the fruits of their changed tactics !was to he -seen in the -result. In the thick of a it* attack the Leigh men again broke the rules, -and Brookes, taking the penalty kick, put the teams on equal - terms, amid Much cheering; -from the home supporters. Attack and counter- attack were the order of the next few' minutes and then Warrington's skill and weight began to tell and Leigh were, for the remainder of the lune, held pretty -tightly in check, save for a 4em, spasmodic and ineffectual breaks-away. bainteth distinguished himself by a dashing 1,110. which considerably fluttered the visitors' "ovecotes, but it was not until the end was Vv..9se at hand that the winning .points 'came. aYwell got going on his own and treated the l'ectittors to a really tricky run. At the viehological moment he passed to Brookes, O nipped over the line in tine style, and, sub-. added to lii laarels by pliably, on teal points. When time was called the read: Warrington, four goals. one try Points); Leigh, two tries (six points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>TUE DEBACLE AT LEEDs. orkshiremen's Thirty-fix e I 11111:11- lenged Points.</head>
        <p>TUE DEBACLE AT LEEDs. orkshiremen's Thirty-fix e I 11111:11- lenged Points.</p>
        <p>Warrington met with the biggest trouncing they have had this season 4,1 n Monday. Leeds administering it. Thirty-five points did the Yorkshiremen pile up, and not a single response could the Wilderspool lads make. It was some-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>AVARRINGTON " BEATEN.</head>
        <p>AVARRINGTON " BEATEN.</p>
        <p>'On Boxing Day Warrington "A" entertained flie St. Helens Rees. at Wilck-rspool. The visi- tors had the best of matters, thongh the home lads put up a game fight and made their oppon- ents exert themselves •to the full. The _final score read: St. Helens' Rees., 2 goals • 2 tries (10 points); Warrington, 1 try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>iNTERMEDIATE RUGBY LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>iNTERMEDIATE RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S MATCHES. At Wilderspool.</p>
        <p>'Wycliffe 0.13. v. St. nn'. 0.11.; referee, Mr. 41. Beardsell. Arpley-street 0.8. v. Beamont Athletic. 0.11:; Cienrge Crozier.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>In Victoria Park</head>
        <p>In Victoria Park</p>
        <p>'St. Barnabas' 0.13. v. St. Alban's "B", 0.B.; W, Bibby, Heatbside Bewsey 0.13. v. Silver-street 0.B.; Mr. R. Williams. St. James' Juniors 0.13. v. Heathside Frogball ‘1)-11.: Mr. W. Jam,.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>LAST WEEK'S RESULTS</head>
        <p>LAST WEEK'S RESULTS</p>
        <p>'St. James' 0.11., 1:3 pt,-..; Arpley-ztreet 0.13.-, 0. ASt. Mary's 0.8., 0; Bolton 0.8., 0. :Sacred Heart,-0.8., 19; St. Alhan's "B" 0.8.. 0 *A protest was registered with regard to this Ina tell.</p>
        <p>NRINCHESTER AND DISTRICT LFAGUE.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS'• RECS. v. CADISHEAD AND IRLAM.</p>
        <p>These teams met in. the First Division of the above League on the GOise:ilane -Recreation around on Boxing Day, a somewhat one-sided game ending in a win for the homesters by 13 points (2 goals 3 tries) to nil.</p>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY. `l'</p>
        <p>RYLANDS' RECS. v. ABRAM RANGERS.</p>
        <p>At Abram:-</p>
        <p>Rylands': F. Horroeks; J. Griffith, A. }for- rocks, H. Povey and C. Povey : G. Warren and J. Gallop; J. Brandwood, J. Whittaker, F. Potter, E. Smith, W.rShaw andiF. Ragaley. Reserve, A. Rowlinson.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL "AS tlsi -AL</p>
        <p>" Tommy" Pleased With The Derision</p>
        <p>An interesting ,sidelight is thrown on the question whether football should he continued during the present crisis by a letter just sent to a football offiCial in Blackburn by Mr. m Haworth, a local referee called up as a reser- vist. He was sent a referee's chart, which lie ,was greatly interested in whiff+ in the, trenches. He writes:—</p>
        <p>"You would be surprised low Tommy takes an interest in football, although he is in tli9 trenches. I think if the F.A. bad decided to abandon the remaining matches, it would have been a great disappointment to all out here. It livens them up to get the results and to hear the jokes' as to which team is best." -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>LEEDS 21 POINTS UP AT HALF-TIME.</head>
        <p>LEEDS 21 POINTS UP AT HALF-TIME.</p>
        <p>At the interval Leeds led by 21 points to nil. All the way -through the .Mbiety Warrington had been compelled to act on the defensive, the Leeds attacking being of the most persistent and dangerous character. Rarely did the Wil- derspool forwards get going, .and, on the few occasions When they did manage something like a run, they foumj, themselves up against a defence that was thooughly reliable; indeed, as. the result proved, Ouite impregnable. Mir- field put on the first try for Leeds, and before the interval had added another to his laurels, whilst further tries came from Campbell, Jones and .W. A. Davies. Three of the tries were con- verted.</p>
        <p>Early on in the second half Carter added a fifth try for the home team, and a few minutes later W. A. Davies put on a sixth. Yet further tries came from Rees and Jones, and when the final whistle sounded the score, board read : Leeds, 4 goals 9 tries (35 points); Warrington, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>HUDDERSFIELD REVENGED!</head>
        <p>HUDDERSFIELD REVENGED!</p>
        <p>Turn Tables on Warrington in New</p>
        <p>Year's Day Game.</p>
        <p>The New :Year's Day match between War- rington and Iluddersfield attracted a specially large crowd to Wilderspool, somewhere about four thousand people passing the • turnstiles. Warrington hold the unique honour of having defeated the Yorkshiremen on their own ground this season. Though Warrington lost they put up .a praiseworthy fight against a team that has evidently come on wonderfully sing the clubs last met. In the first half Huddersfield put up two tries and two goals to Warrington's single try and goal. Warrington started well, but as time wore on their attacks grew fewer and further between.</p>
        <p>Huddersfield- opened their score per Rogers, who -ran in unopposed under the posts. Gronow had no difficulty in landing a goal. The equal- ising points came just before the end of the first half, T. Cunliffe crossing the line and Brookes converting. Almost immediately after Clarke forced his Way over and Grimm* added the goal points.</p>
        <p>• Huddersfield' attat'ked hotly- at the opening of the second half, and Moorhouse nipped over the Gronow failing with the kick. Following the kick out, Warrington pressed,, and Tranter was dribbling through, when he was obstructed.' The referee awarded Warrington a- try, and Brookes kicked a good goal. Just afterwards Moorhouse evaded all opposition and passed to Cileeson, who scored at tile corner, Holland kicking a brilliant goal against the wind. The next minute Lee scored, and before the end an- other try canie to Huddersfield per Todd, Hol- land adding the minor points. When the whistle sounded the score-board read: Hudders- field, 4 goals 6 tries (26 points); Warrington, 2 goals 2 trice (10 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>DESERTERS AND ABSENTEES.</head>
        <p>DESERTERS AND ABSENTEES.</p>
        <p>A Starving Runaway. At the Borough Police Court on Tuesday, Private Wilfred Law, of the 3rd ,Battalion of the Welsh Fusiliers, pleaded guilty to a charge of being a deserter since the 20th December.— It was stated that prisoner gave himself up at the Police Station, saying that he wished to rejoin his regiment.—The Chief Constable in- formed the Bench that Law was in a state of collapse when he did so, having done a good deal of tramping. They had had to give him warm food to bring him round. No one knew why he had deseitted.—Prisoner was handed over to a military escort. . AN ADMITTED ABSENTEE.</p>
        <p>Private Thomas Makinson, of the Loyal North Lancashire Regiment, who had surrendered at the Police Station and admitted being an absentee from his regiment since 'December 22hd, .was ordered to be handed over to a mili- tary escort, at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday. - TERRIERS WHO WANTED TO SPEND CHRISTMAS AT HOME.</p>
        <p>Amongst a number of cases of unauthorised absence from the Army—which, under the chair- manship of Councillor H. Roberts, were dealt with on Tuesday at the Borough Police Court— was one in. which five Warrington Territorials figured. They were Privates Frederick Dicken- son, John •Charnock. George 'Wood, Thomas Cunningham and Matthew Thomas Knowles, all of whom had absented themselves vtrithout leave from the 4th -(Territorial) Battalion of the South' Lancashire Regiment—which is at present in camp at Tunbridga Wells, Kentand travelled up to Warrington, that they might spend Christmas at home. All five pleaded guilty. The Chief Constable stated that' the defend- ants, with the exception of Knowles, had come to the Police Station the previous morning, say- ing that they had absented themselves without leave from their regiment, and that they had no railway tickets or .money -to .rejoin it. They had been • brought before the magistrates on Monday, the Bench then directing that the police should do anything they could for the men. The commanding officer had now. been communicated With, and an escort was being sent. Knowles had been found by Supt. Williamson, who had brought him down to the Police Station. The five defaulters were ordered to be handed over to an escort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>ANOTHER DESERTER CAUGHT.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER DESERTER CAUGHT.</p>
        <p>Private hairy Ratelloffe, of the 4th ,Battalion, South Lancashire Regiment (stationed at Black- -0001) was, at the Borough Police Couit on Thursday, charged with being a deserter since December 27th.-1t was stated that, acting upqn information received, a constable visited 'a house, 16, Lincoln-street, Warrington, and there found prisoner, who said he had been absent from his regiment for three days.—The Chief Constable said that a telegram had been sent to the military authorities, and an escort was on its Way to Warrington.—Prisoner was ordered to be handed over to the escort SAID HE WAS ILL.</p>
        <p>Another absentee—Lance-Corporal John Cart- wright, of the 3rd South Lancashire Regiment —was before the Court on Friday, and his ex- cuse for leaving the camp at Great Crosby with- out leave on November 28th was that he was ill. He added that he had informed the regimental doctor cd the fact.—He was remanded to await an escort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>YEAR'S MEMORIES.</head>
        <p>THE YEAR'S MEMORIES.</p>
        <p>Achievements in Various Branches of Sport. FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>During the 1913-14 football season the War- rington first team played 34 League matches, half of which were won. They finished up 11th in the Northern Union League Table, as com- pared with 18th the previous season. They scored 280 points, and opponents put up 332 against them. The "A" team finished sixth in the Combination Table, as compared with 20th the season before, and, of the 30 matches played, they won 19, scoring 319 points to the 200 regis- tered against them. Up to date this season Warrington first have played 18 matches, nine of which they have won, whilst nine have been lost. The results have been as follows, the Warrington score being given first in each case :—v. Leigh, 6-5; v. Huddersfield, 13-12; v. Broughton Rangers, 5-8; v. Rochdale, 5-8; v. Oldham, 6-10; v. Salford, 5-0;' v. Runcorn, 20-7; v. Barrow (Lancashire Cup-tie), 10-0; v. Hunslet, 5-9; v. Salford (Lancashire Cup, second round), 22-10; v. Rochdale (Lancashire Cup, third round), 5-13; v. St. Helens, 15-6; v. Swinton, 5-0; v. Barrow, 3-9; v. Batley, 3-10; v. Leigh, 11—fl; v. Leeds, 0-35; v. Huddersfield, 10-26. At the annual meeting of the Warrington N.U. Football Club held in May, it was reported that the previous season finished 'up with a profit of £447 15s. 2d. ,</p>
        <p>- Councillor Osboine was unanimously elected president. By a large majority Mr. T. P. Bradshaw was re-elected lion. secretary.</p>
        <p>The 1913-14 football season was a record one for Rylauds' Recs., in that they did not lose a single League match, whilst they were only defeated four times out of a total of 29 fixtures.</p>
        <p>RUNNING.</p>
        <p>"Billy" Bolton won the five miles' cham- pionship of the Warrington Athletic Club in March last.</p>
        <p>The Warrington A.C. won the senior cham- pionship race in connection with the West Lancashire Cross-Country Running Association in February last, having the 2nd, 3rd, 4th, sth, 6th and Bth men home, and having a total of only 28 points, against the 70 registered by the second club—Sutton • Wirral being third with 77 points. Two Warrington runners—F: Antrobus and W. Bolton—made such a good show in the National Cross-Country Championship contest at Chesham in March last that they were</p>
        <p>selected for the English team in the Inter- national Cross-Country event.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the Warrington Cricket and Bowling Club held in March it was reported that the financial standing of the club was better than it had ever been, there being a credit balance of .293 is. 6d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>SOMETHING LIKE A CATCH!</head>
        <p>SOMETHING LIKE A CATCH!</p>
        <p>A pike weighing nib. lloz. and measuring 38in. in length and 16in. in girth was taken from the River Gowy by Mr. W. Bailey, of Orford-street, Warrington, in April.</p>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL BOWLING SEASON.</p>
        <p>The local bowling season was highly success- ful from every point of view, although at the latter end the war interfered to a certain ex- tent. The following is a list of the winners in the principal contests :—ltiberal Association, "Peter Peacock" Cup, 'St. Austin's; annual handicap, Mr. G. Shaw; "Crosfield" Cup, Mr. W. Farrington. Works' Association—" Finlay- son" Cup, Wire Ropery ; individual merit, Mr. A. Gleave; annual main, Mr. P. Wright; War- rington and District Association—Handicap, Mr. J. Hill; "Harold Smith" Cup, St John's No. 1. An innovation was the commencement of a Municipal Bowling League, which proved very popular.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>SCHOLARS' SPORTS.</head>
        <p>SCHOLARS' SPORTS.</p>
        <p>Over 2,500 children competed in the Schools' Sports held on the Warrington Football Ground on Empire Day, May 25th. Annie Houghton Baines (Beamont School) won the girls' championship, and Reginald Herbert Watson (Arpley-street School) the boys'.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>31r. A. H., Crosfield gave) proof of his skill as a cueist when, at the Libeital Club on February 23rd, he indulged in an interesting game of billiards with a well-known local exponent of the game, 111 r. T. Horrabin, and was only beaten by 47 in a game of 250 up, the occasion being the presentation to the Second Division of the Warrington Billiard League by the loser of a handsome challenge shield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>PADGATE UNITED v. GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>At Padgate; kick-off 2.45 :</p>
        <p>Padgati: P. Taylor; W. Taylor and T3ns= worth; Williams, Cartwright and Dennett; Hardman, Potter, Ball, Downes and Woods. Reserves: Pass and Davies. PADDINGTON v. ST. BARNABAS'.</p>
        <p>At Paddington; kick-off 2.45 : Paddington: G. Harrison; E. Holmes (capt.) and C. Smith; W. Holmes, J. Taylor and W. Spencer; T. Wainwright, S. Taylor, W. Garnett, J. Darbyshire and J. Bramliall. Reserves: Chamberlain and Woodhead. PADDINGTON RESERVE v. WALTON. At Walton:—</p>
        <p>Paddington. Reserve: S. Summers (capt.r; J. Dwerryhouse and E. Vickers; J. Goodfellow, J. Griffiths and B. Chadwick; - J. Pickering, G. WariTurton, J. Jackson, J.. Redford and J. Adcroft. Reserves: J. Holcroft and Harrison. GREAT SANKEY v. CRONTON.</p>
        <p>At Cronton, in the Widnes and District Alliance; kick-off 2.45 p.m. : Great Sankey: H. Brindle; E. GloVer and B. Kelly; J. E. Glover, H. DitChfield.and H. Payne; T. Roberts, T. Whitfield, T. Hawthorn, S. Poul- ter and A. Houghton. • .</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH v. CADISHEAD WESLEYANS.</p>
        <p>At West-avenue, in the North Cheshire League :</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath: Hooper; Gosling and Clws- ters; Noden, Barlow and liylanee; Lloyd, Ver- non, _Smith, Pearse and Starkey.</p>
        <p>Cadishead are leaders in the North Cheshire' League, and are. making a great bid foi• ithe championship. Their appearance at Stock"fon Heath should be a great attraction.</p>
        <p>EMMANUEL INSTITUTE v. RISLEY.</p>
        <p>In Victoria Park</p>
        <p>Emmanuel: P. Broadhurst; R Crozier and S. Hughes; T. Hughes; E. Hardy and. W. Ayres; W. Eaton, S. Roberts (capt.), W. Eaton. G. Done and E. Bevan. Reserve, C. Burgess.</p>
        <p>A Weed Destroyer. First American: "I've got a letter from my son out West." Second Alpericau : ."So? What's Torn doing now?" First. American: ".That's what I can'l make out. He says he is engaged in the destruction of weeds. Now, that may. mean he's smoking a good many' cigars, or that he is trying to induce some widow to make a second venture, or it may simply mean that he is doing farm work."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <p>DE R FECTLY healthy children I are generally happy; but a very slight stomach trouble is often enough to make them miserable. That is the time to try a Steedman's Powder. You will find that it acts like magic. Steedman's Powders contain no*l poison or narcotic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 2, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <p>Fairfield United Have Clear Lead.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>PADDINGTON FAIL AT HOME.</head>
        <p>PADDINGTON FAIL AT HOME.</p>
        <p>On Christmas Day the only match amongst the clubs in our local League was played in Vic- toria Park between the Central and .Bank Quay Recs. There was a fairly good attendance, and although the game was evenly contested, there were no exciting movements to record. The final score was two goals each, and it was a good reflex of the display given, although each team missed several good openings of securing the winning goal.</p>
        <p>On Boxing Day one of the best fixtures on the list was. cancelled owing to the holidays, the Fairfield United Club being unable to raise a team to meet the Mission Lads of -Earlestown owing to several of their players going to their respective homes to spend diet': Christmas holi- days.</p>
        <p>The game between the Y.M.C.k. and Newton Cons. was completely spoiled by the inclement weather. Rain was falling at the time arranged to kick off, and the start was delayed. A change took place and the players turned out, and the ground was in a wretched condition. The spec- tators were few. The game ended in favour of the Y.M.C.A. by the odd goal in five, which was as fitting a result as the quality of play served up.</p>
        <p>Bank Quay Recs. and i Orford White Star ought to have played on Boxing Day at Lovely- lane, but owing to the bad weather the ground was unfit and the referee declared the game off.</p>
        <p>At Paddington there was a large crowd to witness the, game between, two keen rivals,- Hol- lins Green bringing a larg3 number of their supporters with them. Nor were they disap- pointed with the display given by both teams, as the game was keenly contested from start to finish. For the first half-hour there was not 'much to choose between the teams. Then, how- -ever, the home backs showed signs of weakness; and the visitors gradually asserted their superiority, and led at the interval by one goal to none. The second portion was more keenly contested than the first, and for a time it looked odds on the homesters equalising, but Ellams in goal was at the to of his form, and he defied all attempts to get the ball past him: The visitors' second goal was a well-earned-point from a free 'kick near their own goal; their forwards gained possession and went to the Opposite goal and scored without a Paddington player even touch- ing the ball. Mellor, at centre-half, for the visitors, played in his best style, while the home team's full-backs were often at fault in the second portion, dribbling with the ball, in- stead of clearing their positions. The Pad- dington team, no doubt, got a set-back, as this was the match they preferred to win in their whole fixtures.</p>
        <p>The Stockton Heath Club journeyed to Altrincham to meet the Linotype team, and playing with only nine players managed to 'share the points. They had their opportunities to win the match, but 'practically threw them away. , They gained three:penalty kicks, and only scored off one. The Heathens • have now drawn five games, and have failed to gather the full points by failing, to: score from penalty kicks on each occasion.</p>
        <p>Notes by "Linesman."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>Warrington and District League Table up to December 31, 1914. ' –</head>
        <p>Warrington and District League Table up to December 31, 1914. ' -</p>
        <p>Goals—</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For. Agst. Ps. Fairfield United Orford White Star Longford United Earlestown St. J. M 11 6 3 2 31 15 14 St. Barnabas' Hollins Green l4 6 7 1 31 27 13 Paddington</p>
        <p>EarlestOwn 10 5 4 1 25 20 11 Newton Cons. Bank Quay Recs. Warrington Central</p>
        <p>Fairfield Athletic</p>
        <p>Elmwood Cons. Latchford Baptists</p>
        <p>• In ,the League table up to the end of Decem- ber, 1914, there are only two clubs who have completed half of their fixtures, these being Fairfield United and Hollins Green, with Pad- dington close up with thirteen. The ,United have established themselves with a clear lead of their nearest rivals, the White Star, and the Longford United Club being 4. good third. The clubs outside the town are led by the Earlestown St. John's Mission, with fourteen points for eleven matches, and on their present form they will get still nearer the top of the ladder. It •will require the best efforts of the United to keep their position, as they have to meet the Mission lads twice yet, and a slip by the United may put their position as leaders in jeopardy.</p>
        <p>As the season is now at the half-way house it will be necessary for some of the clubs •to re- arrange their postponed fixtures as early as pos- sible, if they wish to avoid having to play evening matches later on, and this should be done at the next League meeting, if the fixtures are to be brought to a successful finish. 4;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>VOARRINGTON CENTRAL v. BANK QUAY RECS.</p>
        <p>At Victoria Park on Christmas morning, the above teams opposed each other with the fol- lowing players, under thj3 control of Referee S. C. Essenhigh:— Central : •Williams; Glover and Ashcroft; Williams, Brown and Emson; Farrell, Ken- nedy, Holmes, S. Hands and Manner. - Recs. : Monks; Berry and Smith; Long, Wor- rall and Chester; Weston, Millington, Banner, Davis and Deakin.</p>
        <p>The visiting forwards made the first aggres- sive movement, and in the opening stages Ban- ner slipped when a splendid chance was offered to him, 'and directly after he was unfortunate with a low ground shot, which passed on the wrong side of the post. By good passing be- tween Farrell and Kennedy; the Recs.' goal was put in danger, Smith being at fault, and Berry cleared at the critical moment, but the Central at this time were the smarter lot. The visitors now played with more vim, and put the home- sters on the defensive, when Banner registered the first goal for the Recs. • Each goal was assailed in turq. Worrall was playing a strong game at centre-half for the Recs.•, often beat the home forwards, and placed Well to his own colleagues, and he followed up by adding a second goal. The Central made strong efforts to reduce the lead. A free kick against Banner was well placed, and Monks had to concede a corner, which was got away, half-time being called with the score 2 to none in favour of the Recs.</p>
        <p>The game continued in favour of the Recs., but owing to the slippery condition their for- wards'missed easy. chances. Banner tried an in- dividual effort, but was defeated. A raid on the .Recs.' goal was cleared by Monks, but Hands gained possession and sent the ball be- tween the uprights, scoring the first goaT for the Central. The Recs. were forced to defend and Farrell missed scoring after he had beaten the visitors' backs. It was only by making occasional raids- that the Recs. got past the half- way line, and their defence were hard pressed. Faulty clearances by Smith at full-back enabled the home .forwards to keep up the attack, and at hingili Farrell was successful in equalising the scores. Result: Waitington Central, 2 goals; Bank Quay Recs.; 2 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>PADDINGTON v. HOLLINS GREEN.</head>
        <p>PADDINGTON v. HOLLINS GREEN.</p>
        <p>The above teams met on Boxing Day on the ground of the former club, when, owing to the keen rivalry which exists between the clubs, there was a large crowd present. The follow- ing players turned out Paddington: Harrison; E. Holmes and Cham- berlain; Holmes, Taylor and Garnett; Wain- wright, Holcroft, Darbyshire, Smith and Brain- hall.</p>
        <p>Hollins Green: Ellams; Clarke and Cleworth; Buckley, Etchells and Mellor; Wright, Ather- ton, Billington, Taylor and Atkinson. Referee, W. Dennett.</p>
        <p>Derbyshire -set the ball rolling for the home team, and play opened in a very keen manner. The ball travelled quickly from end to end, each goal being assailed. Gradually the home backs began to kick weakly, and they were repeatedly beaten for possession by their 'opponents. The visitors' forwards were smarter on the ball and adopted the long passing style, which enabled them to attack more often than the home for- wards. The first goal came a few minutes before the interval, E. Holmes being the offender with back charging an opponent, after being dispossessed of the ball. Mellor took the kick and scored. Paddington then Made a strong assault on the Visitors' goal, and a keen scrim- mage ensued in the goalmouth, Ellams eventu- ally clearing. Interval: Hollins Green, 1 goal; Paddington; nil.</p>
        <p>_ The second half opened in a more vigorous fashion, and at times signs of bad temper were shown,, and several free kicks were awarded. The home team at this stage were the more aggressive, and Bramhall had several shots, but was not successful. The visitors' backs de- fended splendidly, and managed to keep their lines intact, Mellor at centre-half being promi- nent both in defence and attack, and he set his forwards going on many occasions, but they failed when near the goal. The home forwards again showed better form, and for about fifteen minutes they held the visitors in their own half, but Ellams between the uprights defied all their efforts. Off-side against Darbyshire brought re- lief. to the visitors, and their forwards raced away- to the home goal, when a grand centre from their right wing was met by Billington, and he had not much difficulty in adding the goal. From now to the close of play the game was strenuously contested, but no further goals were added, the result being: Rollins Green, 2 goals; Paddington, nil.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN Y.M.C.A. v. NEWTON CONS</p>
        <p>On Boxing Day, on the ground of the former. Y.M.C.A.: Martin; Chambers and Mort; Owen, Goulden and Atkinson; Wilson, Gilman. Riley, Hughes and Barlow. Cons.: Glover; Tithers and Lawrance ; Wightman, Lennox and Holcroft ; Gleaves. Rubottam, Clarke, Dodd and Dagnall. The visitors were early in evidence, and from the kick off made tracks for the home goal, and a centre from Gleaves across the goal mouth went a-begging. The leather was now trans- ferred to the opposite end, where a sharp shot from Gilman opened the score, after seven minutes' play. From the centre kick there was a spell of even exchanges chiefly in midfield, and the visitors gradually gained ground in the home half, and Rubottam equalised with a shot well out of the reach of Martin. The visitors' backs were the most reliable, while the home forwards were the more alert. As the interval was drawing near, Hughes gained the lead for the Y.M.C.A. half-time: Y.M.C.A., 2 goals; Cons., 1 goal. On restarting the game was keenly and evenly contested, but owing to the slippery condition of the ground surface many good openings failed to materialise and mis-kicks were numerous. Gleaves made the scores equal with a good effort, but Hughes was snccessful in beating 'Glover. Result: Earlestown Y.M.C.A., 3 goals; Newton Cons., 2 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>CIIESTER AND RUNCORN DISTRICT LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>CIIESTER AND RUNCORN DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>BALFOUR-ROAD OLD BOYS v. WESTON.</p>
        <p>The above teams met in opposition on Boxing Day on the Volunteer Grounds; Runcorn. After even play in midfield the visitors assumed the offensive, and B. Conway was successful in regis- tering the first point. On resuming the visitors continued to show the best form. The home side gained a fruitless- corner, from which Moly- neaux gained possession in his own half and raced away and scored the visitors' second goal. As the end was drawing near the villagers at- tacked the home goal, when one of the backs handled. The resultant penalty kick was en- trusted to Talbot, and he scored the third goal. From now to the finish the visitors gave the best display, and finally ran out winners as follows: Weston, 3 goals; Balfour-road Old Boys, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. • STOCKTON HEATH v. LINOTYPE.</head>
        <p>NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. • STOCKTON HEATH v. LINOTYPE.</p>
        <p>• The above teams met on Boxing Day on the ground of the latter. The Linotypers won the toss and the visitors started the game with only nine players. Owing to the heavy rains the ground was in a terrible condition and accurate passing was impossible. The first half was well contested under the circumstances, and at the interval no score had been made.</p>
        <p>. During the second portion the game was more keenly contested and both teams played hard. The Linotypers registered the first goal, which was soon followed by an equalising point by the visitors. A penalty kick being awarded them, the goalkeeper saved, but the ball was eventually put between the uprights. The visitors had two more penalty kicks, but failed to increase the scare, the result being a drawn game of one goal each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>FRIENDLY NIATCII.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY NIATCII.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH v. WALTON.</p>
        <p>The above teams met on Christmas morning in a friendly encounter. The visitors were minus two of 'their regular team, whilst the Heathens had only four of their first team players on. The Heathens won the toss and the game started before a good crowd of spectators. Owing to a thaw the surface of the ground wAs treacherous. After the formal , opening ex- changes the • visitors adapted themselves more quickly to •the conditions, and had the best of the ginie. A centre from their right wing was converted into a goal. Following this the visit- ors continued to have the best of the exchanges, and play was confined chiefly in the home half, , when a, long shot into goal was only partly cleared. A scramble ensued, and the ball was eventually forced through the goal. Half:, time : Walton, 2 goals; Stockton Heath, nil.</p>
        <p>On resuming, the homesters greatly improved in their display, and by a grand individual effort S. Lloyd scored a capital goal, after seven minutes' play. The same player repeated the feat after twenty-five minutes' play. Following this equalising point the ri...itors attacked strongly, and goals were scored by Mortimer and Marsh for the visitors. Result: Walton, 4 goals; Stockton Heath, 2 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>I. TCHES FOR TO-D.% Y.</head>
        <p>I. TCHES FOR TO-D.% Y.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Bank Quay Bees. v. Fairfield -United. Paddington v. St. Barnabas'. Longford United v: Earkostown Earlestown St. John's Mission v. Newton Con Elmwood Cons. v. Warrington Central.</p>
      </div>
