      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <p>BOARD OF GUARDIAM.</p>
        <p>Brief Last Meeting of the Old</p>
        <p>Year.</p>
        <p>A VACANT SEAT.</p>
        <p>The last meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians in 1914 was held on Wednesday, Mr. A. J. Jolley presiding.</p>
        <p>LADY GUARDIAN'S RESIGNATION.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Agnes Simpson, of Heswall, Cheshire, wrote resigning her seat on the Board, in con- sequence of her removal from the town. At the same time she tendered her thanks to the members and officials for the kindness and courtesy she had always received from them, and extended to them good wishes for the New Year.</p>
        <p>The seat was formally declared vacant. SEASONABLE GREETING FROM THE CHAPLAIN.</p>
        <p>The Chaplain (the Rev. W..Bracecamp) wrote from Tunbridge Wells, where he is acting as chaplain to the 4th Battalion of the South Lan- cashire Regiment, tendering seasonable greetings to the members of the Board. He had, he said, hoped to be with them on Christmas Day, but his leave had been cancelled. -" The people here," he addedi" are all very kind to us, but, in spite of it all, we are all anxious to get a move on. We are quite ready and fit to go to the front at any time."</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: I think the Chap- lain is having the time of his life. It is a de- lightful place, is Tunbridge Wells. (Laughter.) Mr. Backhouse : Is the rev. gentleman inspired by a little bit of jealousy? (Renewed laughter.) The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: No not in the least. WOUNDED SOLDIERS NOT TO DISPLACE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>POOR.</head>
        <p>POOR.</p>
        <p>The Assistant Secretary of the Local Govern- ment Board, writing to the Clerk, stated that the Board raised no objection to the Guardians offering a building to the War Office for ,the accommodation of wounded soldiers. At the same time the Board thought it right to add that while, should occasion arise, they would be glad to facilitate the appropriation of any accommodation Which it might" reasonably be supposed could be spared by the Guardians; it was their duty to remember that the accommo- dation was primarily provided to meet the needs of the poor. It was not the wish of the mili- tary authoriti\es that infirmary accommodation which might possibly be expected to be required for the use of the civil population should be taken for the use of the military. Therefore, before the Board could properly approve a pro- posal such as the Guardians had-in mind, they would need to be satisfied that a sufficient mar- gin of accommodation would be at the disposal of the Guardians for th due fulfilment of their primary duties. A Member: What. do they take us for? _ .</p>
        <p>The Chairman: It is meiely a formal letter. The Board, it will be remembered, had offered the use of the new imbecile and epileptic block at present in course of completion. It would accommodate about 200 patients. THE CHAPLAINCY OF THE COTTAGE HOMES.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. I. Wadeson, rector of Croft, wrote stating that he would willingly, continue .to visit the Padgate Cottage Homes as chaplain until a regular appointment was made. He was not aware that the chaplaincy was a paid office under the Guardians when he offered his ser- vices to Mr. Collier during his absence. He imagined it to be simply. a .part of the routine duty of any Vicar of Padgate. The Bishop of Liverpool, in the course of a letter to the Clerk, said: "I regret that the work of the 'Ordination has prevented me writ- ing earlier to acknowledge the receipt of a copy of the resolution adopted by the Warrington Guardians at their meeting on December 16th, agking me to nominate a clergyman for the posi- tion of chaplain of the Cottage Bomes of Pad- gate. I will attend to the matter at once."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>CHILDREN'S DOCTOR'S SALARY.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S DOCTOR'S SALARY.</p>
        <p>The following letter from Dr. Peacocke, medical officer to the Cottage Homes, Padgate, was read by the .Clerk "For twenty years I have been Paid £3O per annum, and I am sure ! you will see that this salary is very inadequate for the work done .nnd the responsibility for looking after two hundred children. -At one time my telephone was paid for by the Board. That was stopped, though largely used for school purposes. I have always ,given of my best in looking after the children, and my duties will materially increase with the advent of very young children to the schools. I leave the matter with every confidence in your hands, knowing that your sense of fairness will prompt you to give me a substantial increase." . The letter was referred to the Padgate Horne Committee.</p>
      </div>
