      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>£7,540 FOR IMPROVEMENTS.</head>
        <p>£7,540 FOR IMPROVEMENTS.</p>
        <p>Corporation Apply for Sanction for a</p>
        <p>Loan.</p>
        <p>The Corporation having applied for sanction to borrow £7,540 for works of street improve- ment in Buttermarket-street, Upper Bank-street, Factory-lane and Orford-road, Mr. Edgar Dud- ley, Local Government Board Inspector, held an inquiry at the Town Hall yesterday. Scarcely any public interest was manifested in the proceedings, present at which were Alder- men Bennett and Bolton, Councillors Pember- ton and Gough, Mr. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), Mr. T. H. Spinks (assistant town clerk), Mr. A. M. Ker (surveyor), Mr. A. M. Piercey (assistant surveyor), and Mr. W. IT. Woodcock (assistant overseer). The Town Clerk explained that the streets had been widened under powers conferred by a Provisional Order granted by the Local Gov- ernment Board, 1909. They required to be re- formed and re-paved. The Surveyor said that the present width of Buttermarket-street varied from 21 to 28 feet, and it was intended to widen it to 60 feet throughout. It was part of the main road be- tween Manchester and Liverpool, and the traffic on it was very heavy. It was proposed to pave the streets with blocks and put a special strip in the centre. The Tramways Committee had considered the matter, and although they had not decided to run tramways up the street, they thought there was a probability of it, and they asked for provision to be made in case of such an event. A strip of setts would be placed in the centre so that they could be taken out easily for the tram-track to be laid, •if neces- sary.</p>
        <p>Upper Bank-street, he said, was widened from 14 feet to 40 feet, and in regard to Factory-lane that was a macadam highway on to which large* works abutted, and, over which there was a great deal of heavy traffic. It was very expen- sive to keep the road in repair, and they were going to put in a concrete foundation, six inches thick, and pave it with four by five-inch setts.</p>
        <p>Orford-road was widened from 30 feet to 50 feet, with a carriage-Way of macadam. The Town Clerk said that the Council had been considering the advisability of applying to the Roads Board for a grant in aid of the cost: The inquiry was closed.</p>
      </div>
