      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>EXAMINER, SA7</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SA7</p>
        <p>DISTRICT NEWS.</p>
        <p>HIGH LEGH.</p>
        <p>Death of Much-Respected Resident.—At Ros- theme 'Church on Thursday afternoon of last week, the funeral took place of Mrs. Platt, wife of Mr. John Platt, of Northwood Hall, High Legh, who died on the previous Monday as the result of a stroke. The deceased, who was well known and much respected in the district, in which she had resided all her life, was 60 years of age. The Rev. E. H. Edwards (Colonel Legh's private chaplain) officiated at the funeral, and the mourners were: Mr. John Platt (husband), Mrs. Piers Edgerley, Mrs. John Cook, Mrs. D. Mort, Miss Evelyn Platt (daughters), Messrs. John Platt, George Platt and Arthur Platt (sons), Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Wright (Liverpool), Mr. and Mrs. Jas. Wright (Afottram), Mr. and Mrs. Henry Wright (Dairy Farm, High Legh), Mr. A. Wright (Blackpool), ' Mrs. Wilkinson (Blackpool), Mrs. Hubert Wright (Blackpool), Mr. William Platt, Mr. and are. Ernest Whitlow (Heatley), Mr. Piers Edgerley, Mr. David Mort, Mr. D. Okell, Mr. and Mrs. William Cook, Mrs. F. Gleave, Miss Emily Wright, Mr. C. Cook, and Mr. and Miss Bucklow. Messrs. J. H. Wright; T. E. Wright, Ernest Whitlow, J. Hewitt, George Hewitt, and Ernest Edgerley were the bearers. A large number of floral tributes were laid on the grave.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>STOCKTON 'LEATH</head>
        <p>STOCKTON 'LEATH</p>
        <p>Soldiers' Wives and Children Entertained.— On Boxing Day the workers of St. Thomas' Band of Hope invited the wives and children of Stockton Heath soldiers to a tea and entertain- ment in the Parish Room, kindly lent by the Vicar for the occasion. The room bad been nicely decorated by Mr. Wightman and Mr. Newall, a noticeable feature being the large inscription, "God Bless Daddy at the War." Sixty-four children were present, some of whom were accompanied by theft- mothers. An ex-</p>
        <p>cellent tea Was proiided, and thoroughly en- joyed. Various games were afterwards indulged in, while songs were given by Mr. Hough, and recitations by Miss Heath. During light re- freshments the Christmas tree was lighted up, and Mr. Stannard (secretary) thanked all who had helped to make the gathering so successful. Each child received an orange and a present, the latter being kindly provided by St. Thomas' Bicycle Club, and the proceedings concluded with the singing of "Auld lang syne." Praise is due to Mr. Hallows and his willing band of workers, who were untiring in their efforts to please, and who had the satisfaction of know- ing that all present had a really good time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>ORFORD.</head>
        <p>ORFORD.</p>
        <p>Carols on the Church Tower.—The Christmas Day services at the Church of St. Margaret and All Hallows, Orford, were commenced as usual by members of the choir and a few friends sing- ing hymns and carols on the top of the church tower. On Sunday evening the annual carol service was held, conducted by the Vicar (the Rev. G. Vale Owen). Christmas carols and an- thems were sung, including "There were Shepherds Abiding in the Field" (Vincent), " How Beautiful upon the Mountains," and "Angels ever Bright and Fair," as a solo and chorus, the solo being very well taken by Master Walter Ellis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>WIN WICK.</head>
        <p>WIN WICK.</p>
        <p>School Children Entertained.—The elder scholars. in the day and Sunday schools had their annual tea and entertainment on Tuesday evening. A very good tea was partaken of, and the entertainment, which was thoroughly en- joyed and appreciated, was provided by Mr. W. Rigby's Pierrots. The infant scholars had their tea on Wednesday afternoon. Their entertain- ment took the form of a Magic Well. Bath</p>
        <p>infants and elder scholars were supplied with oranges. Both parties were very kindly given by Mr. J. W. Knowles.</p>
        <p>Rector's Theatre Party.—The members of the church choir were invited by the Rev. Canon Penrhyn to visit the Royal Court Theatre, War- rington, on Wednesday evening, to see "The Marriage Market." A 'bus was provided to take them down and seats were booked for them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>PEN KETH.</head>
        <p>PEN KETH.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Christmas Service.—ln the absence of the Rev. H. T. Dixon, the Rev. H. Ogden (pastor of the Golborne-street Baptist Church, Warrington) conducted the Christmas morning service at the Penketh Wesleyan Church. There was a good attendance, and the customary Christmas hymns were rendered. Tiny Tots' Mites for Soldiers.—On Wednesday evening of last week the infants attending the Penketh Council School had their Christmas tree and party. Mrs. Withinshaw kindly dis- tributed the gifts to the little guests, whilst Dr. Murray took the chair. Some of the older children. sang carols, and at the end of the pro- ceedings each child was given an orange. A collection amongst the children brought the week's war contribution up to .R 1 Is., which sum was given to the Infirmary, where a num- ber of wounded soldiers are under treatment.</p>
        <p>Wounded Soldier Sings at Wesleyan Concert. —On Thursday evening a very successful con- cert was held in the Wesleyan Schoolroom, Penketh, the Rev. H. T. Dixon presiding. The concert occupied the first part of the evening, and was much appreciated. The artistes were Miss Rosamond Parker, -Miss Teare, Private Robert Jones (a wounded soldier home on leave), Mr. Proffitt (of Liverpool), and Mr. Bert Smith. In addition an exceedingly funny sketch, en- titled "Paddy's Mistake," was presented by the Misses Davies and Baker, and Messrs. Richard- son and' Owen. Refreshments were then pro- vided, and the remainder of the evening was spent in games and competitions. Most of the audience subsequently attended the Watch Night service held in the chapel, the Rev. H. T. Dixon being the preacher.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>PAD GATE.</head>
        <p>PAD GATE.</p>
        <p>Late Mr. John Bennett's Will.—The will of Mr. John Bennett, of Grange Mount, Padgate, a well-known cattle dealer, whose death took place on September 22, has been proved at £14,945.</p>
        <p>Watchnight Service.—A large congregation assembled for the usual watchnight service in the Wesleyan Chapel. It was conducted by the Rev. A. J. Morton, and cdncluded with the sing- ing of the favourite hymn "Come, let us anew."</p>
        <p>Musical Service.—A special musical service was held in the Wesleyan Church on Sunday evening, when selections from the " Mes- siah " and a number of Christmas carols were given by an augmented choir, under the conduc- torship of Mr. W. J. Bate. The soloists were Mrs. J. Bennett, the Misses, Mason and M. Whitmore, Dr. Bennett, and Mr. E. H. Pearce. A very interesting address on. music Nras .given by the Rev. A. J. Morton. Mr. W. Ward pre- sided at the organ.</p>
        <p>Carol Singing for Children's Orphanage.—The carol singers of the Wesleyan Church again ventured out for their annual effort on behalf of the National Children's Orphanage, and although the weather during the four nights they were singing was the worst experienced for many yeirs, they trudged on undauntedi Sup- pers for the singers were provided en route by friends, and these were very much appreciated. The total amount realised by the effort was £92s.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild Christmas Social—The Christ- mas social in connection pith the Wesley Guild was held on New Year's Eve in the schoolroom, and was presided over by the Rev. A. J. Mor- ton. The soloists were Miss Irene Stewart, Miss J. Ward, and Messrs. G. Walker and W. J. Bate. The old trio, "A Little Farm Well Tilled" was given by the Rev. A. J. Morton, and Messrs. G. Walker and W. J. Bate, and a quartet entitled "How Sweet the Calm" was creditably rendered by the Misses J. Ward and M. Appleton. and Messrs. G. Walker and W. J. Bate. Refreshments were provided, and the musical items were interspersed with games. &amp;c. A very enjoyable time was spent by all present. Mrs. W. J. Bate officiated at the piano.</p>
        <p>That light, sparkling musical play, "The Marriage Market," which created such a furore in London, has proved a great holiday attrac- tion at the Royal Court Theatre, large audi- ences having been nightly delighted with the captivating music, the beautiful dresses, the whimsical story, and the elaborate staging effects. Those who have been fortunate enough to witness this entertaining play have been unanimous in their opinion that it is a sure cure for anyone in the "dumps," for there-is not a dull moment in it from the rise to the fall of the curtain. Messrs. Macdonald and Young's company bring out all the salient features in decidedly clever fashion, each artiste having thoroughly mastered the part allocated to him or her. A sprightly Slippery Jack is found in Mr. A. Itillward, who acts in a most unaffected Manner. Miss Daisy Grace is a charming Mariposa Gilroy, and, along with Mr. Millward, she delights the audience in the duets, "Mariposa Mine" and " The• One I Love." As Blinker and Lord Hurlingham, Mr. Fred Payne and Mr. Myles Clifton are highly successful in their humorous numbers. Miss May Stevenson makes a captivating Kitty Kent, and Miss Marie Stockdale is a capital maid to Mariposa. Mr. Ernest Pierce is well placed as the Sheriff, and Mr. Walter Williams is a breezy Captain of a steam yacht. As the "Middy," Miss Gladys Attenborough is also successful.</p>
        <p>Mr. Frederick Rossi directs the chorus and augmented orchestra in a very capable manner.</p>
        <p>Pantomime Next- Week.</p>
        <p>For next week the management have secured _Mr. and Mrs. John Walters' Company in an up-to-date version,of the ever-popular old pan- tomime "Cinderella," and doubtless there will be crowded houses nightly, as well as at the two matinees on Monday and Saturday. In addition to the, principals the company will consist of a chorus and ballet of fifty artistes.</p>
        <p>NUARY 2, 1915. L uuldu anwardwr.NTS• I WAR ITEMS. SPARKLING YULETIDE FARE AT COMMISSION FOR MR. RYMER YOUNG, Junior. THE ROYAL COURT. Mr. leith Rymer Young, Clare College, Cam- bridge, son of Mr. J. Rymer Young, chemist, has been appointed temporary Second-lieuten- ant in the 10th Battalion Lancashire Fusiliers. GUARDING THE WATERWORKS. Major Hope Johnstone, of the General Staff Western Command, having advised that all waterworks should be guarded by the local authorities, the Water Committee of the War- rington Corporation have authorised their en- gineer to take such steps as he may think advis- able.</p>
        <p>JOINED UP!</p>
        <p>Mr. T. L. Crompton, of the Boreugh Sur- veyor's Department, Warrington, has joined H.M. Forces, and it was reported to the Street Improvement Committee at a recent meeting that two other assistants in the same depart- ment had intimated their intention of doing likewise. Dr. Lumb, of the Health Depart- ment, has also been accepted for service with the forces. WARRINGTON-AUSTRALIAN FOR THE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <p>(From the Dutch paper "De 14otenkraker.") "THE TELEGRAM." "With God's help, we defeated the enemy, who left behind them thousands of dead and wounded. • ."</p>
      </div>
