      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>FINE HOLIDAY " BILL " AT .THE HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>FINE HOLIDAY " BILL " AT .THE HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The holiday fare at the Hippodrome is excep- tionally good, the numerous turns all being of a first-class order. A delightfully entertaining musical programme is given by tie Four Powers, who manipulate many and varied instruments in clever fashion, their interpretation of several popular selections being excellent. The andi- ences are exceptionally delighted with the play- ing of the anthems of the Allies. Cecil Lyle, late of Messrs. Maskelyne and Devant's Com- pany, and described as "The magical milliner," thoroughly justifies the title, for the rapid and finished manner in which he trims several hats must make the ladies envious. The chocolate box trick,- which he also performs, completely mystifies the audience. William Jackson's "Eight Lancashire Lads" are favouritel—With the audience. They play several musical in- struments skilfully, sing excellently, and dance cleverly. Miss Alberta Flhaey is an accom- plished violinist, and her partner a beautiful tenor vocalist. Marvellous and hair-raising tricks are performed, on the trapeze by Camp- bell and Scott, who are aptly described as the flying Scotsmen. Mabel Thomas is a smart dancer and club swinger, and Crawford and Howard appear in an entertaining "diversion," entitled "A Boy, a Girl and a Wall." War news in picture form is shown by means of the bio- scope, and the audiences are vociferous in their applause. A Karno Company Next Week.</p>
        <p>_ Fred Karno's Company, in "The Steam Laundry," a screamingly funny sketch, will provide the " star " attraction at the Hippo- drome next week. Other items in an attractive bill will be provided by Jack Walsh, comedian; Bouquet and Derieux, baritone and pianist (who are billed as "Two Genuine Belgian Refugees"); Rosie Reys, vocalist; the Nelson Comedy Skat- ing Trio; Hymar and Afetford, dancers; Fred Masson, Hebrew comedian; and Will Hay, the schoolmaster comedian of "Bend down !" fame.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>CHRISTMAS ATTRACTIONS AT THE – STAR KINEMA.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS ATTRACTIONS AT THE - STAR KINEMA.</p>
        <p>The Star Kinema motto seems to be not "Business as usual," but "Business still better than usual," and at all performances this week the house has been filled to its utmost by pleasure-seekers; anxious to see all the special Christmas attractions. The fine programme which occupied the first three days included: "The Spellbound Multitude," being Oil ninth instalment of the exciting serial, "Adventures of Kathlyn." " Patheis Animated Gazette" was a special Scarborough war number, followed by the gramophone song, "God bless you, Tommy Atkins "—which was illUstrated by a series of beautifully coloured patriotic slides. That celebrated coloured film-drama, "A Queen's Lore," held the audiences spellbound, and an excruciatingly funny. comedy, "The Old Maid's Baby," concluded .the programme.</p>
        <p>The second excellent bill of- fare—which in- cludes the thrilling drama, "His Last Round" —does not conclude until this evening, and should not be missed. Time and prices are as usual.</p>
        <p>For Next Week.</p>
        <p>The leading film in an attractive programme, arranged for the first three nights of next week, will be "The Pride of Jennico," whilst for the week-end a filmed version of "Bootle's Baby" has been booked.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>WAR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wm. Barratt, 149, Barrow-street, Mel- bourne, writes to say that his son, Wallace Bar- ratt, who for some years was employed at Fletcher, Russell and td., Works, War- rington, had just sailed Co.,(November 4th) with the Ist Expeditionary Force, being attached to the Engineers. "There has," he adds, "already 'sailed 20,000 men from the Australian Colonies. I am pleased to say, although far separated from home, we feel it a privilege• to be able to do honour to the old country by sending our sons to fight for freedom, right and justice. We must and will win. I believe 60 per cent. of the men who have volunteered from here are British-born lads."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>AFTER-CHRISTNIAS RECRUITING. This Week's Figures Much Better.</head>
        <p>AFTER-CHRISTNIAS RECRUITING. This Week's Figures Much Better.</p>
        <p>Recruiting for the Army has been very much better during the week, cicise upon 200 men hav- ing joined the service. It is anticipated that now the festive season is over the figures will be considerably improved upon next week and in the near future.</p>
        <p>Major Tyler, who has been in charge of the recruiting office at the Police Station, has been appointed assistant to the Insfiecreir of Recruit- ing at 'Chester, and will enter upon his new duties next week. He will be succeeded in Warrington by Colonel McCarthy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>PREP IR ITORY TRAINING CORPS.</head>
        <p>PREP IR ITORY TRAINING CORPS.</p>
        <p>FAIRFIELD AND HOWLEY COMPANY.</p>
        <p>On the rifle range at llylands' Recreation Ground the best five shots were made by T. E. Peters. T. Whittle made a tie with D. Plinston for •second place; the competition 'for fourth place was very keen, and eventually a tie re- sulted between S. Oddy, T. Healer and J. Cup- page. J. Norman proved to be the best novice.</p>
        <p>A month ago many members rarely hit" the target; now the "misses" are rarest, so the _progress made is very satisfactory. The ammunition for this shoot was kindly given by Mr. H. Ashton.</p>
        <p>Stewards--At the Firing Point: A. Bailie and TI. Gaunt; at the Butts: G. Bamber, T. H. Newton and W. O'Neill.</p>
        <p>At the London and North-Western Railway. Company's rifle range the company firing re- sulted :--1., T. E. Peters; 2, R. Allman, tied with T. Lowe; 4, T. Healer; 5, W. O'Neill; 6, D. Plinston.</p>
        <p>ORFORD AND ST. JOHN'S 'COMPANY.</p>
        <p>Presentation to Mr. W. De!ooze</p>
        <p>Last week, when dispersing for the holidays, the members of the Orford and St. John's Com- pany expressed appreciation of the services of their Sergeant-Instructor, and on behalf of the company Mr. W. Smith presented Mr. Delooze with a smoker's companion. SETTING TO WORK AGAIN AFTER THE HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>The drills in connection with the various</p>
        <p>wards of the town And district affiliated with the Warrington and District Preparatory Training Corps will re-commence on Monday evening next, January 4th; the times and places being the same as in the last week of drill previous to the holidays. It is desirable that there should be a good muster in each case, and that the enthusiasm which up to now has been shown by the members of the corps should be fully maintained. New companies, which show promise of great success, have been formed at Whitley, Sankey and Penketh.</p>
        <p>The corps is now affifiated with the Central Association, of which Lord Desborough is presi- dent, and is officially recognised by the Govern- ment.</p>
        <p>It is expected that in the near future the rules and regulations of the Central Association will be placed before the members and 'fully ex- plained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>Vital Statistics.</head>
        <p>Vital Statistics.</p>
        <p>The report of the Medical Officer of Health for the week ending December 26th showed a total of 30 births and 21 deaths, as compared with 41 and 22 respectively in the corresponding week of last year. Of the deaths recorded in Christmas week nine were those of children under the age of five.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>SECONDARY SCHOOL ROLL OF HONOUR.</head>
