      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-01-02_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>WORKING MEN'S MISSION.</head>
        <p>WORKING MEN'S MISSION.</p>
        <p>Business and Pleasure at Yuletide</p>
        <p>Gatherings.</p>
        <p>A most successful evening was passed at the Working Men's Mission, Bank Quay, on Mon- day, when the senior scholars held their annual party. After full justice had been done to an excellent tea, the annual meeting took place, Mr. T. Dale (senior superintendent) presidinw and making a brief speech.</p>
        <p>YEAR'S WORK IN. THE SUNDAY SCHOOL. Ile Secretary (Mr., A. Edwards) presented his annual report, and said that the Sunday School had long been looked upon as one of the chief recruiting grounds for the church. The number of teachers and scholars on their books ,was 950 i. They were sorry to have to record the sudden, deaths of Mr. Thomas:Preston Taylor ka teacher connected with them over 30 years) and an- eleven-year-old scholar, William Thomas: The attendances on Sundays averaged 267 (morn- ings) and 61.5 .(afternoons)—a decrease of 11 on the average of the previous year. They had presented 220 attendance prizes for 1913; and, twelve of their scholars having sat .for the Sun- day School Union Scripture examinations, they. gained four prizes and ten certifiCates-7for which result they thanked Mr. George Head,. who bad trained the candidates. The anniver- sary collections had totalled nearly £47, the. services being very successful; as also was Walk- ing Day. On Decision Day—October 18th-41 or 'their • sChelars had become workers for the- Master; whilst the Men and Women's Bible, (lasses were also doing a good work, and. assisted in the League of Help, • Foreign Mift-. sinus, Children's Lantern Services, &amp;c. The. Young People's Christian Endeavour Society— which was now twenty years • old—was still a mighty power for good; and the Band of Hope had been specially honoured this year,. one of its members (Miss Eva Tanner) having been made TemPerauee Queen by the Band of Hope Union.. Miss Tanner had got 137 of their scholars, and many Territorials now on active service, to sign the pledge. The Rechabite Tents were strong. Four collections taken for Mis- sionary work • had realised nearly £6. 'Ten of their scholars bad joined the Church, whilst a good number had enlisted. Their best thankt were due to Mrs. John Crosfield for having ones, again been so l ind in enabling them to have that Christmas treat; and also for the valuable. help i which she bad rendered during the year... Let. them hope that their prosperity would. be- come still more firmly established. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>An enjoyable programme of songs, recitation&amp; and dialogues was interspersed bet Ween th• I)w'4iness items, and games followed. The, junior scholars' party was iield on Tues. day evening, and also proved very successful; whilst last night the Young Men's Bachelor Party was held, tieing attended by about 1:26, who indulged in games and competitions.</p>
        <p>Printed and published by John Walker and Co. (Warrington), Limited, at the EXAMINEZ General Printing Works, Bewsey-street, War- rington; also published by them at the EXAMINER Offices, 2, Victoria-road, Widnes; 32, George-street, St. Helens; 3, Market-street, Earlestown, all in the County of Lancaster; and at Church-street, Runcorn, in the Connto of Chester.</p>
      </div>
