        <p>Warrington Equitable Industrial Co=operative Society, Ltd. BAKERY DEPARTMENT. TO OUR MEMBERS : It,.has been submitted to us that the time has arrived for an advance in the price of Bread, and, providing we have an assurance that the advance will be general, we have agreed that ON AND AFTER MODAL JANUARY 4th, 19159 the price of the 4-Ib. loaf will be advanced Id., this bringing the retail price of Bread to : . for SECONDS, per 4-lbs. 71d. for BEST, per 4-lbs. The present position is, that practically the whole of the Flour Contracts placed by our General Manager before the outbreak of the War have been cleared. We were fortu- nately able to get all the flour bought, although in many instances to do so we were compelled to pay 4al least 10 PER CENT. increase for War Risks Insurance. At the present time Flour has advanced at least 10/- per sack over what it stood at, in August. This is equal to ltd. increased cost per 4-lb. LOAF, and fully justifies the proposed increase in the price of Bread. IN MOST OTHER TOWNS PRICES HAVE LONG SINCE ADVANCED, but your Committee had determined to give to the Members the full benefit of the keen buying of the Manager, and not to advance until our stocks were so far depleted that we ourselves had to pay advanced prices. In the meahtime, had we advanced prices at the out- break of War, our Members WOULD HAVE PAID consider- ably over £3,500 EXTRA FOR BREAD, and, in addition, the people of Warrington, who are NOT Members, would have also paid advanced prices. The lesson to the Workers is very clear—JOlN, and SUPPORT the CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY in your own interest and in the interest of your fellow-workers. For the Committee of Management, J. JARMAN, Secretary.</p>
